context:
{"patient_information": "A 40-year-old male farmer from the Peruvian Amazon, who was found to be HTLV-1-positive 10 years prior to presentation. A recent HIV ELISA test was negative.", "chief_complaint": "A 4-month history of chronic productive cough and chest pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-month history of chronic productive cough, chest pain, dyspnoea on exertion, and a weight loss of about 20 kg. He denied diarrhoea, fever, or night sweats. Three and a half months earlier, he was hospitalized for similar complaints and presumptively diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis based on clinical and radiographic findings, despite three negative sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli (AFB). He received first-line TB therapy (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) but showed no clinical improvement, with persistence of his productive cough, which later included streaking of blood in the sputum. This led to his referral with a suspected diagnosis of multi-drug resistant TB.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a farmer from a rural area in the central Peruvian Amazon. He denies any recent travel.", "vitals": "Temperature was 37.2\u00b0C (98.96\u00b0F), blood pressure was 120/75 mmHg, pulse was 70 bpm regular, and respiratory rate was 15 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared fatigued and cachectic. On inspection, a few small, mobile, soft, and non-tender cervical and retroauricular lymph nodes were palpable. Examination of the lungs revealed crackles and rhonchi bilaterally. There was no hepatosplenomegaly, and the rest of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a platelet count of 1093 x 10^9/L, indicating thrombocytosis; the rest of the full blood count was within normal limits. Three initial sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) were negative, as were two subsequent AFB smears. The tuberculin skin test was also negative. A chest x-ray showed bilateral confluent reticulonodular infiltrates and multiple cavities in the right lung. A definitive diagnosis was made when a sputum KOH preparation revealed the characteristic yeast forms of Paracoccidioides brasiliensis, which was subsequently grown in culture.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses considered were multi-drug resistant TB (MDR TB), histoplasmosis, and paracoccidioidomycosis. While TB is endemic in Peru, MDR TB was considered less likely given the patient's origin from a rural area. Histoplasmosis was also less likely as its chronic form is more common in individuals with pre-existing lung disease. Paracoccidioidomycosis was deemed the most probable diagnosis given the patient's age, gender, occupation, origin, and clinical presentation. Less common possibilities included paragonimiasis or pulmonary hydatid cyst.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with a full course of first-line anti-tuberculosis therapy for several months without any clinical improvement. After the diagnosis of paracoccidioidomycosis was confirmed, the TB treatment was discontinued. Due to the extensive pulmonary lesions and his HTLV-1 co-infection, he was started on treatment with amphotericin B, to be followed by itraconazole.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was chronic (adult) paracoccidioidomycosis. This was established by direct visualization of the characteristic yeast forms of Paracoccidioides brasiliensis on a KOH preparation of the patient's sputum, which was subsequently confirmed by fungal culture. The diagnosis was consistent with the patient's epidemiological risk factors (a male farmer from the Peruvian Amazon), chronic pulmonary symptoms with significant weight loss, and failure to respond to empirical anti-tuberculosis therapy.", "disease_name_short": "Paracoccidioidomycosis", "source_filename": "55---A-40-Year-Old-Male-Farmer-from-Peru-With-Ch_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 52-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and poorly controlled hypertension.", "chief_complaint": "Abdominal pain and vomiting.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-day history of retro-orbital headache, lethargy, myalgia, and fevers. These initial symptoms had begun to improve over the 24 hours prior to presentation, at which point he developed abdominal pain and vomiting.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in an urban hospital setting in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam, a high-endemicity area for dengue. He had no history of travel outside the city for several years. The risk of malaria is very low in this urban area, and there was no suspected rodent exposure.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient was afebrile with a temperature of 36.5\u00b0C. He was in compensated shock with a heart rate of 120 bpm, blood pressure of 105/90 mmHg resulting in a narrow pulse pressure, a respiratory rate of 28 breaths per minute, and a peripheral oxygen saturation of 93% on air.", "physical_exam": "The patient was drowsy but rousable with a GCS of 14/15. He had a weak radial pulse. Respiratory examination revealed a dull percussion note and reduced breath sounds at both lung bases. The abdomen showed mild distension with shifting dullness and a tender liver edge palpable 3cm below the costal margin. Skin examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed haemoconcentration with a haematocrit of 53.9%, which later rose to 55.1%. He had leukopenia (WCC 2.1 x10^9/L) and significant thrombocytopenia. C-reactive protein was low (<4 mg/L). There was evidence of shock with a raised serum lactate of 3.5 mmol/L. Ultrasound scans confirmed moderate bilateral pleural effusions, ascites, mild hepatomegaly, and a thickened gallbladder wall. The diagnosis of dengue was confirmed by a positive NS1 ELISA and Reverse Transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR).", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was severe dengue with vascular leak and compensated shock, which was considered highly likely given the clinical picture and location. Other important considerations included rickettsial infections like scrub typhus, even without an eschar. Malaria was considered but less likely due to the low urban risk and lack of haemolysis. Leptospirosis was also a possibility, though the absence of rodent exposure made it less probable. Melioidosis was considered given the patient's diabetes and Southeast Asian origin.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with compensated shock and admitted to a high-dependency unit for supportive care. He received careful fluid resuscitation with Ringer\u2019s lactate solution, starting at 10mL/kg over 1 hour and increasing to 15mL/kg/hr due to persistent tachycardia, oliguria, and a rising haematocrit. The fluid rate was subsequently tapered down as his condition improved. He was monitored closely with two-hourly clinical assessments and six-hourly haematocrit checks. The patient made a complete recovery and was discharged from the hospital 3 days later.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was severe dengue. This was based on the typical clinical progression from a febrile phase to a critical phase at the time of defervescence, characterized by signs of vascular leakage (pleural effusions, ascites, haemoconcentration) and compensated shock. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a positive dengue NS1 ELISA and a positive RT-PCR for dengue virus.", "disease_name_short": "Severe Dengue", "source_filename": "9---A-52-Year-Old-Man-from-Vietnam-With-Evo_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 44-year-old male rice farmer from Laos with a history of type 2 diabetes diagnosed 4 years prior. He was reportedly not compliant with his oral antidiabetic medications or follow-up.", "chief_complaint": "A one-month history of fever, headache, generalized myalgia, arthralgia, and a painful swelling over the left scapula.", "history_of_present_illness": "For one month, the patient experienced fever, body aches, and a painful swelling on his left scapula that gradually increased in size without preceding trauma. A week before admission, the lesion was incised and drained at a local health center, and he was started on oral cloxacillin 1g four times daily. Despite this, his condition did not improve. In the two days prior to admission, he deteriorated with high fever, chills, and severe malaise.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a rice farmer in Southern Laos and presented during the rainy season. His occupation and geographic location are significant risk factors for melioidosis, which is commonly acquired via percutaneous inoculation through cuts while working in rice fields.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient appeared septic with a fever of 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F), a heart rate of 136 bpm, blood pressure of 140/80 mmHg, and a respiratory rate of 30 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was pale and jaundiced. There was a 2.5 x 4 cm erythematous swelling in the left scapular region with pus discharging from a central wound. No regional lymphadenopathy was noted. Chest auscultation revealed bilateral crepitations and reduced breath sounds at both lung bases. Heart sounds were normal, and the abdomen was soft without organomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a white blood cell count of 16.2 x10^9/L with 92.1% polymorphs, haemoglobin of 12.5 g/dL, and elevated glucose at 20.6 mmol/L. Liver function tests were abnormal with a total bilirubin of 111.2 \u00b5mol/L and direct bilirubin of 56.4 \u00b5mol/L. Pus culture from the back abscess grew Burkholderia pseudomallei after 48 hours, and blood cultures later grew the same organism. A chest radiograph revealed bilateral pleural effusions. An abdominal ultrasound showed hepatosplenomegaly but no liver or splenic abscesses.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included a bacterial abscess with sepsis caused by common organisms like Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus species. However, given the patient's epidemiological risk factors (diabetic rice farmer from Laos in the rainy season) and clinical presentation, melioidosis caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei was a primary consideration. Tuberculosis was also considered due to the sub-acute history and respiratory findings, though the abscess was not typical of a 'cold' tuberculous abscess. Disseminated fungal infection was deemed unlikely.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated empirically with ceftriaxone and gentamicin. After 48 hours, upon suspicion of melioidosis based on culture results, treatment was switched to ceftazidime. He improved after 7 days, became afebrile by day 14, and was then transitioned to oral co-trimoxazole to complete a 20-week course. His glycaemic control was also established. He was discharged after 21 days of hospitalization to continue the 5-month oral eradication therapy and remains well.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with melioidosis, presenting as septicemia with a back abscess and pneumonia. The diagnosis was confirmed by the isolation of Burkholderia pseudomallei from both pus culture from the abscess and blood cultures. The clinical picture of sepsis with disseminated infection in a patient with uncontrolled diabetes and relevant occupational exposure in an endemic region was highly consistent with this diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Melioidosis", "source_filename": "32---A-44-Year-Old-Male-Farmer-from-Laos-With-Di_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old Guatemalan man with no past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Several minutes of weakness and numbness of the leg.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with weakness and numbness of the leg lasting several minutes, which resolved just before his arrival at the hospital. Two weeks prior, he also experienced two episodes of facial twitching that subsided without intervention after 10 seconds each.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient was born and lived in a rural area of Guatemala before immigrating to New York 5 years ago. In Guatemala, his family owned uncorralled pigs and his house lacked sanitation services.", "vitals": "His vitals were normal.", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was unremarkable. Cranial nerves were intact. Motor and sensory examinations, deep tendon reflexes, and gait were all normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial cell blood count and serum chemistry were within normal limits. A serum immunoblot for cysticercosis was positive, as was the serum cysticercosis antigen at the beginning of therapy. A CT of the head demonstrated multiple calcifications in the brain parenchyma. An MRI of the head revealed complex cystic structures in the subarachnoid space, dilation of the suprasellar and Sylvian cisterns, peripheral enhancement after gadolinium, and an old lacunar infarct. A CT angiography showed patent cerebral arteries. An MRI of the spine showed no evidence of spinal involvement.", "differential_diagnosis": "The patient's presentation was consistent with a transitory ischemic attack. However, the combination of imaging findings, including parenchymal calcified lesions and complex cystic structures, along with the patient's epidemiological risk factors, made neurocysticercosis the most likely diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a high dose of prednisone followed by a taper, and dual antiparasitic therapy with albendazole and praziquantel. After 9 months of treatment, his serum antigen was negative and his lesions had resolved on imaging. He has been followed closely with no recurrence.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is subarachnoid neurocysticercosis, caused by the larval form of Taenia solium. This was determined based on the combination of epidemiological risk factors (origin from rural Guatemala with exposure to pigs), characteristic neuroimaging findings of parenchymal calcifications and complex subarachnoid cysts, and was confirmed by a positive serum immunoblot for cysticercosis.", "disease_name_short": "Subarachnoid neurocysticercosis", "source_filename": "82---A-31-Year-Old-Man-from-Guatemala-With-Acute-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old Peruvian man with a past medical history of hepatitis of unknown etiology 20 years prior. He had not received any vaccinations since his early childhood.", "chief_complaint": "Fever, retro-orbital headache, jaundice, bleeding, and altered mental status for eight days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began eight days prior to presentation with fever and retro-orbital headache. Two days into the illness, he developed jaundice, coffee-ground vomiting, gross haematuria, and increasing mental obtundation, leading to admission at a local hospital in the jungle. During transfer to the referral hospital in Lima, he experienced a generalized seizure.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient worked as a logging supervisor for the past 5 years in a remote jungle area near Pucallpa, Peru, on the shores of the Ucayali River. This occupation created possible contact with sylvatic mosquitoes and animals. He reported not taking any malaria prophylaxis.", "vitals": "On admission, his temperature was 38.8\u00b0C, blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg, heart rate was 110 beats per minute, and respiratory rate was 24 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was agitated, unresponsive to verbal commands, and intermittently stuporous. He was jaundiced, with spontaneous bleeding from his oral mucosa and at venipuncture sites, and had multiple large ecchymoses on his face, trunk, and limbs. Neurological examination revealed a marked flapping of hands (asterixis) with no focal or meningeal signs. Lung auscultation revealed crepitant rales in both bases. The liver was felt 3 cm below the right costal margin, while the spleen was not palpable and there was no lymphadenopathy.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial labs showed anaemia (haematocrit 30%), thrombocytopenia (platelets 110,000/\u03bcL), severe transaminitis (AST 2890 U/L, ALT 2676 U/L), hyperbilirubinemia (total bilirubin 11.6 mg/dL), and coagulopathy (INR 2.6, aPTT 80 s). Urinalysis showed proteinuria and hematuria. Three malaria thick films were negative, as were blood cultures and serologies for Brucella and Hepatitis B (IgM anti-Hbc). The diagnosis was confirmed by a highly positive IgM capture ELISA for yellow fever at a 1:10000 titer. Anti-dengue IgM was negative. A chest x-ray eventually showed bilateral pulmonary infiltrates compatible with ARDS.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for this acute febrile illness with liver failure included severe malaria, though the transaminases were exceptionally high. Other considerations were viral hepatitis (A, B, C, D, E), toxin-mediated hepatitis, leptospirosis, typhoid fever, and brucellosis. Severe dengue was less likely due to the prolonged fever and low hematocrit. Given his occupation, jungle exposure, and unvaccinated status, a viral haemorrhagic fever, particularly yellow fever, was highly suspected.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient received presumptive treatment for malaria, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and supportive care. Over the subsequent three days, his condition worsened with increasing hepatic encephalopathy, renal failure (creatinine 6.5 mg/dL), coma, and disseminated intravascular coagulation with severe bleeding. He developed ARDS confirmed by chest x-ray. His liver transaminases dropped suddenly on day 3, indicating acute hepatic disintegration. The patient died one day later.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Yellow Fever. This was based on the clinical presentation of fever, jaundice, bleeding, and multi-organ failure in an unvaccinated individual with significant occupational exposure as a logger in an endemic region of Peru. The diagnosis was confirmed serologically by a highly positive IgM capture ELISA for yellow fever (1:10000). Alternative diagnoses such as malaria, bacterial sepsis, hepatitis B, and dengue were ruled out by negative laboratory investigations.", "disease_name_short": "Yellow fever", "source_filename": "93---A-35-Year-Old-Male-Logger-from-Peru-With-Fe_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 42-year-old German man with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-day history of fever up to 40\u00b0C, arthralgias, and retro-orbital pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 42-year-old man presented to a hospital in Germany with a 6-day history of fever reaching 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F), accompanied by arthralgias and retro-orbital pain. His symptoms started while traveling, and he presented the day after returning from a 10-week trip to Thailand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had just returned from a 10-week trip to Thailand. He spent two months on Little Koh Chang in the Andaman Sea, followed by five days in Hua Hin and five days in Bangkok. He did not take any antimalarial chemoprophylaxis, consistent with national recommendations for his travel itinerary.", "vitals": "On examination, his temperature was 39.1\u00b0C (102.4\u00b0F), blood pressure was 156/80 mmHg, pulse was 106 beats per minute, respiratory rate was 16 breaths per minute, and SpO2 was 95% on ambient air.", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was largely unremarkable. The patient was fully alert with a GCS of 15/15. There was no skin rash or lymphadenopathy. His chest was clear on auscultation, and the abdominal examination revealed no tenderness or organomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed thrombocytopenia of 81 x 10^9/L, an elevated CRP of 55 mg/L, and elevated liver enzymes (AST 62 U/L, ALT 106 U/L). A Dengue NS1 antigen test and a rapid diagnostic test for malaria were both negative. A subsequent thick blood film showed Plasmodium species trophozoites at a density of 920/\u03bcL. The species could not be identified on the thin film, but a species-specific PCR ultimately confirmed a mono-infection with P. knowlesi.", "differential_diagnosis": "Given the clinical syndrome and travel history, the primary differential diagnosis was dengue fever, although the duration of fever was long and the NS1 test was negative. Other considerations included Chikungunya, Zika, influenza, and acute HIV infection. Enteric fever was considered less likely. Malaria was a critical diagnosis to exclude, as a negative rapid diagnostic test is not sufficient to rule it out, especially for non-falciparum species.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the infectious diseases ward. Based on the positive blood film suspecting non-falciparum malaria, treatment was started with atovaquone/proguanil 250/100mg, four tablets once daily for three days. The patient recovered rapidly and was discharged on the fourth day.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Knowlesi malaria, caused by Plasmodium knowlesi. The diagnosis was confirmed by species-specific PCR after an initial thick blood film showed Plasmodium species trophozoites at 920/\u03bcL. This was despite a negative malaria rapid diagnostic test and initial difficulty in identifying the species by microscopy, which can be mistaken for P. malariae.", "disease_name_short": "Knowlesi malaria", "source_filename": "92---A-42-Year-Old-Traveller-Returning-from-Thaila_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 62-year-old male Thai police sergeant major from Kalasin Province, Thailand. His past medical history was notable for a liver fluke infection treated with praziquantel 10 years prior to presentation. Hepatitis B serology suggested a previous, resolved infection.", "chief_complaint": "An incidentally discovered solitary liver mass found on abdominal ultrasonography during an annual physical check-up.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was asymptomatic at the time of presentation for his annual physical. He denied any clinical symptoms, including weight loss, jaundice, or other gastrointestinal symptoms. A solitary mass was found in the liver on abdominal ultrasonography, and he was referred for further investigation.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Kalasin Province in northeast Thailand, a region where the liver fluke, Opisthorchis viverrini, is endemic. An important risk factor is the local tradition of eating raw or undercooked freshwater fish, which frequently contains metacercariae of the fluke. The patient had a confirmed history of liver fluke infection treated a decade ago, and re-infection is common in the region.", "vitals": "Vital signs were normal.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared in good health. Physical examination showed no jaundice, and the liver and spleen were not palpable. There were no liver stigmata or lymphadenopathy detected.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed normal liver function tests (AST, ALT, ALP, bilirubin). Hepatitis B serology was consistent with past infection (HBsAg negative, Anti-HBs positive, Anti-HBc positive), and Anti-HCV was negative. Tumor markers were significant for a markedly elevated CA19-9 of 342.80 U/mL, while AFP and CEA were within normal limits. Abdominal MRI revealed a single solid liver mass in segments VI/VII of the right liver lobe without marked bile duct dilatation. Post-surgical pathology of the 9x9 cm mass confirmed infiltrative compact glandular structures lined by dysplastic columnar cells with necrosis. Additionally, liver flukes (O. viverrini) were found in the adjacent bile duct.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses for the liver mass included hepatocellular carcinoma, cholangiocarcinoma (CCA), a metastatic tumor, and infections such as tuberculosis. Given the patient was asymptomatic with a high serum CA19-9 level and no history of chronic viral hepatitis, CCA was considered the most likely diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the hospital and underwent a hemihepatectomy. A large, 9 x 9 cm whitish-yellowish tumor was resected from the right liver lobe. Postoperatively, the patient was started on chemotherapy.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (CCA). This was confirmed by histology of the resected liver mass. The cancer is strongly associated with the patient's chronic liver fluke infection, evidenced by the presence of Opisthorchis viverrini flukes in the adjacent bile duct found during surgery. Key supporting evidence included the patient's epidemiological risk from living in northeast Thailand with a history of eating raw freshwater fish, and a markedly elevated CA19-9 tumor marker.", "disease_name_short": "Cholangiocarcinoma", "source_filename": "80---A-62-Year-Old-Man-from-Thailand-With-a_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A previously healthy 20-year-old man from southern India. One month before the onset of symptoms, he received five doses of anti-rabies vaccine (purified chick embryo cell vaccine) following a dog bite, but did not receive rabies immunoglobulin (RIG).", "chief_complaint": "Fever and quadriparesis.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient had a fever for 4 days, which was followed by tingling and weakness that began in his left leg. Over the subsequent 2 days, the weakness progressed to involve all four limbs, resulting in quadriparesis.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from southern India, a rabies-endemic country. He had a history of a stray dog bite (WHO category III) on his left lower limb one month prior to symptom onset. He received a full course of five active anti-rabies vaccinations but did not receive anti-rabies immunoglobulin as part of his post-exposure prophylaxis.", "vitals": "On admission, his blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg, pulse was 90 beats per minute, respiratory rate was 26 breaths per minute, and his temperature was 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient was conscious with normal mental functions (GCS 15/15). Neurological examination revealed a flaccid, areflexic quadriparesis.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Blood investigations including serum electrolytes, renal function, and liver function tests were normal. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis showed a protein of 52 mg/dL, glucose of 60 mg/dL, and 340 cells/mm3 (60% polymorphs and 40% lymphocytes). A saliva sample collected at admission was positive for rabies viral RNA by RT-PCR; a CSF sample was negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary clinical differential was Guillain Barr\u00e9 syndrome, given the rapidly progressive ascending paralysis. Other possibilities considered included post-vaccination encephalomyelitis and other encephalitides such as those caused by arthropod-borne viruses or herpes simplex virus.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Following admission, the patient developed autonomic instability, evidenced by increased perspiration and significant variability in heart rate and blood pressure. He subsequently developed dysphagia and respiratory distress, requiring mechanical ventilation on the third day of hospitalization. His condition deteriorated, and he died from a sudden cardiac arrest on the fifth day of hospitalization, nine days after the onset of his symptoms.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was paralytic rabies. This was based on the clinical presentation of fever and rapidly progressive flaccid quadriparesis one month after a WHO category III dog bite in a rabies-endemic area. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the detection of rabies viral RNA via RT-PCR in a saliva sample. The development of the disease, despite vaccination, was attributed to the omission of rabies immunoglobulin (RIG) from the post-exposure prophylaxis.", "disease_name_short": "Paralytic rabies", "source_filename": "91---A-20-Year-Old-Male-from-India-With-Fever-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 21-year-old male migrant from rural Mali who is illiterate. His past medical history is notable for a prior work-up for splenomegaly and bicytopenia at a haematology department, where the underlying condition could not be established. HIV serology was negative; EBV and CMV serologies indicated previous infection.", "chief_complaint": "Referral for massive splenomegaly, bicytopenia, intermittent fever, and a feeling of abdominal heaviness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient reports intermittent fever and a sensation of \u201cheaviness\u201d on the left side of his abdomen for some time, though the exact duration is difficult to specify. He denies night sweats and his weight has been stable. Despite the splenomegaly, he is clinically quite well.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a migrant from rural Mali, an area with stable malaria transmission. He arrived in Germany one year prior to presentation. His journey involved crossing Niger and spending a few weeks at a detention centre in Libya before traveling through Italy to Germany. He comes from a poor family.", "vitals": "At presentation, he was afebrile with a temperature of 36.1\u00b0C (96.98\u00b0F). Blood pressure was 120/80mmHg and pulse was 64 bpm.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared clinically well. The spleen was massively enlarged, palpable around 15 cm below the left costal margin. There was no clinical anaemia or lymphadenopathy. Heart sounds were clear with no murmurs. An abdominal ultrasound confirmed a spleen of 21.8 cm in longitudinal diameter with homogenous parenchyma. The liver was normal in size and appearance, and flow in the portal and splenic veins was normal. There was no lymphadenopathy or free fluid.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a mild bicytopenia. White blood cell count was 3.3 x 10\u2079/L and haemoglobin was 13.8 g/dL. Platelet count was not specified but noted as low in the referral. Inflammatory markers were normal, with CRP at 0.5 and ESR at 9/21 mm/h. LDH was less than 220 U/L. Immunoglobulin M (IgM) was markedly elevated at 8.59 g/l. Thick and thin blood films for Plasmodium spp. were negative. However, P. falciparum serology by IFAT was positive at a titer of 1:256. Subsequently, a PCR for P. falciparum came back positive. Serologies for schistosomiasis and leishmaniasis were negative, and no ova of S. mansoni were found in the stool. Blood cultures were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included lymphoma or leukaemia, which was considered unlikely due to the normal differential count and non-elevated LDH, CRP, and ESR. Visceral leishmaniasis was less likely as the patient did not appear severely ill and lacked pancytopenia. Schistosomiasis was ruled out by the normal liver ultrasound, which showed no signs of portal hypertension or fibrosis. Bacterial endocarditis and brucellosis were also considered less probable given the massive, rather than moderate, splenomegaly and absence of cardiac murmurs. The combination of massive splenomegaly, very high IgM, positive malaria serology, and origin from an endemic area made hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly (HMS) the most likely diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a single course of dihydroartemisinin/piperaquine. Following treatment, his reported episodes of fever subsided. A follow-up malaria PCR at 2 weeks was still positive, but has remained negative since. He has been monitored for 2.5 years. His IgM levels took 2 years to return to normal. The spleen size slowly decreased, measuring 14 cm in diameter at his last visit. His overall condition improved, and he was able to learn to read, write, and attend school in Germany.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Hyperreactive Malarial Splenomegaly (HMS). This was established based on the patient's presentation with massive splenomegaly, a history of intermittent fevers, and his origin from rural Mali, a malaria-endemic region. The diagnosis was confirmed by laboratory findings of a markedly elevated polyclonal IgM, a positive P. falciparum IFAT serology (1:256), and a positive P. falciparum PCR, despite negative malaria blood films. The significant clinical improvement and slow resolution of splenomegaly and IgM levels following a single course of antimalarial therapy further solidified the diagnosis, while other causes like hematologic malignancy and schistosomiasis were ruled out.", "disease_name_short": "Hyperreactive Malarial Splenomegaly", "source_filename": "73---A-21-Year-Old-Male-Migrant-from-Rural-Mali-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
