{"patient_information": "A 52-year-old man with an unremarkable past medical history presented to a clinic in Germany.", "chief_complaint": "The patient presented with a fever for the past 2 days, accompanied by night sweats and a frontal headache.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient reported a two-day history of fever, night sweats, and frontal headache. He denied any joint pains and had not noticed a rash. He had also noted a skin lesion on his left upper thigh which he had covered with a plaster.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient was a safari tourist who had returned 10 days prior from a two-week holiday trip to South Africa. His travel included Cape Town, the Garden Route, KwaZulu\u2013Natal, Kruger Park, and several other game reserves. He did not take any antimalarial chemoprophylaxis.", "vitals": "His tympanic temperature was 37.5\u00b0C (99.5\u00b0F) after taking 1g of paracetamol. His pulse was 80 bpm and his blood pressure was 130/70 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "The patient was in fair general condition. His neck was supple and he was not jaundiced. There were enlarged lymph nodes in his left groin. On his left upper thigh, there was a small necrotic skin lesion, approximately 0.7 cm in diameter, with surrounding inflammation and lymphangiitis, which was identified as a typical eschar.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A malaria rapid diagnostic test and a thick and thin film for malaria were both negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was malaria, as the patient was febrile and had returned from a malaria-endemic region (KwaZulu\u2013Natal) without taking chemoprophylaxis. However, the presence of a typical eschar and regional lymphadenopathy strongly suggested African tick-bite fever.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was prescribed doxycycline 100mg twice daily by mouth for one week. His fever settled within a few days, the lymphadenopathy subsided, and the eschar healed after about two weeks.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was African tick-bite fever, caused by Rickettsia africae. The diagnosis was made clinically based on the patient's travel history as a safari tourist in South Africa, a common endemic area, and the characteristic clinical presentation of fever with a pathognomonic inoculation eschar and regional lymphadenitis. Malaria, the main differential, was ruled out with negative rapid diagnostic and microscopic tests.", "disease_name_short": "African tick-bite fever", "source_filename": "54---A-52-Year-Old-Male-Safari-Tourist-Returning-fro_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 51-year-old female Swiss traveller with no reported chronic underlying diseases and no regular intake of any medication.", "chief_complaint": "Generalized pruritic rash, conjunctivitis, and peripheral edema, with symptom onset two days prior to presentation.", "history_of_present_illness": "Four days after returning from her trip, the patient developed a generalized, slightly pruritic maculopapular rash on her face, trunk, and extremities. She was afebrile. The following day, the rash worsened, and she developed a non-purulent bilateral conjunctivitis. At the time of presentation, she also had tender edema of the hands, elbows, knees, and feet, along with generalized lymphadenopathy.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient presented six days after returning from a 2-week holiday to Guatemala and El Salvador. The clinical presentation and incubation period were consistent with arboviral infections which are prevalent in Central and South America.", "vitals": "The patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed a non-purulent bilateral conjunctivitis and a generalized maculopapular rash involving the face. There was tender edema of the hands, elbows, knees, and feet, as well as generalized lymphadenopathy affecting the cervical, axillary, and inguinal regions.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The full blood count and liver function tests were normal. C-reactive protein was 8 mg/L, and creatinine was mildly elevated at 87 \u03bcmol/L. Rapid diagnostic tests for dengue and chikungunya were negative. Blood samples sent to a reference laboratory confirmed Zika virus. The first serum sample, obtained 6 days after symptom onset, was positive for Zika RT-PCR. A second serum sample obtained 7 days later showed seroconversion, with Zika IgM IIFT becoming positive at 1:640 and Zika IgG IIFT rising from 1:20 to 1:5120. A rise in Dengue IgG IIFT to 1:5120 was interpreted as cross-reactivity.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis primarily included arboviral infections common in Central America, specifically dengue, chikungunya, and Zika. These infections can be clinically indistinguishable; however, the combination of a skin rash, conjunctivitis, and peripheral edema in an afebrile patient was considered highly suggestive of a Zika virus infection.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Rapid diagnostic tests for dengue and chikungunya were negative. Blood samples were sent to a reference laboratory for further testing, which confirmed the clinical suspicion of Zika virus. As arboviral infections are self-limiting and lack specific treatment, clinical management is exclusively supportive.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Zika virus infection. This was confirmed based on laboratory results which included a positive Zika RT-PCR on the initial blood sample and documented seroconversion on paired sera, with a negative to positive result for Zika IgM (1:640) and a significant fourfold rise in Zika IgG antibody titres (1:20 to 1:5120). The rise in anti-Dengue IgG was considered to be due to cross-reactivity.", "disease_name_short": "Zika virus infection", "source_filename": "77---A-51-Year-Old-Female-Traveller-Returning-from-Cen_2022_Clinical-Cases-i.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old Caucasian male with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Fatigue and a rash on his neck that started as a papule 2 days earlier.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a rash on his neck that began as a papule two days prior. The lesion was non-pruritic but was associated with significant, non-painful swelling and a pressure sensation in the neck. He reported no fever, but had at least one episode of diaphoresis with mild confusion and headache. He recalled a break in the skin at the site three days before the lesion appeared while shaving. He had been seen 24 hours earlier for similar symptoms, was given a cephalosporin, and discharged, but returned due to worsening malaise, increased neck swelling, and mild difficulty breathing.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a postal worker in the United States. He denied any foreign travel within the previous year, contact with animals, or insect bites. The presentation occurred during an investigation into a possible bioterrorism event. It was later confirmed that he was exposed during his occupation while handling contaminated mail.", "vitals": "Temperature was 36.9\u00b0C (98.4\u00b0F), pulse was 118 bpm, blood pressure was 138/90 mmHg, and the respiratory rate was 20 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "Examination revealed a 2cm irregularly shaped, indurated, non-tender patch on the left anterior neck with mild erythema and several 2-3mm vesicles. The main lesion had a 6mm shallow ulceration. There was massive neck oedema, with neck circumference increased from a baseline of 57cm to 81cm, making lymph nodes difficult to assess. The remainder of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood work, including full blood count and chemistries, was normal except for a mildly elevated haemoglobin at 18.7 g/dL. Blood cultures taken at the first clinic visit grew gram-positive rods. Gram stain and culture from the skin lesion were negative for B. anthracis. A full-thickness skin biopsy performed at a reference laboratory showed abundant bacilli in the dermis, and immunohistochemical (IHC) staining detected B. anthracis antigens. Convalescent serology revealed the presence of antibody to protective antigen.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for an ulcerative skin lesion with massive soft tissue swelling included bacterial ecthyma (Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus aureus), rickettsial diseases, necrotic arachnidism, rat-bite fevers, ulceroglandular tularemia, and bubonic plague. Given the context of a possible bioterrorism event in the United States at the time, cutaneous anthrax was considered the most important diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with a first-generation cephalosporin but his condition worsened. Upon hospital admission, he received intravenous levofloxacin and ampicillin-sulbactam and subsequently recovered.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cutaneous anthrax caused by Bacillus anthracis. Although the direct culture of the skin lesion was negative, the diagnosis was confirmed by several key findings. Blood cultures grew gram-positive rods. A skin biopsy showed abundant bacilli, and immunohistochemical staining was positive for B. anthracis antigens. Furthermore, serology on convalescent serum was positive for antibodies to the protective antigen. The exposure was determined to be occupational, from handling contaminated mail during a bioterrorism event.", "disease_name_short": "Cutaneous anthrax", "source_filename": "21---A-35-Year-Old-American-Man-With-Fatigue-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropica.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old male farmer from the Peruvian highlands who was previously healthy.", "chief_complaint": "A 2-month history of a slowly growing ulcer and multiple painless nodules on his left arm.", "history_of_present_illness": "Two months before presentation, the patient sustained a scratch on his left hand from a tree branch. A few days later, a small painless erythematous papule developed, which evolved into a pustule. He took antibiotics and used topical traditional medicines without improvement. Over the following weeks, the lesion ulcerated and increased in size. Four weeks after the initial papule, he noticed the appearance of painless firm nodules on his left forearm. He received a 20-day course of pentavalent antimonials at a regional hospital for suspected cutaneous leishmaniasis but showed no improvement, leading to his referral.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a farmer from the Peruvian highlands. The infection began after a traumatic inoculation from a tree branch scratch while he was working in the fields. He denied recent contact with animals or travel.", "vitals": "His vital signs were normal and he was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "On his left hand, there was a single ulcerative lesion (20 x 30mm) with irregular elevated borders. Multiple subcutaneous erythematous nodules (10 x 10mm) were present along the lymphatic tract of the left arm. The rest of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The patient's WBC was 4.5 x 10^9/L with a normal differential, and his haemoglobin was 11.3 g/dL. A chest x-ray was normal and a PPD skin test was negative. Direct microscopy for leishmania parasites was negative, and a leishmanin skin test was also negative. Mycobacterial cultures were negative. However, cultures of an ulcer scraping and a nodule aspiration were positive for Sporothrix schenckii.", "differential_diagnosis": "The main differential diagnoses for a chronic ulcer with nodular lymphangitis in this patient included cutaneous leishmaniasis, sporotrichosis, and mycobacterial infection. Leishmaniasis was initially suspected due to the patient's origin and clinical presentation but was considered unlikely due to the lack of response to a full course of pentavalent antimonials. Sporotrichosis was a key consideration as the clinical features were typical for the disease. Mycobacterial infection with M. tuberculosis was considered, while M. marinum was unlikely due to no history of exposure to aquatic environments.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's condition did not improve with initial treatments of antibiotics, traditional medicines, or a 20-day course of pentavalent antimonials. Following the definitive diagnosis, he was started on itraconazole 200mg per day orally. After 8 months of treatment, the ulcer and nodular lesions had completely resolved, and the patient was considered cured.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis caused by the fungus Sporothrix schenckii. This was confirmed by positive cultures of S. schenckii from both an ulcer scraping and an aspirate of a lymphatic nodule. The diagnosis was further supported by the classic clinical presentation of nodular lymphangitis following a traumatic plant injury and the failure to respond to empiric therapy for leishmaniasis, which was also ruled out by negative diagnostic tests.", "disease_name_short": "Lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis", "source_filename": "34---A-35-Year-Old-Male-Farmer-from-Peru-With-a-Chro_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
