{"patient_information": "A 27-year-old male Swiss tourist with no other relevant medical history mentioned.", "chief_complaint": "Persisting, incapacitating polyarthralgias for several weeks after returning from travel.", "history_of_present_illness": "During the second week of a 3.5-week trip, the patient developed an acute febrile illness with chills, malaise, frontal headache, and generalized myalgia. About one week after fever onset, a transient, non-pruritic maculopapular rash started on his forearms, spread to his trunk, neck, and face, then faded after 3 days. He also experienced slowly progressive, debilitating polyarthralgias in peripheral joints with transient swelling, and self-limiting painful cervical and inguinal lymphadenopathy. While in Peru, he was clinically diagnosed with dengue fever and treated with paracetamol. The fever and other symptoms subsided within a week, but the polyarthralgias persisted, showing a symmetrical pattern affecting the small joints of the hands and feet, wrists, ankles, and knees. He reported stiffness in these joints, mainly in the morning and after immobility. The joint pain continued for over two months after returning home.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had spent 3.5 weeks travelling in the rainforests of the Amazon Basin in northern Peru. His illness began during the second week of his trip. Mayaro virus infection is strongly associated with occupational or recreational exposure in rainforest environments in South America.", "vitals": null, "physical_exam": "Upon evaluation in Switzerland, the physical examination was completely unremarkable. The affected joints did not reveal any clinical signs of inflammation such as swelling, redness, or effusion.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial serological testing in Switzerland for dengue virus, chikungunya virus, parvovirus B19, Epstein-Barr virus, Borrelia burgdorferi, Chlamydia trachomatis, Salmonella Typhi, and S. Paratyphi did not reveal a cause. Subsequent tests showed a normal full blood count, normal liver function tests, and a mildly elevated C-reactive protein at 9 mg/L (normal <5). The diagnosis was confirmed by serology, which was positive for Mayaro virus on two separate samples using indirect immunofluorescence assay (IgM-IFA, IgG-IFA) and a plaque reduction neutralization test (PRNT). Tests for other alphaviruses including Sindbis, Chikungunya, Ross River, and Barmah Forest were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The main differential diagnoses considered were reactive arthritis and viral arthritis. Reactive arthritis was considered, but the patient's symmetrical polyarthritis affecting small peripheral joints was more characteristic of viral arthritis. Given the travel history and clinical syndrome, arthropod-borne alphaviruses were the primary suspicion among viral causes.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient initially received symptomatic treatment with paracetamol while in Peru. After the diagnosis of Mayaro infection was confirmed, he was treated symptomatically with ibuprofen. The polyarthralgias subsided slowly over the following weeks and months before disappearing completely.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Mayaro fever, caused by the Mayaro virus (MAYV), an alphavirus. This was confirmed serologically with positive IgM and IgG antibodies on indirect immunofluorescence assay (IFA) and a positive plaque reduction neutralization test (PRNT). The diagnosis is strongly supported by the clinical presentation of an acute febrile illness with a rash, followed by persistent, debilitating, symmetrical polyarthralgias, and a relevant travel history to the Amazon rainforest in Peru, an endemic region for MAYV.", "disease_name_short": "Mayaro fever", "source_filename": "87---A-27-Year-Old-Male-Traveller-Returning-from-the_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
