{"patient_information": "A 20-year-old housewife, who is a Sudanese refugee living in a camp in northern Uganda.", "chief_complaint": "A 2-day history of fever, severe asthenia, chest and abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhoea.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 2-day history of fever, severe asthenia, chest and abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, and a slight non-productive cough. On admission, she was prostrate and semiconscious. Her condition rapidly worsened following admission, progressing to multi-organ dysfunction, severe hemorrhage, and shock.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient was a Sudanese refugee residing in a camp in northern Uganda. This case occurred during an outbreak of Ebola virus disease in the region. The patient denied any contact with sick people.", "vitals": "On admission, vital signs were: temperature 39.6\u00b0C (103.3\u00b0F), blood pressure 90/60 mmHg, pulse 90 bpm, and respiratory rate 24 cycles per minute. Her condition later deteriorated to hypothermic shock with a temperature of 36.0\u00b0C, an unreadable blood pressure, a pulse of 150 bpm, and a respiratory rate of 36 cycles per minute.", "physical_exam": "On admission, the patient was prostrate and semiconscious with abdominal tenderness, especially in the right upper quadrant, hepatosplenomegaly, and bleeding from the gums. Her lungs were clear, and no rash or lymphadenopathy was noted. Her condition progressed to include subconjunctival haemorrhage and copious bleeding from the mouth, nose, and rectum before she became comatose.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initially, no routine clinical laboratory data were available due to biosafety precautions during the outbreak. Subsequent testing at a specialized laboratory showed positive ELISA antigen and PCR tests for Ebola virus. An ELISA IgG antibody test for Ebola virus was negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The clinical presentation was considered classic for a viral haemorrhagic fever (HF) syndrome. The primary differential diagnoses for febrile syndromes with haemorrhage in the region include complicated malaria, typhoid fever, bacterial gastroenteritis, and leptospirosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to an isolation ward and treated with intravenous fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and analgesics. Despite supportive care, her condition rapidly worsened. She developed severe, widespread haemorrhage, dyspnoea, and hypothermic shock, became comatose, and died approximately 24 hours after admission.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Ebola virus disease. The patient presented with a classic viral hemorrhagic fever syndrome including fever, shock, and hemorrhage, during a known Ebola outbreak in northern Uganda. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by positive ELISA antigen and PCR tests for Ebola virus from a specialized laboratory.", "disease_name_short": "Ebola virus disease", "source_filename": "1---A-20-Year-Old-Woman-from-Sudan-With-Fever--_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
