{"patient_information": "A 55-year-old woman with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension for which she has been on treatment for 4 years.", "chief_complaint": "A 1-day history of fever, headache and vomiting, followed by confusion on the second day of illness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented to a rural hospital with a one-day history of fever, headache, and vomiting. A blood film for malaria was negative, and she was treated with fluids and paracetamol. On the second day of her illness, she developed confusion and was referred to a hospital in Ho Chi Minh City for further investigation.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from a rural area in Vietnam where she raises pigs on her smallholding and works as a butcher in a local market, selling pork and chicken. Both P. falciparum and P. vivax malaria are endemic in rural Vietnam.", "vitals": "On admission, she had a fever of 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F), tachycardia at 124 bpm, hypotension at 85/60 mmHg, tachypnoea with 32 breaths per minute, and hypoxia with an O2 saturation of 86% on ambient air.", "physical_exam": "The patient was confused with a Glasgow Coma Score of 12/15. She exhibited neck stiffness and photophobia with no focal neurological signs. Examination revealed a diffuse petechial rash over her trunk and marked purpuric, non-blanching lesions on her left leg. The rest of her examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood film for malaria was negative. Admission labs showed a WBC of 10.25 x 10^9/L with 86.5% neutrophils, hemoglobin of 11.8 g/dL, random blood glucose of 15.4 mmol/L, and a high lactate of 11.2 mmol/L. A non-contrast CT scan of the brain and a chest x-ray were unremarkable. Lumbar puncture revealed turbid cerebrospinal fluid with 2870 cells/\u03bcL (92% neutrophils), protein of 541.16 mg/dL, and a CSF glucose of 2.44 mmol/l with a concurrent blood glucose of 19 mmol/L (CSF/serum ratio 0.12). CSF Gram stain showed a Gram-positive coccus in short chains. Repeat malaria film and rapid diagnostic tests were negative. Five days later, both blood and CSF cultures grew Streptococcus suis, which was sensitive to penicillin and ceftriaxone.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included severe malaria, which must be excluded in rural Vietnam. Enteric fever was considered but the rapid progression was uncharacteristic. Dengue fever was also a possibility, but the rapid deterioration with signs of meningitis and shock was not typical. Melioidosis was considered due to her diabetes, but it is comparatively rare in Vietnam. The most likely diagnosis was acute bacterial meningitis complicated by septic shock, with Streptococcus suis being the most common identified cause in adults in Vietnam, often presenting with meningitis, shock, and a purpuric rash.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated empirically with intravenous ceftriaxone and vancomycin, along with supportive care including intravenous fluids. Dexamethasone was administered for the first four days. Following culture results confirming S. suis, she completed a 12-day course of antibiotic therapy. She responded to treatment but developed residual bilateral hearing impairment.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Streptococcus suis meningitis complicated by septic shock. The diagnosis was confirmed by the growth of Streptococcus suis from both blood and cerebrospinal fluid cultures. The clinical presentation of acute meningitis, shock, a characteristic purpuric rash, and CSF findings of high neutrophilic pleocytosis and a very low CSF/serum glucose ratio were all consistent with this diagnosis. Her occupation as a pig farmer and butcher provided a clear epidemiological link for this zoonotic infection.", "disease_name_short": "Streptococcus suis meningitis", "source_filename": "85---A-55-Year-Old-Female-Pig-Farmer-from-Vietnam-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
