{"patient_information": "A 55-year-old, previously healthy Swiss couple, both fitness instructors. No other comorbidities or relevant medical history is mentioned.", "chief_complaint": "A 55-year-old man presented with fever, myalgias, and generalized weakness for 4 days, followed by shortness of breath. His wife presented three weeks later with similar complaints.", "history_of_present_illness": "The husband developed a fever of 39\u00b0C, myalgias, and generalized weakness on his flight back to Switzerland, which did not respond to symptomatic treatment. Four days later at the hospital, he remained febrile and was too weak to walk unassisted. He then developed shortness of breath with oxygen desaturation. His wife presented three weeks later with similar complaints and deteriorated rapidly over 24 hours after admission, developing cardiorespiratory failure and multi-organ failure.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The couple had recently returned from a 3-month trip from Ecuador to Chile. The last month of their trip was spent hiking, camping, and taking occasional mud baths in the Chile-Argentinian border region. The mode of transmission remained unclear; both human-to-human-transmission and acquisition from a common source were considered possible.", "vitals": "Husband at presentation: Blood pressure 90/55 mmHg, pulse 120 beats per minute, oxygen saturation 94% on ambient air, and temperature 39\u00b0C (102.2\u00b0F). Wife on admission: normal blood pressure, heart rate and oxygen saturation, temperature 38.6\u00b0C (101.5\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "For both the husband and wife, there were no abnormal findings on cardiopulmonary auscultation at the time of their respective admissions.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "On admission, the husband's labs showed WCC 7.3 G/L with 35% band neutrophils, platelets 48 G/L, and CRP 68 mg/L. His wife's labs showed WCC 1.88 G/L, platelets 98 G/L, and CRP 7 mg/L. Both had normal glomerular filtration rates and transaminases. The husband's respiratory pathogen panel and HIV test were negative. Chest radiography was normal at presentation in both patients, but during the course of the disease, both developed extensive bilateral infiltrates with pleural effusions.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for acute respiratory distress syndrome after travel to South America included influenza and other respiratory viruses, pneumococcal pneumonia, and severe malaria. Rickettsial diseases like spotted fever group rickettsiosis and scrub typhus were considered. New World hantaviruses were a strong consideration due to the cardiopulmonary presentation, the long incubation period which could explain the three-week gap between cases, and the potential for human-to-human transmission.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The husband was transferred to the ICU for non-invasive ventilator support for 5 days and made a full recovery after a month of rehabilitation. His wife experienced a more dramatic course, requiring extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) for nearly a month. She developed multi-organ failure with renal replacement therapy and disseminated intravascular coagulation, leading to profuse bleeding and requiring mass transfusions. She underwent several surgical interventions for bleeding complications, developed nosocomial infections, and remained in intensive care for 5 months, followed by an additional 12 months in rehabilitation.", "final_diagnosis": "Both patients were diagnosed with Hantavirus Cardiopulmonary Syndrome (HCPS) caused by Andesvirus (ANDV), a New World species of the family Hantaviridae. The diagnosis was confirmed by both PCR and serology. This diagnosis explained the severe cardiorespiratory failure, thrombocytopenia, and the epidemiological link to their travel in a region where ANDV is endemic.", "disease_name_short": "Hantavirus Cardiopulmonary Syndrome", "source_filename": "90---A-55-Year-Old-Couple-Both-Returning-from-Chile-a_2022_Clinical-Cases-in.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 71-year-old Japanese man, born and currently living in the Kyushu district of southern Japan. He is clinically well with no reported complaints.", "chief_complaint": "Referral for further work-up of an asymptomatic nodular lesion in the right lung.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was found to have a lung lesion two years prior during a routine health check. Initially, the lesion was of a linear shape and was followed regularly. On a recent follow-up visit, chest radiography and CT scan revealed that the linear shadow had transformed into a nodular lesion approximately 2 cm in diameter. The patient remained asymptomatic throughout this period.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a lifelong resident of the Kyushu district in southern Japan, a region where paragonimiasis is endemic. He has no history of travelling overseas.", "vitals": "The vital signs were normal.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination was normal. The chest was clear on auscultation.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A full blood count revealed a white blood cell count of 6.5 x 10^9/L with an eosinophil percentage of 8.3% (total eosinophil count 0.54 x 10^9/L), indicating mild eosinophilia. Total IgE was elevated at 185.7 U/mL. Liver function, renal function, electrolytes, LDH, and CRP were all normal. Serologies for Cryptococcus Ag, Aspergillus Ag, and \u03b2-D-glucan were negative, as was a QuantiFERON test for tuberculosis. A chest radiograph and CT scan identified a nodular lesion of about 2 cm in the right lung. An FDG-PET-CT showed increased FDG uptake in this lesion. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) cytology was negative for malignant cells and acid-fast bacilli, and tumor markers were negative. The diagnosis was confirmed with a multiple-dot ELISA which was strongly positive for P. westermani.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses for a lung nodule with increased FDG uptake were pulmonary tuberculosis and lung cancer. Systemic endemic mycoses were considered but deemed unlikely as the patient had no travel history to endemic areas outside of East Asia. Given the patient's origin in southern Japan, eosinophilia, and elevated IgE, pulmonary helminth infection, specifically lung fluke infection (paragonimiasis), was strongly suspected.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a 3-day course of oral praziquantel at a dose of 25 mg/kg three times daily. Following treatment, the lung lesion gradually faded and eventually disappeared. His serum antibody titre for P. westermani also declined, indicating a successful response to therapy.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is pulmonary paragonimiasis caused by the lung fluke Paragonimus westermani. This was established based on the patient's epidemiological risk as a resident of an endemic area in Japan, the clinical presentation of an asymptomatic lung nodule, and laboratory findings of peripheral eosinophilia and elevated IgE. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a strongly positive multiple-dot ELISA for P. westermani and further supported by the complete resolution of the lung lesion and decline in antibody titers following treatment with praziquantel.", "disease_name_short": "Paragonimiasis", "source_filename": "71---A-71-Year-Old-Man-from-Japan-With-Eosinophili_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
