{"patient_information": "A 36-year-old male restaurant owner presented for assessment. He is married and took malaria chemoprophylaxis for his recent travel. His HIV antibody test was negative.", "chief_complaint": "A mobile itchy mass under the skin for three weeks.", "history_of_present_illness": "Three weeks prior to presentation, the patient first noted a mass in his groin which lasted for 4 days before subsiding. Following this, he developed an itchy, serpiginous rash that tracked from his groin to his chest over several days and then disappeared. The mass then reappeared on his right shoulder, prompting him to seek assessment. He reported no history of fever or systemic illness.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "Five weeks before presentation, the patient returned from a fishing holiday in the Okavango Delta in Botswana with four friends. During the trip, he slept on a boat under a mosquito net, swam in fresh river water, and ate freshwater fish from the river. He later admitted that they had made and consumed sushi from fresh fish caught in the river. Two of the four friends who accompanied him developed the same complaint, indicating a common source exposure.", "vitals": "The patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "On his right upper deltoid, there was a firm mass with clear margins, 3x8 cm in size. A serpiginous tract was visible, tracking from the mass across his upper chest wall. The remainder of the organ system examination was normal, with no additional rash and no lymphadenopathy.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a normal total white cell count of 8.9 x 10^9/L but a significant eosinophilia with an eosinophil count of 0.9 x 10^9/L (reference <0.5). Liver function tests, creatinine, and electrolytes were normal. Serology for Gnathostoma was negative. The diagnosis was confirmed when the patient expressed a larva from his nostril, which was identified as Gnathostoma spinigerum.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis included other parasitic causes of migratory swelling and eosinophilia. Loa loa infection (calabar swelling) was considered but was unlikely as it is not endemic in the Okavango Delta and has a minimum incubation period of 5 months. Sparganosis, another rare cause, typically occurs in Asia. Other creeping eruptions, such as those from animal hookworms (cutaneous larva migrans) or Strongyloides stercoralis, were less likely as they present with fine, serpiginous tracks rather than a large mobile mass.", "management_and_clinical_course": "A clinical diagnosis of cutaneous gnathostomiasis was made, and the patient was started on albendazole 400mg twice daily for 21 days and a single dose of ivermectin 200 mcg/kg. Over the next six days, the lesion migrated over his shoulder, neck, disappeared for 24 hours, and then reappeared between his eyebrows, moving across his forehead and face before being felt inside his nose. On the sixth day of treatment, he successfully expressed the larva from his nostril. His two symptomatic friends received similar treatment with full resolution of their symptoms.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cutaneous gnathostomiasis caused by the nematode Gnathostoma spinigerum. This was established based on the classic clinical presentation of a migratory subcutaneous mass and serpiginous rash, significant peripheral eosinophilia, and a clear epidemiologic link of eating raw freshwater fish in an endemic area. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the physical recovery and identification of the G. spinigerum larva from the patient's nostril during treatment.", "disease_name_short": "Gnathostomiasis", "source_filename": "6---A-36-Year-Old-Male-Traveller-Returning-from-B_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 55-year-old indigenous Australian woman. She has been living in a crowded back room of a house in a remote community.", "chief_complaint": "Widespread exfoliating rash and signs of sepsis.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient's family reported that she had become increasingly withdrawn and had not been getting out of the house much at all prior to presentation.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in a remote indigenous community in tropical northern Australia where scabies is endemic. She was living in a crowded back room. Many household members, particularly children, were found to have multiple scabies lesions, some with secondary pyoderma, indicating she was a 'core transmitter'.", "vitals": "Temperature was 39.5\u00b0C (103.1\u00b0F), heart rate 110 bpm, respiratory rate 28 breaths per minute, and blood pressure was 85 mmHg systolic. Oxygen saturation was 92% on room air.", "physical_exam": "The patient was pale, clammy, and poorly responsive. She had a widespread exfoliative rash involving all limbs, with particular severity in the armpits, buttocks, and thighs. Numerous skin flakes were present on her mattress. She also had fissures over her wrists and knees.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Microscopy of skin scrapings confirmed the diagnosis, showing multiple scabies mites of varying maturity and multiple eggs. Initial blood cultures grew Staphylococcus aureus resistant to flucloxacillin and clindamycin but sensitive to gentamicin, co-trimoxazole, and doxycycline (community-acquired MRSA). Repeated blood cultures 48 hours after admission were negative. A transthoracic echocardiogram showed no features of endocarditis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary provisional diagnosis was crusted scabies, given the classical exfoliating rash in a patient from an endemic community. Secondary sepsis was also diagnosed, with the likely cause being Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes bacteremia from inoculation through skin fissures. Gram-negative or polymicrobial sepsis were also considered possibilities.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Initial management included oxygen, a wide-bore cannula, 1 litre of normal saline, and intravenous ceftriaxone and gentamicin. After stabilization, she was evacuated to a regional hospital and managed in a single room with contact precautions. Treatment for scabies consisted of a five-dose course of ivermectin (200 \u03bcg/kg/dose) combined with topical benzyl benzoate and a keratolytic cream. Following culture results, she was treated with two weeks of IV vancomycin for the MRSA bacteremia. She became afebrile and was off oxygen within 48 hours. After three weeks, her skin showed no residual hyperkeratosis and she returned to her community. Her family members were also treated for scabies.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was crusted scabies with secondary sepsis caused by community-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). The crusted scabies was confirmed by microscopy of skin scrapings showing numerous mites and eggs. The secondary MRSA bacteremia was confirmed by blood cultures. The patient showed significant clinical improvement and resolution of skin lesions following targeted treatment with ivermectin, topical scabicides, and intravenous vancomycin.", "disease_name_short": "Crusted scabies", "source_filename": "10---A-55-Year-Old-Indigenous-Woman-from-Australia-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 32-year-old Malawian woman who appeared wasted and slightly anaemic. An HIV test was positive, and her CD4 count was very low at 22/\u03bcL.", "chief_complaint": "A 3-week history of headache and blurred vision.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a headache of gradual onset over 3 weeks that did not respond to over-the-counter painkillers. She also reported blurred vision. There was no fever, history of convulsions, or head trauma. Prior to presentation, she received presumptive antimalarial treatment with artemether/lumefantrine and a 5-day course of amoxicillin from a local health centre, neither of which provided any benefit.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and resides in a region with a high prevalence of HIV.", "vitals": "All vital signs were normal and the patient was afebrile on presentation.", "physical_exam": "The patient was wasted and slightly anaemic with a GCS of 15/15 and no neck stiffness. Visual acuity was normal. Examination revealed an abduction deficit of the left eye on left lateral gaze, consistent with an abducens nerve palsy. The rest of the examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood tests showed a WBC of 3.7 x 10^9/L, normocytic anaemia with a haemoglobin of 10.2 g/dL, and thrombocytopenia. A malaria RDT was negative. HIV serology was positive with a CD4 count of 22/\u03bcL. A lumbar puncture revealed a markedly increased opening pressure of 50 cmH2O. The CSF was clear with 0 leukocytes/\u03bcL, elevated protein at 0.8 g/L, and low glucose at 1.97 mmol/L. India Ink stain of the CSF was negative, but the fungal culture was positive for Cryptococcus neoformans.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was chronic meningitis, with the most likely causes being cryptococcal meningitis and tuberculous meningitis, given the patient's presentation and epidemiology. Partially treated bacterial meningitis was considered but was less likely due to the gradual onset of symptoms. Malaria was considered unlikely due to the absence of fever, a negative RDT, and lack of cranial nerve palsy.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with cryptococcal meningitis and started on oral fluconazole 1200mg, as amphotericin B and flucytosine were unavailable. She was also started on co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. To manage the raised intracranial pressure and headache, she received repeated therapeutic lumbar punctures. Antiretroviral therapy was initiated 4 weeks after starting antifungal treatment. Despite this management, the patient returned to her village and died 6 weeks later of an unknown cause.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cryptococcal meningitis caused by Cryptococcus neoformans. This was confirmed by a positive fungal culture from the cerebrospinal fluid. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical presentation of chronic headache and a cranial nerve palsy in a severely immunocompromised patient (newly diagnosed with HIV and a CD4 count of 22/\u03bcL), along with a markedly elevated CSF opening pressure.", "disease_name_short": "Cryptococcal meningitis", "source_filename": "35---A-32-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-Heada_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 28-year-old woman from Sierra Leone.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-day history of fever, weakness, sore throat and retrosternal chest pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 28-year-old woman presented to a rural hospital with a 6-day history of fever, weakness, sore throat, and retrosternal chest pain. For the past two days, she also had loose stools twice daily. Prior to admission, she was treated at a local health post with artemether-amodiaquine and amoxicillin but her condition worsened. In the hospital, she received IV ceftriaxone and completed her antimalarials but continued to deteriorate over two days. On the second day after admission, she developed conjunctivitis, cough, and breathlessness, becoming so unwell that she was unable to walk unaided.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient presented to a small rural hospital in eastern Sierra Leone, an area where Lassa fever is common, accounting for 16\u201320% of hospital admissions in some studies.", "vitals": "On physical examination, her axillary temperature was 38.2\u00b0C (100.8\u00b0F), blood pressure was 80/55 mmHg, and pulse rate was 100 bpm.", "physical_exam": "Her initial examination was unremarkable except for mild pharyngitis. Later in her course, she developed conjunctivitis. Chest auscultation revealed bilateral fine crepitations. She was noted to be very unwell and unable to walk unaided.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Her malaria slide was negative. A chest radiograph revealed diffuse bilateral infiltrates. Blood chemistry showed mild renal impairment and raised transaminases, with an aspartate transaminase (AST/GOT) of 514 U/L. The diagnosis of Lassa fever was confirmed with antigen testing.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for her severe febrile illness with pharyngitis, conjunctivitis, and chest involvement included several conditions. Adenovirus and measles were considered, but were less likely as adenovirus is typically mild and a rash would be expected with measles. Mycoplasma pneumoniae is rarely this severe. Other pneumonias do not normally cause conjunctivitis. Leptospirosis can cause pulmonary and renal issues but typically presents with conjunctival suffusions rather than conjunctivitis. Typhoid fever was considered, but improvement on ceftriaxone would be expected and conjunctivitis is not typical. Lassa fever was highly suspected as all her signs and symptoms were consistent with the disease and it is common in her region.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with artemether with amodiaquine and amoxicillin, followed by IV ceftriaxone in the hospital, but she continued to worsen. After the diagnosis of Lassa fever was confirmed with antigen testing, she was managed in a specialist isolation unit and received IV ribavirin. Unfortunately, the patient was already very unwell by this stage and died two days later. Public health measures were initiated, and all close contacts were advised to monitor their temperature for 21 days.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Lassa fever. This was confirmed by a positive antigen test. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical syndrome of a severe febrile illness with pharyngitis, conjunctivitis, pneumonitis, renal impairment, and markedly elevated liver transaminases that did not respond to broad-spectrum antibiotics or antimalarials. The patient's presence in eastern Sierra Leone, a known endemic area for Lassa fever, provided strong epidemiological evidence.", "disease_name_short": "Lassa fever", "source_filename": "74---A-28-Year-Old-Woman-from-Sierra-Leone-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 30-year-old male Chinese itinerant trader.", "chief_complaint": "A 7-day history of fever, chills, headache and a dry cough.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with 7 days of fever, chills, headache and a dry cough. He had recently developed slide-positive falciparum malaria whilst living in southern Laos and was treated with intramuscular artemether 80 mg for 5 days, which he had brought from China as standby therapy. He did not improve; the fever persisted, and jaundice developed, leading to his transfer to a hospital in Vientiane.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a Chinese itinerant trader who was living in southern Laos, where he contracted falciparum malaria. The case notes that artemisinin-resistant falciparum malaria has been described from southern Laos.", "vitals": "On admission, he was febrile with a temperature of 39.5\u00b0C (103.1\u00b0F). His blood pressure and Glasgow Coma Score were normal.", "physical_exam": "On admission, the patient had nausea, dry cough, moderate dehydration, chest pain and abdominal tenderness. His chest was clear on auscultation and no hepatosplenomegaly was detected.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A Giemsa smear on admission was negative for malaria parasites, but a rapid diagnostic test (HRP-2) was positive for Plasmodium falciparum, consistent with recent infection. Serum creatinine and glucose were normal. Laboratory results showed elevated direct bilirubin at 6.4 \u00b5mol/L (reference <4.3 \u00b5mol/L) and elevated liver transaminases (ALT, AST).", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnoses included bacterial coinfection with falciparum malaria, especially with Salmonella species. Other common infectious diseases in Laos such as scrub typhus, murine typhus, leptospirosis, tuberculosis, or dengue fever were also considered. The possibility of artemisinin-resistant falciparum malaria, incorrect dosing, poor drug quality, or issues with intestinal absorption of the artemether were also raised.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with oral quinine sulphate 10 mg/kg every 8 hours and doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days. His fever cleared 35 hours after starting this therapy, and he was discharged well. No evidence was found for any other infections. Subsequent analysis of the artemether vial he used, via high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) and mass spectrometry (MS), demonstrated it was substandard and only contained 59 mg of artemether, which is 74% of the stated amount.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was treatment failure of falciparum malaria due to the use of a substandard medication. The diagnosis was based on the patient's persistent fever after a course of artemether, a positive HRP-2 test for Plasmodium falciparum, and a rapid clinical response to a standard course of quinine and doxycycline. The cause was confirmed by chemical analysis showing the artemether he used was subtherapeutic, containing only 74% of the stated active ingredient, which resulted in underdosing.", "disease_name_short": "Falciparum malaria", "source_filename": "39---A-30-Year-Old-Male-Chinese-Trader-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 58-year-old Sri Lankan woman from a rural area with no other comorbidities mentioned.", "chief_complaint": "High-grade fever, body aches, cough, and shortness of breath for 10 days, with recent onset of hearing loss and confusion.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 10-day history of high-grade fever, chills, body aches, a non-productive cough, and progressive shortness of breath. Two days prior to admission, she developed tinnitus and hearing loss. One day before admission, she became increasingly confused.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in a rural area of Sri Lanka. The case discusses scrub typhus, an infection transmitted by the bite of an infected chigger (mite larva), which is common in various habitats including scrubs and forests in Asia.", "vitals": "On admission, her temperature was 39.3\u00b0C (102.7\u00b0F), heart rate was 120 bpm, and blood pressure was 90/60 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "On admission, the patient was confused with a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score of 13/15 and had severely impaired hearing (WHO grade 3). There were enlarged right axillary lymph nodes, hepatomegaly (5 cm below the costal margin), a palpable spleen, and scattered crackles at the lung bases. There was no rash or neck stiffness. On day 2, her GCS deteriorated to 8, and she developed coarse tremors and saccadic eye oscillations. A careful re-examination revealed a well-demarcated, crater-like eschar hidden in the right axilla.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial labs showed a normal white cell count (8.5 x 10^9/L), slight thrombocytopenia, and mildly elevated AST and ALT with normal bilirubin. Creatinine was elevated at 123.8 \u00b5mol/L. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis revealed a lymphocytic pleocytosis (35 cells/\u00b5L, 80% lymphocytes), with elevated protein (0.64 g/L) and slightly decreased glucose. A thick film for malaria was negative, and blood cultures showed no growth. Serologies for Leptospira and Salmonella were negative. A chest radiograph and brain CT scan were normal, while an EEG showed widespread slowing. After treatment was initiated, the patient\u2019s O. tsutsugamushi IFA-IgG titre was reported as high (>1:4096).", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses considered for this undifferentiated febrile illness in a patient from Asia included scrub typhus, other rickettsial infections, and leptospirosis. Malaria and bacterial sepsis with meningoencephalitis due to pathogens like Salmonella were also considered, but the thick film, blood cultures, and specific serologies were negative.", "management_and_clinical_course": "After an eschar was found on day 2, the patient was started on doxycycline. She improved significantly over the following 48 hours. Her hearing returned to normal, and there were no other neurological deficits upon discharge. She was discharged on day 6 after admission.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was scrub typhus caused by Orientia tsutsugamushi. This was based on the patient's clinical presentation of fever, deafness, and confusion, her residence in an endemic rural area, the key physical finding of an eschar in the right axilla with corresponding lymphadenopathy, and a rapid clinical response to doxycycline therapy. The diagnosis was supported by a high convalescent O. tsutsugamushi IFA-IgG titre of >1:4096.", "disease_name_short": "Scrub typhus", "source_filename": "70---A-58-Year-Old-Woman-from-Sri-Lanka-With-Fev_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 24-year-old woman from rural Uganda.", "chief_complaint": "A 5-day history of a febrile illness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began five days prior with fever, sore throat, and generalized aching. She subsequently developed abdominal pain and diarrhoea. Over the course of the past few days, she has become increasingly unwell, experiencing significant weakness to the point of needing assistance to stand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in rural Uganda. Her husband died from a severe febrile illness 6 days before she fell ill. He worked in a local gold mine, and she had cared for him at home during his final illness. Marburg virus disease has been associated with mines.", "vitals": "Her blood pressure is 85/65 mmHg, pulse rate is 105 bpm, and temperature is 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient appears very unwell and has bilateral conjunctivitis. There is no rash or lymphadenopathy. The heart sounds are normal, her chest is clear, and the abdominal examination is normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A malaria slide showed a low level of parasitaemia with P. falciparum. A sample was sent for Marburg and Ebola PCR, which later returned a positive result for Marburg virus.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis was broad given the non-specific presentation. Malaria and typhoid fever were considered but do not typically cause conjunctivitis. Other bacterial sepsis, including meningococcal disease, was a possibility. Severe viral infections like adenovirus or influenza were considered less likely due to the severity of illness. Measles was considered due to conjunctivitis but was less likely without a rash. Zika virus was also considered but usually causes a milder illness. The death of her husband from a similar illness raised high suspicion for a viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF), particularly Marburg virus disease, which has been associated with mines.", "management_and_clinical_course": "A presumed diagnosis of Marburg virus disease was made. The patient was isolated and treated empirically with IV artesunate followed by artemether/lumefantrine for malaria, IV ceftriaxone for possible sepsis, and was resuscitated with IV fluids. She remained very unwell for several days before starting to improve. After recovery, she was kept in isolation for two additional days before being discharged home. Public health authorities were alerted, and her contacts were monitored for 21 days.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Marburg virus disease. This was confirmed by a positive Marburg virus PCR result. The clinical presentation of a severe febrile illness with shock and conjunctivitis, along with the critical epidemiological link to her husband who worked in a mine and died of a similar illness, strongly pointed towards a viral hemorrhagic fever. A co-infection with Plasmodium falciparum was noted but was not considered the cause of her severe presentation.", "disease_name_short": "Marburg virus disease", "source_filename": "53---A-24-Year-Old-Woman-from-Uganda-With-Fe_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 28-year-old Malawian man who works as a fisherman. He was diagnosed with arterial hypertension two years prior but was non-compliant with prescribed medication. A recent HIV test was negative, and his medical and family histories are otherwise unremarkable.", "chief_complaint": "Progressive shortness of breath for the past 5 days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 5-day history of progressive shortness of breath, accompanied by orthopnoea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea. He also developed continuous, dull bilateral flank pain and constant nausea in the days leading up to admission. He reported no cough and no fever.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a fisherman from a town on the southern shore of Lake Malawi. His occupation involves regular contact with the lake water, which is known to be infested with Schistosoma haematobium.", "vitals": "On admission, his blood pressure was 200/130 mmHg, pulse was 66 bpm, temperature was 36.8\u00b0C, and respiratory rate was 32 breath cycles per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was in respiratory distress with notably pale conjunctivae. His apex beat was slightly displaced, and there were bilateral fine crackles over the lung bases. The abdomen was flat and non-tender, but there was bilateral renal angle tenderness with ballottable kidneys. Jugular venous pressure was not raised, and there was no peripheral oedema. Later in his course, he developed a new pericardial friction rub.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a haemoglobin of 6.0 mg/dL, WBC of 3.8 x10^9/L, and evidence of severe renal failure with a BUN of 89.3 mmol/L and hyperkalaemia (K+ 7.2 mmol/l). His creatinine was noted to be very high. An ultrasound of the kidneys revealed bilateral hydronephrosis with massive dilatation of the renal pelvis and calyces, and very thin renal parenchyma. Cystoscopy showed a hyperaemic mucosa with multiple \u2018sandy patches\u2019 suggestive of granulomatous lesions.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential was to determine the cause of his combined cardiac and renal failure. The enlarged kidneys suggested either a primary condition like polycystic kidney disease or a post-renal obstruction with hydronephrosis. Renal failure secondary to his known hypertension was considered less likely, as this typically results in small kidneys. Given his occupation as a fisherman in Lake Malawi, chronic urogenital schistosomiasis causing obstructive uropathy was the leading differential diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with high doses of furosemide but remained oliguric. His hyperkalaemia was managed with glucose and insulin. Due to clinical deterioration, marked by drowsiness, vomiting, and a uraemic pericarditis, he underwent a bilateral percutaneous nephrostomy followed by peritoneal dialysis in the ICU. He improved rapidly and was discharged home on peritoneal dialysis. However, two months later, he was readmitted with fever and abdominal pain, was treated for suspected bacterial peritonitis, and died three weeks after admission.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with advanced urogenital schistosomiasis, caused by Schistosoma haematobium. The diagnosis was made based on the strong epidemiological link of being a fisherman in Lake Malawi, clinical signs of end-stage renal disease, ultrasound findings of severe bilateral hydronephrosis, and characteristic cystoscopy findings of \u2018sandy patches\u2019 in the bladder mucosa, which are pathognomonic for the disease. Histology was not available for confirmation.", "disease_name_short": "Urogenital schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "7---A-28-Year-Old-Male-Fisherman-from-Malawi-Wi_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old man, diagnosed with HIV 6 years ago, who is not yet on antiretroviral therapy (ART) or co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. His CD4 count was 280/\u03bcL one month prior to presentation, and on admission was 150/\u03bcL with an HIV viral load of 1,500,000 copies/mL. He denies any recreational drug use and has never smoked tobacco. He has no other significant past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-week history of increasing shortness of breath on exertion, dry cough, and drenching night sweats.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 6-week history of progressively worsening shortness of breath on exertion, a dry cough, and drenching night sweats. He also reported general fatigue and a weight loss of about 10kg over the preceding 5 months.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a business traveller who travels extensively. In the past 6 months, he had work trips to Europe, China, Korea, Japan, Singapore, and the United States. He lives with his male partner.", "vitals": "On presentation, his temperature was 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F), blood pressure was 120/70mmHg, pulse was 100 bpm, and respiratory rate was 26 breaths per minute. His oxygen saturation was 87% on ambient air, which improved to 97% on 15L of O2.", "physical_exam": "The patient was alert and able to complete full sentences, but was short of breath at rest. Auscultation of the chest was clear. The rest of the physical examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a normal full blood count and normal renal and liver function tests. C-reactive protein was elevated at 6.7 mg/dL. Arterial blood gases on ambient air showed a pH of 7.44 and a PaO2 of 7.6 kPa, consistent with type 1 respiratory failure. His CD4 count was 150/\u03bcL. A chest radiograph showed bilateral perihilar infiltrates. A CT-pulmonary angiogram ruled out pulmonary embolism but revealed extensive \u201cground-glass\u201d infiltrates in both lungs, especially in the lower lobes. Serum 1,3 \u03b2-D-glucan was markedly elevated at >500 pg/mL. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid analysis confirmed the diagnosis, with Grocott staining revealing Pneumocystis jirovecii cystic forms and a strongly positive PCR for P. jirovecii DNA.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP), given the patient's presentation as an ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive individual with a low CD4 count, progressive shortness of breath, and typical chest radiograph findings. Other considerations included tuberculosis, atypical bacterial pneumonia, cryptococcosis, or histoplasmosis. A CT pulmonary angiogram was performed to rule out pulmonary embolism.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was managed with high-flow oxygen, high-dose co-trimoxazole, and prednisolone. His tachycardia and hypoxia improved with treatment. Ten days into treatment, he commenced antiretroviral therapy with tenofovir, emtricitabine, and efavirenz. Two weeks into PCP treatment, he developed a pruritic maculopapular rash, leading to a change from co-trimoxazole to clindamycin and primaquine for the final week of his 21-day course, and his efavirenz was replaced. After completing treatment, he was started on dapsone and pyrimethamine for secondary prophylaxis.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is severe Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) caused by Pneumocystis jirovecii. This was established in an ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive patient with a low CD4 count (150/\u03bcL) presenting with classic subacute respiratory failure and hypoxia. The diagnosis was supported by imaging showing bilateral ground-glass infiltrates and a highly elevated serum \u03b2-glucan level (>500 pg/mL). It was definitively confirmed by bronchoalveolar lavage fluid analysis, which identified P. jirovecii cystic forms via Grocott staining and was strongly positive by PCR.", "disease_name_short": "Pneumocystis pneumonia", "source_filename": "23---A-31-Year-Old-HIV-Positive-Business-Traveller-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}

{"patient_information": "A 42-year-old German man with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-day history of fever up to 40\u00b0C, arthralgias, and retro-orbital pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 42-year-old man presented to a hospital in Germany with a 6-day history of fever reaching 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F), accompanied by arthralgias and retro-orbital pain. His symptoms started while traveling, and he presented the day after returning from a 10-week trip to Thailand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had just returned from a 10-week trip to Thailand. He spent two months on Little Koh Chang in the Andaman Sea, followed by five days in Hua Hin and five days in Bangkok. He did not take any antimalarial chemoprophylaxis, consistent with national recommendations for his travel itinerary.", "vitals": "On examination, his temperature was 39.1\u00b0C (102.4\u00b0F), blood pressure was 156/80 mmHg, pulse was 106 beats per minute, respiratory rate was 16 breaths per minute, and SpO2 was 95% on ambient air.", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was largely unremarkable. The patient was fully alert with a GCS of 15/15. There was no skin rash or lymphadenopathy. His chest was clear on auscultation, and the abdominal examination revealed no tenderness or organomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed thrombocytopenia of 81 x 10^9/L, an elevated CRP of 55 mg/L, and elevated liver enzymes (AST 62 U/L, ALT 106 U/L). A Dengue NS1 antigen test and a rapid diagnostic test for malaria were both negative. A subsequent thick blood film showed Plasmodium species trophozoites at a density of 920/\u03bcL. The species could not be identified on the thin film, but a species-specific PCR ultimately confirmed a mono-infection with P. knowlesi.", "differential_diagnosis": "Given the clinical syndrome and travel history, the primary differential diagnosis was dengue fever, although the duration of fever was long and the NS1 test was negative. Other considerations included Chikungunya, Zika, influenza, and acute HIV infection. Enteric fever was considered less likely. Malaria was a critical diagnosis to exclude, as a negative rapid diagnostic test is not sufficient to rule it out, especially for non-falciparum species.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the infectious diseases ward. Based on the positive blood film suspecting non-falciparum malaria, treatment was started with atovaquone/proguanil 250/100mg, four tablets once daily for three days. The patient recovered rapidly and was discharged on the fourth day.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Knowlesi malaria, caused by Plasmodium knowlesi. The diagnosis was confirmed by species-specific PCR after an initial thick blood film showed Plasmodium species trophozoites at 920/\u03bcL. This was despite a negative malaria rapid diagnostic test and initial difficulty in identifying the species by microscopy, which can be mistaken for P. malariae.", "disease_name_short": "Knowlesi malaria", "source_filename": "92---A-42-Year-Old-Traveller-Returning-from-Thaila_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 43-year-old German man with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Recurrent fever for three days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient complains of recurrent fever for the past three days, with a temperature as high as 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F) the day before presentation. He also reports some headache and diarrhoea. Approximately three weeks prior, while in Mozambique, he experienced fever and self-treated for presumed malaria with atovaquone/proguanil from April 27th to 29th.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a traveller who was in Mozambique from April 16th to April 30th, followed by a trip to Chile from April 30th to May 17th. He did not take any malaria chemoprophylaxis while in Mozambique. He reports freshwater contact in a small lake near Maputo, Mozambique.", "vitals": "On presentation, the patient was febrile with a temperature of 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F). The previous day, his temperature had reached 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was normal, with no rash, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, or lymphadenopathy noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a normal total white blood cell count (9.0 x 10^9/L) but significant eosinophilia (2.1 x 10^9/L). C-reactive protein was elevated at 57.9 mg/L, and liver enzymes (AST, ALT, GGT) and LDH were slightly raised. Thick films for Plasmodium species were negative. A chest radiograph and CT scan revealed nodular lesions (2-5mm) in the periphery of the lower lung zones bilaterally. Initial microscopy of three stool and urine samples for Schistosoma eggs were negative, as were initial antischistosomal antibody tests (enzyme-immunoassay and IFA). However, four weeks later, antischistosomal antibodies became positive (IFA 1:1280; cercarial and egg-ELISA positive), and Schistosoma mansoni ova were found in the stool.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis for fever in a returned traveler from Mozambique was malaria, especially given the lack of prophylaxis. Other considerations included typhoid fever and amoebic liver abscess. Viral infections like hepatitis, EBV, and CMV were considered due to elevated liver enzymes, but the absence of lymphocytosis made them less likely. The pronounced eosinophilia, however, strongly suggested a helminth infection. Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome) was the most likely diagnosis given the combination of fever, eosinophilia, and a history of freshwater exposure. Acute fascioliasis was also a rare possibility.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent diagnostic investigations. After initial tests for malaria and schistosomiasis were negative, repeat testing was performed. Four weeks after presentation, schistosomiasis was confirmed by positive serology and the detection of ova in the stool. The patient was subsequently treated with praziquantel.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome) caused by Schistosoma mansoni. This was established by the delayed appearance of positive antischistosomal antibodies (IFA 1:1280 and positive ELISA) and the eventual identification of S. mansoni ova in the stool, four weeks after initial negative tests. The clinical picture of fever, profound eosinophilia, nodular pulmonary lesions, and a clear history of freshwater exposure in Mozambique strongly supported this diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Acute schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "20---A-43-Year-Old-Male-Traveller-Returning-from-M_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 33-year-old male refugee from Afghanistan.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-month history of recurrent fever, night sweats, and progressive back pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient began feeling ill approximately 6 months prior to presentation while living in a refugee camp in Iran. His symptoms included recurrent fever, night sweats, and progressive back pain. He received treatment with tablets and intragluteal injections for several weeks, which led to a temporary subsidence of symptoms, but they recurred after the treatment ended. All medical documents were lost during his subsequent journey to Germany.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a refugee from Afghanistan, a country where spinal brucellosis is highly endemic. He lived in a refugee camp in Iran before traveling through Turkey and Eastern Europe to reach Germany four months prior to presentation.", "vitals": "Upon presentation, the patient was afebrile and his vital signs were normal.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed marked spinal tenderness in the lumbar region. The remainder of the examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory tests showed normal differential blood count, liver and renal function, and LDH. Inflammatory markers were slightly raised, with a C-reactive protein of 26 mg/L (<5 mg/L) and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 40 mm/hour (<20 mm/hour). Urinalysis was unremarkable. An MRI of the spine revealed signal enhancement of the L3 and L4 vertebral bodies, the corresponding vertebral disc, and the right psoas muscle, with disc space narrowing and irregularities of the adjacent vertebral endplates. A chest x-ray showed no abnormalities. Several blood cultures and sputum samples for acid-fast bacilli were negative. A CT-guided biopsy of the spinal lesion was performed, and culture of the tissue sample grew Brucella melitensis. An echocardiogram showed no signs of endocarditis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis for infectious spondylitis in this patient included tuberculosis, which is the main cause globally, and spinal brucellosis, which is highly prevalent in his country of origin, Afghanistan. Other less likely infectious causes considered were Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, and Gram-negative bacteria such as Salmonella species.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's condition was classified as complicated brucellosis with spondylitis and para-vertebral abscess formation. He was treated with a 16-week course of doxycycline 100 mg twice daily and rifampicin 900mg daily. Additionally, he received intramuscular streptomycin (1 g daily) for the first 3 weeks. His fever subsided within several days, and inflammatory markers returned to normal several weeks after initiating therapy. A follow-up MRI at the end of treatment showed full resolution of the paravertebral abscess. One year after completing treatment, the patient showed no signs of relapse.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is complicated brucellosis with spondylitis of L3/L4 and a paravertebral abscess. This diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the isolation of the causative agent, Brucella melitensis, from a culture of a CT-guided biopsy tissue sample from the affected vertebra. The clinical presentation of chronic fever and back pain, imaging findings of spondylodiscitis, and the patient's epidemiological background from a highly endemic region strongly supported this diagnosis over alternatives like spinal tuberculosis.", "disease_name_short": "Brucellosis", "source_filename": "81---A-33-Year-Old-Refugee-from-Afghanistan-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 28-year-old woman from Sierra Leone.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-day history of fever, weakness, sore throat and retrosternal chest pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 28-year-old woman presented to a rural hospital with a 6-day history of fever, weakness, sore throat, and retrosternal chest pain. For the past two days, she also had loose stools twice daily. Prior to admission, she was treated at a local health post with artemether-amodiaquine and amoxicillin but her condition worsened. In the hospital, she received IV ceftriaxone and completed her antimalarials but continued to deteriorate over two days. On the second day after admission, she developed conjunctivitis, cough, and breathlessness, becoming so unwell that she was unable to walk unaided.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient presented to a small rural hospital in eastern Sierra Leone, an area where Lassa fever is common, accounting for 16\u201320% of hospital admissions in some studies.", "vitals": "On physical examination, her axillary temperature was 38.2\u00b0C (100.8\u00b0F), blood pressure was 80/55 mmHg, and pulse rate was 100 bpm.", "physical_exam": "Her initial examination was unremarkable except for mild pharyngitis. Later in her course, she developed conjunctivitis. Chest auscultation revealed bilateral fine crepitations. She was noted to be very unwell and unable to walk unaided.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Her malaria slide was negative. A chest radiograph revealed diffuse bilateral infiltrates. Blood chemistry showed mild renal impairment and raised transaminases, with an aspartate transaminase (AST/GOT) of 514 U/L. The diagnosis of Lassa fever was confirmed with antigen testing.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for her severe febrile illness with pharyngitis, conjunctivitis, and chest involvement included several conditions. Adenovirus and measles were considered, but were less likely as adenovirus is typically mild and a rash would be expected with measles. Mycoplasma pneumoniae is rarely this severe. Other pneumonias do not normally cause conjunctivitis. Leptospirosis can cause pulmonary and renal issues but typically presents with conjunctival suffusions rather than conjunctivitis. Typhoid fever was considered, but improvement on ceftriaxone would be expected and conjunctivitis is not typical. Lassa fever was highly suspected as all her signs and symptoms were consistent with the disease and it is common in her region.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with artemether with amodiaquine and amoxicillin, followed by IV ceftriaxone in the hospital, but she continued to worsen. After the diagnosis of Lassa fever was confirmed with antigen testing, she was managed in a specialist isolation unit and received IV ribavirin. Unfortunately, the patient was already very unwell by this stage and died two days later. Public health measures were initiated, and all close contacts were advised to monitor their temperature for 21 days.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Lassa fever. This was confirmed by a positive antigen test. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical syndrome of a severe febrile illness with pharyngitis, conjunctivitis, pneumonitis, renal impairment, and markedly elevated liver transaminases that did not respond to broad-spectrum antibiotics or antimalarials. The patient's presence in eastern Sierra Leone, a known endemic area for Lassa fever, provided strong epidemiological evidence.", "disease_name_short": "Lassa fever", "source_filename": "74---A-28-Year-Old-Woman-from-Sierra-Leone-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 58-year-old Sri Lankan woman from a rural area with no other comorbidities mentioned.", "chief_complaint": "High-grade fever, body aches, cough, and shortness of breath for 10 days, with recent onset of hearing loss and confusion.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 10-day history of high-grade fever, chills, body aches, a non-productive cough, and progressive shortness of breath. Two days prior to admission, she developed tinnitus and hearing loss. One day before admission, she became increasingly confused.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in a rural area of Sri Lanka. The case discusses scrub typhus, an infection transmitted by the bite of an infected chigger (mite larva), which is common in various habitats including scrubs and forests in Asia.", "vitals": "On admission, her temperature was 39.3\u00b0C (102.7\u00b0F), heart rate was 120 bpm, and blood pressure was 90/60 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "On admission, the patient was confused with a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score of 13/15 and had severely impaired hearing (WHO grade 3). There were enlarged right axillary lymph nodes, hepatomegaly (5 cm below the costal margin), a palpable spleen, and scattered crackles at the lung bases. There was no rash or neck stiffness. On day 2, her GCS deteriorated to 8, and she developed coarse tremors and saccadic eye oscillations. A careful re-examination revealed a well-demarcated, crater-like eschar hidden in the right axilla.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial labs showed a normal white cell count (8.5 x 10^9/L), slight thrombocytopenia, and mildly elevated AST and ALT with normal bilirubin. Creatinine was elevated at 123.8 \u00b5mol/L. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis revealed a lymphocytic pleocytosis (35 cells/\u00b5L, 80% lymphocytes), with elevated protein (0.64 g/L) and slightly decreased glucose. A thick film for malaria was negative, and blood cultures showed no growth. Serologies for Leptospira and Salmonella were negative. A chest radiograph and brain CT scan were normal, while an EEG showed widespread slowing. After treatment was initiated, the patient\u2019s O. tsutsugamushi IFA-IgG titre was reported as high (>1:4096).", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses considered for this undifferentiated febrile illness in a patient from Asia included scrub typhus, other rickettsial infections, and leptospirosis. Malaria and bacterial sepsis with meningoencephalitis due to pathogens like Salmonella were also considered, but the thick film, blood cultures, and specific serologies were negative.", "management_and_clinical_course": "After an eschar was found on day 2, the patient was started on doxycycline. She improved significantly over the following 48 hours. Her hearing returned to normal, and there were no other neurological deficits upon discharge. She was discharged on day 6 after admission.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was scrub typhus caused by Orientia tsutsugamushi. This was based on the patient's clinical presentation of fever, deafness, and confusion, her residence in an endemic rural area, the key physical finding of an eschar in the right axilla with corresponding lymphadenopathy, and a rapid clinical response to doxycycline therapy. The diagnosis was supported by a high convalescent O. tsutsugamushi IFA-IgG titre of >1:4096.", "disease_name_short": "Scrub typhus", "source_filename": "70---A-58-Year-Old-Woman-from-Sri-Lanka-With-Fev_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 32-year-old man from Malawi with a past medical history notable for an episode of a blistering and painful skin lesion on the right side of his abdomen three years prior, which healed spontaneously and was suspected to be herpes zoster.", "chief_complaint": "Pain in the right upper abdomen and a feeling of faintness, especially when standing.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was well until one month prior to admission when he began to experience a feeling of fullness in his upper abdomen. In the week before admission, he developed pain in the right upper abdomen, which was particularly apparent when sleeping on his right side. He recently started to feel breathless on lying down and faint upon standing and walking.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi. The clinical context notes that in Africa, tuberculosis is the most common cause of pericardial effusion and there is a strong association between tuberculosis and HIV infection.", "vitals": "Pulse was regular at 130 bpm with a pronounced pulsus paradoxus measured at 15 mmHg. Blood pressure in the supine position was 90/60 mmHg. He was slightly breathless in the supine position.", "physical_exam": "The patient was thin. His jugular venous pulse appeared elevated, the apex beat was impalpable, and heart sounds were quiet without murmurs. Chest auscultation was normal. Examination of the abdomen revealed an enlarged, tender, palpable liver measured at 8 cm below the right costal junction. There was also evidence of mild peripheral oedema of the legs and sacral area.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Haemoglobin was 10.5 g/dL and the white blood cell count was 9.8 G/L. A chest radiograph showed an enlarged globular heart with clear lung fields. A therapeutic pericardial aspiration yielded 500 mL of bloodstained pericardial fluid. An HIV test was subsequently found to be positive.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary clinical syndrome was identified as pericardial tamponade from a large pericardial effusion. The differential diagnosis for the cause in this African setting included tuberculosis, other bacterial infections, malignancy, and HIV-related Kaposi\u2019s sarcoma. Given the history of a likely herpes zoster attack, HIV-associated tuberculosis was considered the most probable diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent therapeutic pericardial aspiration, with 500 mL of fluid removed, leading to an immediate increase in blood pressure to 120/80 mmHg. He was started on standard first-line antituberculous treatment (rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for 2 months, followed by rifampicin and isoniazid for 4 months) and prednisolone at 60 mg daily, tapered over ten weeks. Following a positive HIV test, antiretroviral therapy was started four weeks after commencing antituberculous treatment. He made a full and uneventful recovery.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with HIV-associated tuberculous pericarditis complicated by a large pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade. The diagnosis was based on the classic clinical signs of tamponade (tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus, hypotension), radiographic evidence of a globular heart, a history suggestive of immunosuppression (herpes zoster), a positive HIV test, and a complete response to pericardial drainage and empiric antituberculous therapy.", "disease_name_short": "HIV-associated tuberculous pericarditis", "source_filename": "66---A-32-Year-Old-Man-from-Malawi-With-Pain-in-the-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 67-year-old German woman with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Palpitations and transient subcutaneous swellings for the past year.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 67-year-old woman presented with palpitations and a one-year history of transient subcutaneous swellings. She reported no fever or constitutional symptoms. A 24-hour ECG performed prior to presentation showed paroxysmal supraventricular extrasystoles, and an echocardiogram revealed a minimal pericardial effusion.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient has lived as an expatriate in Cameroon for the past 4 years.", "vitals": "Vital signs were normal and the patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "On physical examination, there were no visible subcutaneous swellings. Heart sounds were clear and regular with no murmurs. No other pathological findings were noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial full blood count showed an absolute eosinophil count of 0.868 x10^9/L. A chest radiograph showed no abnormalities. Serology for filarial infections was positive. Day-blood samples were filtered and stained with Giemsa, which revealed sheathed microfilariae with a tapered tail characteristic for Loa loa. The microfilarial count was 1400/mL.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnoses for skin swellings and eosinophilia included non-specific urticaria, loiasis (Calabar swellings), cysticercosis, Strongyloides stercoralis infection (larva currens), and Mansonella perstans infection. Acute schistosomiasis was considered less likely due to the absence of fever. Gnathostomiasis and sparganosis were also considered but are geographically less common in Africa.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated first with albendazole, followed by ivermectin. The therapy was well tolerated. Following treatment, her absolute eosinophil count returned to normal and the microfilarial count declined. Her palpitations settled, and no sequelae were observed. A follow-up echocardiography 3 months later was normal, showing resolution of the pericardial effusion, and a repeat 24-hour ECG was also normal.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is loiasis caused by the filarial nematode Loa loa. This was established based on the patient's clinical presentation of transient subcutaneous swellings ('Calabar swellings'), marked eosinophilia, and a 4-year residency in Cameroon. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by positive filarial serology and the microscopic identification of sheathed L. loa microfilariae in a daytime blood sample. The resolution of her cardiac symptoms and eosinophilia after targeted anti-filarial therapy further supported the diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Loiasis", "source_filename": "28---A-67-Year-Old-Female-Expatriate-Living-in-Came_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-T.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 14-year-old boy from rural Malawi.", "chief_complaint": "The patient presented to a local hospital with an extensive necrotic wound on his right foot, three weeks after being bitten by a snake.", "history_of_present_illness": "Three weeks prior to presentation, the patient was bitten by a snake while playing. Shortly after the bite, he developed haemorrhagic bullae on his right leg, his gums started to bleed, and he vomited extensively. He was initially taken to a traditional healer, after which the bleeding and vomiting settled. However, he subsequently developed intense pain and swelling at the site of the bite, which progressed to the necrotic wound seen on admission.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from rural Malawi and was bitten on a path leading through rocky grassland to his family's maize field. The snake was described as brown with V-shaped black bands on its back, approximately one metre long, and it hissed loudly before biting. This description is consistent with an African puff-adder (Bitis arietans).", "vitals": "On admission, the patient appeared weak and was in respiratory distress. His temperature was 39.4\u00b0C, pulse was 126 bpm, blood pressure was 85/60 mmHg, and respiratory rate was 30 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "There was an extensive, foul-smelling necrosis affecting his right foot and ankle, with exposed tendons. The right lower leg was swollen, and he was unable to bend his right ankle. The inguinal lymph nodes were enlarged on the right side. His urine was clear, a rectal exam showed no signs of bleeding, there was no haemoptysis upon provoked coughing, and fundoscopy was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The 20-minute whole blood clotting test (WBCT20) was normal. Blood tests showed a haemoglobin of 9.5 mg/dL (reference 12\u201314) and potassium (K+) of 4.2 mmol/L. The WBC and platelet counts were not specified in the provided table.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary diagnostic challenge was identifying the snake species. The African puff-adder (Bitis arietans) was considered the most likely cause due to the snake's described appearance (one metre long, V-pattern on back), aggressive behavior (hissing), and the clinical syndrome of massive local swelling, haemorrhagic bullae, extensive necrosis, and systemic haemorrhage. The saw-scaled viper (Echis species) can produce similar symptoms. Bites by mambas were considered less likely as they are relatively uncommon.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Due to the three-week delay, antivenom was not administered. The patient was diagnosed as septic and managed with broad-spectrum antibiotics, fluid resuscitation, and 'ventilatory support'. He also received tetanus prophylaxis. The wound required surgical management with several debridements, followed by successful mesh-grafting. He was discharged several weeks later with some residual functional impairment of his right foot, requiring outpatient physiotherapy.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was a severe cytotoxic and haematotoxic snake envenomation with delayed presentation, complicated by extensive tissue necrosis and sepsis. The causative agent was most probably an African puff-adder (Bitis arietans), a conclusion based on the eyewitness description of the snake, the geographic location, and the clinical evolution characterized by initial systemic haemorrhage (bleeding gums) and severe, progressive local effects (haemorrhagic bullae, swelling, and deep necrosis).", "disease_name_short": "Snake envenomation", "source_filename": "26---A-14-Year-Old-Boy-from-Malawi-Who-Has-Bee_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 32-year-old Malawian woman who appeared wasted and slightly anaemic. An HIV test was positive, and her CD4 count was very low at 22/\u03bcL.", "chief_complaint": "A 3-week history of headache and blurred vision.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a headache of gradual onset over 3 weeks that did not respond to over-the-counter painkillers. She also reported blurred vision. There was no fever, history of convulsions, or head trauma. Prior to presentation, she received presumptive antimalarial treatment with artemether/lumefantrine and a 5-day course of amoxicillin from a local health centre, neither of which provided any benefit.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and resides in a region with a high prevalence of HIV.", "vitals": "All vital signs were normal and the patient was afebrile on presentation.", "physical_exam": "The patient was wasted and slightly anaemic with a GCS of 15/15 and no neck stiffness. Visual acuity was normal. Examination revealed an abduction deficit of the left eye on left lateral gaze, consistent with an abducens nerve palsy. The rest of the examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood tests showed a WBC of 3.7 x 10^9/L, normocytic anaemia with a haemoglobin of 10.2 g/dL, and thrombocytopenia. A malaria RDT was negative. HIV serology was positive with a CD4 count of 22/\u03bcL. A lumbar puncture revealed a markedly increased opening pressure of 50 cmH2O. The CSF was clear with 0 leukocytes/\u03bcL, elevated protein at 0.8 g/L, and low glucose at 1.97 mmol/L. India Ink stain of the CSF was negative, but the fungal culture was positive for Cryptococcus neoformans.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was chronic meningitis, with the most likely causes being cryptococcal meningitis and tuberculous meningitis, given the patient's presentation and epidemiology. Partially treated bacterial meningitis was considered but was less likely due to the gradual onset of symptoms. Malaria was considered unlikely due to the absence of fever, a negative RDT, and lack of cranial nerve palsy.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with cryptococcal meningitis and started on oral fluconazole 1200mg, as amphotericin B and flucytosine were unavailable. She was also started on co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. To manage the raised intracranial pressure and headache, she received repeated therapeutic lumbar punctures. Antiretroviral therapy was initiated 4 weeks after starting antifungal treatment. Despite this management, the patient returned to her village and died 6 weeks later of an unknown cause.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cryptococcal meningitis caused by Cryptococcus neoformans. This was confirmed by a positive fungal culture from the cerebrospinal fluid. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical presentation of chronic headache and a cranial nerve palsy in a severely immunocompromised patient (newly diagnosed with HIV and a CD4 count of 22/\u03bcL), along with a markedly elevated CSF opening pressure.", "disease_name_short": "Cryptococcal meningitis", "source_filename": "35---A-32-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-Heada_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 37-year-old woman with an unremarkable past medical history. She does not drink alcohol and an HIV test conducted 3 months prior was negative.", "chief_complaint": "Vomiting blood three times over the past 24 hours.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with three episodes of vomiting bright red blood over the past 24 hours. She reported no epigastric pain, no previous history of vomiting, no fever or abnormal bleeding, and her stool had been normal in colour. Before the onset of symptoms, she was well and had not taken any regular painkillers.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from the Lower Shire Valley in southern Malawi, where she lives and works on a large sugar plantation. The region is highly endemic for schistosomiasis, with most water bodies infested with Schistosoma mansoni and S. haematobium.", "vitals": "Blood pressure was 90/60 mmHg, pulse was 110 bpm, and respiratory rate was 28 breaths per minute. The patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "On examination, the patient was slim with slightly pale conjunctivae but was not jaundiced. The abdomen was non-tender and not distended, with the spleen palpable 10cm below the left costal margin and a slightly enlarged liver. There were no stigmata of chronic liver disease, no shifting dullness, and no peripheral oedema. Her lymph nodes were not enlarged, and the rest of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results on admission showed pancytopenia with a WBC of 2.8 x 10\u2079/L, haemoglobin of 8.3 g/dL (normocytic anaemia), and low platelets. Hepatitis B and C serologies were negative. A stool sample for S. mansoni ova was negative. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of oesophageal varices. An ultrasound of the liver revealed a pattern described as typical of pipestem fibrosis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential was upper gastrointestinal bleeding from oesophageal varices due to portal hypertension, as suggested by splenomegaly and pancytopenia. Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis was considered the most likely cause of portal hypertension due to the patient's residence in an endemic area. Other causes of bleeding like peptic ulcer disease were less likely as they would not explain the splenomegaly. Visceral leishmaniasis was also considered but was less probable due to the absence of fever and its rarity in Southern Africa.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient received intravenous fluids and was transferred for endoscopy, where band ligation of the oesophageal varices was performed. She was treated with a single dose of praziquantel 40mg/kg and was subsequently discharged home.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was hepatosplenic schistosomiasis causing portal hypertension and bleeding from oesophageal varices. This was based on the clinical presentation of haematemesis, marked splenomegaly, and pancytopenia in a patient from a highly endemic region. The diagnosis was supported by an ultrasound showing a pattern typical of pipestem fibrosis, while other causes like viral hepatitis were ruled out. Endoscopy confirmed oesophageal varices as the source of the bleeding.", "disease_name_short": "Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "57---A-37-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-H_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 26-year-old German student with no significant medical history reported.", "chief_complaint": "A localized swelling on her left leg that has developed over the past 3 weeks.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 26-year-old female student presented with a localized swelling on her left leg that had been developing slowly over the past 3 weeks. The lesion was itchy but not painful. She reported no history of fever or arthropod bites and was otherwise feeling fine.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had just returned from a 6-week backpacking trip to Ghana. Furuncular myiasis in sub-Saharan Africa is commonly caused by the Tumbu fly, Cordylobia anthropophaga, whose larvae infect humans who come into contact with eggs laid on sandy ground or on damp laundry.", "vitals": "The patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "On examination, there was a localized swelling on the left leg, approximately 1.5 cm in diameter, with slightly hyperaemic surrounding skin. The boil was covered by a blackish scab. There was no lymphadenopathy, and the rest of the physical examination was normal. After the scab was removed, a whitish, pulsating matter oozing transparent fluid was observed underneath, which was identified as the respiratory spiracles of a larva.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "No laboratory or imaging studies were performed. The diagnosis was made based on clinical findings, specifically the visual identification of a pulsating larva within the lesion after removing the scab.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnoses considered included rickettsial disease or cutaneous anthrax, which were deemed unlikely due to the absence of systemic symptoms. A spider bite was also considered but was less likely given the slow growth of the lesion over several weeks. Localized bacterial skin infections were another possibility. However, myiasis was considered the most likely diagnosis due to the slow growth, localized itch, and lack of systemic symptoms or lymphadenopathy.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The scab was removed and the lesion was covered with white petroleum jelly and bandaged to deprive the larva of oxygen. The patient returned two days later, and the maggot of a Tumbu fly was easily removed with a pair of forceps. The remaining lesion appeared clean and required no further treatment.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cutaneous furuncular myiasis caused by the larva of the Tumbu fly (Cordylobia anthropophaga). This was confirmed by the patient's travel history to Ghana, the characteristic clinical presentation of a slow-growing, itchy boil without systemic symptoms, the direct visualization of the pulsating larva after removing the scab, and the successful removal of the maggot following occlusive therapy.", "disease_name_short": "Cutaneous myiasis", "source_filename": "8---A-26-Year-Old-Female-Traveller-Returning-from_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 7-year-old girl from a tropical region of a West African country. There is no history of relevant trauma or other systemic symptoms.", "chief_complaint": "Progressive skin ulcers on the right hand and wrist with associated hand contracture over a period of three months.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began three months prior after an insect bite, starting as an itchy papule on the back of her right hand that gradually enlarged. Initial treatments from a traditional healer with herbal remedies were ineffective. The lesion ulcerated a few weeks later, and she received diclofenac and dexamethasone at a local health post. A second lesion then appeared, and a course of oxacillin was initiated with no effect. Concurrently, she became progressively unable to use her right hand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from a district in the tropical region of a West African country. The history mentions an insect bite, but this is considered an incidental finding as the mode of transmission for the causative organism is unknown.", "vitals": "Pulse was 108 bpm, blood pressure was 100/70 mmHg, and temperature was 37.9\u00b0C (100.2\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient was an anxious girl in good general condition, holding her right wrist in a 45\u00b0 flexion and 20\u00b0 abduction position. Two skin ulcers were present: a 3 x 3 cm ulcer on the back of her right hand filled with necrotic tissue, and a 0.5 x 1 cm ulcer on the medial side of her right wrist. The ulcers were surrounded by an ill-defined induration (12 x 8 cm) with hypo- and hyperpigmentation, lichenification, desquamation, and oedema extending from the lower arm to the fingers. A 0.5 x 0.5 cm nodule was also noted above the medial right elbow.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory confirmation was achieved via PCR from a wound swab, which confirmed the presence of Mycobacterium ulcerans.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary suspected diagnosis was Buruli ulcer, based on the geographical region, patient's age, and clinical picture of progressive cutaneous ulcers on an extremity without major trauma. Other differential diagnoses for ulcers in tropical countries include various infectious (bacterial, viral, fungal, parasitic) and non-infectious (trauma, envenoming, autoimmune, haematological, neoplastic) causes.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent 8 weeks of specific antimycobacterial therapy and wound care. During this period, the ulcer on the back of her hand healed almost completely, but the second, smaller ulcer paradoxically increased in size to 8 x 10 cm. Due to this, she required surgical debridement and skin grafting. The contracture of her wrist was corrected with physiotherapy, and she made an uneventful recovery.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Buruli ulcer disease caused by Mycobacterium ulcerans. The diagnosis was strongly suspected based on the clinical presentation of progressive, necrotizing skin ulcers with surrounding induration and oedema, leading to a joint contracture in a child from an endemic region in West Africa. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a positive PCR test for M. ulcerans from a wound swab.", "disease_name_short": "Buruli ulcer", "source_filename": "41---A-7-Year-Old-Girl-from-West-Africa-With-Two-Ski_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old man, diagnosed with HIV 6 years ago, who is not yet on antiretroviral therapy (ART) or co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. His CD4 count was 280/\u03bcL one month prior to presentation, and on admission was 150/\u03bcL with an HIV viral load of 1,500,000 copies/mL. He denies any recreational drug use and has never smoked tobacco. He has no other significant past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-week history of increasing shortness of breath on exertion, dry cough, and drenching night sweats.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 6-week history of progressively worsening shortness of breath on exertion, a dry cough, and drenching night sweats. He also reported general fatigue and a weight loss of about 10kg over the preceding 5 months.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a business traveller who travels extensively. In the past 6 months, he had work trips to Europe, China, Korea, Japan, Singapore, and the United States. He lives with his male partner.", "vitals": "On presentation, his temperature was 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F), blood pressure was 120/70mmHg, pulse was 100 bpm, and respiratory rate was 26 breaths per minute. His oxygen saturation was 87% on ambient air, which improved to 97% on 15L of O2.", "physical_exam": "The patient was alert and able to complete full sentences, but was short of breath at rest. Auscultation of the chest was clear. The rest of the physical examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a normal full blood count and normal renal and liver function tests. C-reactive protein was elevated at 6.7 mg/dL. Arterial blood gases on ambient air showed a pH of 7.44 and a PaO2 of 7.6 kPa, consistent with type 1 respiratory failure. His CD4 count was 150/\u03bcL. A chest radiograph showed bilateral perihilar infiltrates. A CT-pulmonary angiogram ruled out pulmonary embolism but revealed extensive \u201cground-glass\u201d infiltrates in both lungs, especially in the lower lobes. Serum 1,3 \u03b2-D-glucan was markedly elevated at >500 pg/mL. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid analysis confirmed the diagnosis, with Grocott staining revealing Pneumocystis jirovecii cystic forms and a strongly positive PCR for P. jirovecii DNA.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP), given the patient's presentation as an ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive individual with a low CD4 count, progressive shortness of breath, and typical chest radiograph findings. Other considerations included tuberculosis, atypical bacterial pneumonia, cryptococcosis, or histoplasmosis. A CT pulmonary angiogram was performed to rule out pulmonary embolism.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was managed with high-flow oxygen, high-dose co-trimoxazole, and prednisolone. His tachycardia and hypoxia improved with treatment. Ten days into treatment, he commenced antiretroviral therapy with tenofovir, emtricitabine, and efavirenz. Two weeks into PCP treatment, he developed a pruritic maculopapular rash, leading to a change from co-trimoxazole to clindamycin and primaquine for the final week of his 21-day course, and his efavirenz was replaced. After completing treatment, he was started on dapsone and pyrimethamine for secondary prophylaxis.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is severe Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) caused by Pneumocystis jirovecii. This was established in an ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive patient with a low CD4 count (150/\u03bcL) presenting with classic subacute respiratory failure and hypoxia. The diagnosis was supported by imaging showing bilateral ground-glass infiltrates and a highly elevated serum \u03b2-glucan level (>500 pg/mL). It was definitively confirmed by bronchoalveolar lavage fluid analysis, which identified P. jirovecii cystic forms via Grocott staining and was strongly positive by PCR.", "disease_name_short": "Pneumocystis pneumonia", "source_filename": "23---A-31-Year-Old-HIV-Positive-Business-Traveller-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 40-year-old Thai woman, a housewife who has lived in Germany for the past 13 years, presented with an unremarkable past medical history. She had a mild microcytic anaemia noted during her admission.", "chief_complaint": "Severe headache for the past 8 days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient reported a severe headache of gradual onset that had been present for 8 days and was unresponsive to painkillers. She denied any fever, chills, visual problems, limb weakness, or memory problems. Three days prior to this presentation, she had visited a municipal hospital where a CT scan of her brain and a blood count were reported as normal.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "Ten days prior to presentation, the patient had returned from a 4-week trip to north-east Thailand, where she visited friends and relatives. During her visit, she consumed traditional regional dishes, including freshwater fish, seafood, snails, and frogs. Her brother-in-law, with whom she had shared several meals, had also fallen ill with a similar severe headache.", "vitals": "On examination, the patient was afebrile. The initial cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure was elevated at 34 cmH2O (reference range 12\u201320).", "physical_exam": "On examination, the patient was afebrile with a supple neck and a negative Las\u00e8gue\u2019s sign. Her pupils were equal, round, and reactive to light and accommodation, and there were no cranial nerve palsies. The remainder of the neurological examination, including higher cortical functions, was unremarkable. There was no rash, oral thrush, or subcutaneous swellings. Cardiopulmonary examination was normal, and the liver and spleen were not enlarged. There was no lymphadenopathy.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "An initial CT scan of the brain was normal. Laboratory results revealed a white blood cell count of 12.3 x 10^9/L with marked eosinophilia (2.34 x 10^9/L) and elevated IgE levels. A lumbar puncture was performed, showing 1877 leukocytes/\u03bcL in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), with a differential of 42% eosinophils, 39% lymphocytes, and 18% monocytes. CSF protein was elevated at 1.59 g/L, and glucose was low at 1.85 mmol/L. No larvae were seen on microscopic examination of the CSF. Ziehl\u2013Neelsen stain and cryptococcal antigen were negative. Serologies for Taenia solium, Fasciola hepatica, and Paragonimus were negative. Toxocara canis serology was weakly positive without a change in titre. After discharge, a Western Blot result came back positive for specific antibodies against the 31kD Angiostrongylus cantonensis antigen.", "differential_diagnosis": "The presumptive diagnosis was a food-borne parasitic infection of the meninges, specifically eosinophilic meningitis. The main differential diagnoses included fungal meningitis (e.g., Coccidioides immitis), lymphoma, eosinophilic leukaemia, sarcoid, and idiopathic hypereosinophilic syndrome. Other parasitic causes such as neurocysticercosis, schistosomiasis, paragonimiasis, fascioliasis, and toxocariasis were also considered and tested for.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was managed symptomatically. She underwent serial lumbar punctures, which relieved her headache by reducing the elevated intracranial pressure. No specific anthelmintic treatment was prescribed. She received iron tablets for a mild microcytic anaemia. She was discharged significantly improved after a one-week hospital stay. At a 4-week follow-up, she had fully recovered, and her peripheral eosinophil count had returned to normal.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was eosinophilic meningitis caused by the nematode Angiostrongylus cantonensis (rat lungworm). This was established based on the clinical presentation of severe headache, a relevant exposure history including travel to an endemic area (north-east Thailand) and consumption of raw or undercooked intermediate hosts (snails, frogs). The diagnosis was confirmed by laboratory findings of significant peripheral blood eosinophilia, eosinophilic pleocytosis in the cerebrospinal fluid, and the detection of specific antibodies against the 31kD Angiostrongylus cantonensis antigen via Western blot.", "disease_name_short": "Angiostrongyliasis", "source_filename": "64---A-40-Year-Old-Woman-from-Thailand-and-Her-Bro_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 64-year-old Japanese man with a medical history of hypertension and a prior right cerebellar infarction. He also had a history of recent simple partial seizures that were well controlled with an antiepileptic agent.", "chief_complaint": "Generalized tonic-clonic seizures followed by weakness of the right lower extremity.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was admitted to a hospital after experiencing generalized tonic-clonic seizures which were followed by weakness in his right lower extremity. His pre-existing simple partial seizures had previously been well controlled by medication.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a resident of Japan. The probable infection route was through the consumption of raw chicken meat, which he reported eating approximately once a month before the onset of the generalized seizure.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "Upon admission, the patient's Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) was 15/15. The neurological examination did not reveal any abnormality.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed no abnormalities, including no eosinophilia or leukocytosis. A head CT revealed a small high-density nodule with an enhanced perifocal low-density area in the left occipital lobe. An MRI of the brain showed a ring-enhancing, tunnel-shaped lesion in the left occipital lobe, a finding described as the \"tunnel sign\". Angiography was normal aside from a right vertebral artery occlusion consistent with his past stroke. Post-operatively, enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) on serum and cerebrospinal fluid revealed strong positivity for Spirometra mansoni.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnoses for a space-occupying lesion in the brain include brain tumours or metastases, neurocysticercosis, cerebral schistosomiasis, and paragonimiasis. In immunocompromised patients, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcomas, and tuberculomas would also be considered.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent an open surgery with craniotomy, during which a live, white, tape-like, worm-like structure was identified and completely removed from the targeted lesion in the brain. After the surgical removal of the worm, the patient was well and had no further seizures at his most recent examination.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is cerebral sparganosis caused by a larva of the tapeworm *Spirometra mansoni*. This diagnosis was established by the surgical removal of a live sparganum larva from a ring-enhancing, tunnel-shaped lesion in the left occipital lobe, which was visualized on MRI. The diagnosis was further confirmed by strongly positive ELISA results for *Spirometra mansoni* in both serum and cerebrospinal fluid.", "disease_name_short": "Cerebral sparganosis", "source_filename": "84---A-64-Year-Old-Japanese-Man-With-Generalize_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 71-year-old Japanese man, born and currently living in the Kyushu district of southern Japan. He is clinically well with no reported complaints.", "chief_complaint": "Referral for further work-up of an asymptomatic nodular lesion in the right lung.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was found to have a lung lesion two years prior during a routine health check. Initially, the lesion was of a linear shape and was followed regularly. On a recent follow-up visit, chest radiography and CT scan revealed that the linear shadow had transformed into a nodular lesion approximately 2 cm in diameter. The patient remained asymptomatic throughout this period.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a lifelong resident of the Kyushu district in southern Japan, a region where paragonimiasis is endemic. He has no history of travelling overseas.", "vitals": "The vital signs were normal.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination was normal. The chest was clear on auscultation.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A full blood count revealed a white blood cell count of 6.5 x 10^9/L with an eosinophil percentage of 8.3% (total eosinophil count 0.54 x 10^9/L), indicating mild eosinophilia. Total IgE was elevated at 185.7 U/mL. Liver function, renal function, electrolytes, LDH, and CRP were all normal. Serologies for Cryptococcus Ag, Aspergillus Ag, and \u03b2-D-glucan were negative, as was a QuantiFERON test for tuberculosis. A chest radiograph and CT scan identified a nodular lesion of about 2 cm in the right lung. An FDG-PET-CT showed increased FDG uptake in this lesion. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) cytology was negative for malignant cells and acid-fast bacilli, and tumor markers were negative. The diagnosis was confirmed with a multiple-dot ELISA which was strongly positive for P. westermani.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses for a lung nodule with increased FDG uptake were pulmonary tuberculosis and lung cancer. Systemic endemic mycoses were considered but deemed unlikely as the patient had no travel history to endemic areas outside of East Asia. Given the patient's origin in southern Japan, eosinophilia, and elevated IgE, pulmonary helminth infection, specifically lung fluke infection (paragonimiasis), was strongly suspected.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a 3-day course of oral praziquantel at a dose of 25 mg/kg three times daily. Following treatment, the lung lesion gradually faded and eventually disappeared. His serum antibody titre for P. westermani also declined, indicating a successful response to therapy.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is pulmonary paragonimiasis caused by the lung fluke Paragonimus westermani. This was established based on the patient's epidemiological risk as a resident of an endemic area in Japan, the clinical presentation of an asymptomatic lung nodule, and laboratory findings of peripheral eosinophilia and elevated IgE. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a strongly positive multiple-dot ELISA for P. westermani and further supported by the complete resolution of the lung lesion and decline in antibody titers following treatment with praziquantel.", "disease_name_short": "Paragonimiasis", "source_filename": "71---A-71-Year-Old-Man-from-Japan-With-Eosinophili_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
