{"patient_information": "A 6-year-old boy from Malawi who is known to be HIV-positive but is not yet on antiretroviral therapy (ART).", "chief_complaint": "High fever, dyspnoea, and a dry cough for 3 days, with subsequent impaired consciousness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began 3 days prior to review with high fever, dyspnoea, and a dry cough. He was admitted the night before and presented with impaired consciousness. A fine maculopapular rash, not previously described, was noted on examination behind his ears and on his forehead. Within the next 3 days, the rash spread to cover his trunk, and skin scaling started over his chest.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a boy from Malawi, being treated in a Malawian Central Hospital. No other specific exposures are detailed.", "vitals": "On examination, the patient had a temperature of 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F) and laboured breathing.", "physical_exam": "Examination revealed a sick child with impaired consciousness, evidenced by a Blantyre coma score of 3/5. He presented with coryza, bilateral conjunctivitis, and bilateral axillary lymphadenopathy. A fine maculopapular rash was present behind both ears and on the forehead.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Malaria smears were performed on admission and were found to be negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The initial differential diagnosis was broad and included malaria, typhoid fever, atypical pneumonia, and various viral infections. However, the presence of the classic triad of cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, combined with a rash starting behind the ears, made measles the primary suspicion. Other viral causes like rubella and enterovirus, and bacterial infections such as scarlet fever and meningococcal disease, were also considered.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Presumptive antimalarial treatment was started on admission but was stopped after malaria smears were confirmed to be negative. The patient required IV fluids and was given vitamin A for corneal protection. He was isolated due to the contagious nature of his illness. He developed severe pneumonia and received antibiotic treatment. After recovery, antiretroviral therapy was initiated in the out-patient clinic.", "final_diagnosis": "The diagnosis is measles in an immunocompromised child. This is based on the classic clinical presentation featuring the triad of cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, followed by a characteristic maculopapular rash starting behind the ears and spreading caudally with subsequent desquamation. The diagnosis is further supported by the development of severe complications, including pneumonia and likely encephalitis (given the impaired consciousness), which are common in HIV-positive individuals. Malaria was ruled out by negative smears.", "disease_name_short": "Measles", "source_filename": "31---A-6-Year-Old-Boy-from-Malawi-With-Fever--Cou_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
