{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old Guatemalan man with no past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Several minutes of weakness and numbness of the leg.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with weakness and numbness of the leg lasting several minutes, which resolved just before his arrival at the hospital. Two weeks prior, he also experienced two episodes of facial twitching that subsided without intervention after 10 seconds each.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient was born and lived in a rural area of Guatemala before immigrating to New York 5 years ago. In Guatemala, his family owned uncorralled pigs and his house lacked sanitation services.", "vitals": "His vitals were normal.", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was unremarkable. Cranial nerves were intact. Motor and sensory examinations, deep tendon reflexes, and gait were all normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial cell blood count and serum chemistry were within normal limits. A serum immunoblot for cysticercosis was positive, as was the serum cysticercosis antigen at the beginning of therapy. A CT of the head demonstrated multiple calcifications in the brain parenchyma. An MRI of the head revealed complex cystic structures in the subarachnoid space, dilation of the suprasellar and Sylvian cisterns, peripheral enhancement after gadolinium, and an old lacunar infarct. A CT angiography showed patent cerebral arteries. An MRI of the spine showed no evidence of spinal involvement.", "differential_diagnosis": "The patient's presentation was consistent with a transitory ischemic attack. However, the combination of imaging findings, including parenchymal calcified lesions and complex cystic structures, along with the patient's epidemiological risk factors, made neurocysticercosis the most likely diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a high dose of prednisone followed by a taper, and dual antiparasitic therapy with albendazole and praziquantel. After 9 months of treatment, his serum antigen was negative and his lesions had resolved on imaging. He has been followed closely with no recurrence.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is subarachnoid neurocysticercosis, caused by the larval form of Taenia solium. This was determined based on the combination of epidemiological risk factors (origin from rural Guatemala with exposure to pigs), characteristic neuroimaging findings of parenchymal calcifications and complex subarachnoid cysts, and was confirmed by a positive serum immunoblot for cysticercosis.", "disease_name_short": "Subarachnoid neurocysticercosis", "source_filename": "82---A-31-Year-Old-Man-from-Guatemala-With-Acute-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 32-year-old, previously fit woman of African descent born in Europe.", "chief_complaint": "Fever, jaundice, and confusion.", "history_of_present_illness": "According to her husband, 18 days after returning from a trip to Nigeria, the patient developed a fever. Three days later, she became very unwell with abnormal behaviour.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient travelled to rural Jos in Nigeria to visit relatives. She is of African descent but was born in Europe and would be unlikely to have any immunity to malaria. People returning from visiting friends and relatives are at high risk of not taking antimalarial prophylaxis.", "vitals": "On examination, her temperature was 37.5\u00b0C (99.5\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient was mildly jaundiced and confused, with a Glasgow Coma Scale of 14/15. She had no rash or palpable lymph nodes, and her throat and sclerae were not injected. There were some fine crepitations at the lung bases.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A malaria blood film revealed a Plasmodium falciparum parasite count of 18%. Her plasma creatinine was 430 \u03bcmol/L, indicating acute kidney injury. Later in the clinical course, chest radiography findings were suggestive of ARDS.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was severe falciparum malaria, the most common cause of fever with jaundice or confusion in West Africa. Other possibilities considered included acute viral hepatitis, typhoid fever, leptospirosis, and ascending cholangitis. Viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF) was raised as a concern by the laboratory, but was considered less likely due to the long incubation period (18 days) and the absence of injected sclerae or a rash.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with intravenous artesunate. Despite the parasite count dropping to 5%, she lapsed into a deep coma. Her renal function deteriorated, and she required haemofiltration after her urine output failed to respond to fluid challenges. After the parasites cleared on day 5, her consciousness began to improve, but she developed laboured breathing and ARDS, requiring prolonged respiratory support. She eventually made a full recovery.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was severe falciparum malaria. This was confirmed by a malaria blood film showing a high parasite count (18%) of Plasmodium falciparum in a non-immune patient with a relevant travel history to rural Nigeria. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical presentation of fever, confusion, and jaundice, and the development of severe complications including acute kidney injury requiring haemofiltration, cerebral malaria (coma), and ARDS.", "disease_name_short": "Severe falciparum malaria", "source_filename": "22---32-Year-Old-Woman-from-Nigeria-With-Jaund_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 56-year-old Peruvian male with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 2-week history of fever, jaundice, and confusion.", "history_of_present_illness": "Daily fever began 3 months after a trip to a rural area. In the second week of his illness, the patient developed dark urine and jaundice. A few days prior to admission, his wife noted he had become confused and somnolent.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient spent 3 weeks on vacation in a rural area in the highlands of northern Peru at an altitude of 2400m. While there, he and his wife were bitten at night by tiny mosquitoes and used no personal protection. He had no other animal contact. The illness began 3 months after leaving this area.", "vitals": "On admission, his blood pressure was 90/60mmHg, pulse was 110 bpm and regular, temperature was 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F), and respiratory rate was 22 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was confused and disorientated (GCS 14/15) without focal neurological findings or meningeal signs. His skin and conjunctivae were markedly pale with scleral jaundice. Cardiovascular and pulmonary exams were normal. The liver was slightly enlarged, but there was no splenomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a WBC of 14.9 x 10^9/L with 13.1 x 10^9/L neutrophils and 0.9 x 10^9/L lymphocytes. Creatinine, electrolytes, and alkaline phosphatase were normal. Coomb\u2019s test was negative and CSF results were normal. A CT scan of the brain was normal, and an abdominal ultrasound revealed hepatomegaly without focal lesions. Blood films were negative for malaria. A thin film revealed massive red blood cell infestation by pleomorphic cocco-bacillary structures compatible with B. bacilliformis. Blood cultures, including on special media for Bartonella, were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses were malaria and bartonellosis. Plasmodium vivax was considered due to travel to the Peruvian highlands, but the patient's severe symptoms were atypical. Bartonellosis was highly suspected given the travel history to an endemic area for sandflies. Less common considerations included rickettsial diseases, leptospirosis, typhoid fever, brucellosis, and viral diseases such as yellow fever, though these were less likely as they do not typically cause significant hemolysis and some are not endemic to the region.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's condition worsened, progressing to shock requiring transfer to the ICU and treatment with vasopressors. He was started on intravenous ciprofloxacin and ceftriaxone for 10 days. His altered mental status resolved after 3 days of treatment, and his fever subsided after 5 days. By day 4, the bacteria were no longer visible on his blood smears. No further complications were observed during a 3-month follow-up period.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Bartonellosis, specifically the acute phase known as Oroya fever, caused by Bartonella bacilliformis. This conclusion is based on the clinical triad of prolonged fever, severe hemolytic anemia, and altered mental status following exposure in an endemic highland region of Peru. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the microscopic identification of massive infestation of red blood cells by cocco-bacillary structures consistent with B. bacilliformis on a thin blood smear, while tests for malaria were negative. The patient's rapid response to treatment with ciprofloxacin and ceftriaxone further supports the diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Bartonellosis (Oroya fever)", "source_filename": "52---A-56-Year-Old-Man-from-Peru-With-Prolonged-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
