{"patient_information": "A 55-year-old woman with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension for which she has been on treatment for 4 years.", "chief_complaint": "A 1-day history of fever, headache and vomiting, followed by confusion on the second day of illness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented to a rural hospital with a one-day history of fever, headache, and vomiting. A blood film for malaria was negative, and she was treated with fluids and paracetamol. On the second day of her illness, she developed confusion and was referred to a hospital in Ho Chi Minh City for further investigation.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from a rural area in Vietnam where she raises pigs on her smallholding and works as a butcher in a local market, selling pork and chicken. Both P. falciparum and P. vivax malaria are endemic in rural Vietnam.", "vitals": "On admission, she had a fever of 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F), tachycardia at 124 bpm, hypotension at 85/60 mmHg, tachypnoea with 32 breaths per minute, and hypoxia with an O2 saturation of 86% on ambient air.", "physical_exam": "The patient was confused with a Glasgow Coma Score of 12/15. She exhibited neck stiffness and photophobia with no focal neurological signs. Examination revealed a diffuse petechial rash over her trunk and marked purpuric, non-blanching lesions on her left leg. The rest of her examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood film for malaria was negative. Admission labs showed a WBC of 10.25 x 10^9/L with 86.5% neutrophils, hemoglobin of 11.8 g/dL, random blood glucose of 15.4 mmol/L, and a high lactate of 11.2 mmol/L. A non-contrast CT scan of the brain and a chest x-ray were unremarkable. Lumbar puncture revealed turbid cerebrospinal fluid with 2870 cells/\u03bcL (92% neutrophils), protein of 541.16 mg/dL, and a CSF glucose of 2.44 mmol/l with a concurrent blood glucose of 19 mmol/L (CSF/serum ratio 0.12). CSF Gram stain showed a Gram-positive coccus in short chains. Repeat malaria film and rapid diagnostic tests were negative. Five days later, both blood and CSF cultures grew Streptococcus suis, which was sensitive to penicillin and ceftriaxone.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included severe malaria, which must be excluded in rural Vietnam. Enteric fever was considered but the rapid progression was uncharacteristic. Dengue fever was also a possibility, but the rapid deterioration with signs of meningitis and shock was not typical. Melioidosis was considered due to her diabetes, but it is comparatively rare in Vietnam. The most likely diagnosis was acute bacterial meningitis complicated by septic shock, with Streptococcus suis being the most common identified cause in adults in Vietnam, often presenting with meningitis, shock, and a purpuric rash.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated empirically with intravenous ceftriaxone and vancomycin, along with supportive care including intravenous fluids. Dexamethasone was administered for the first four days. Following culture results confirming S. suis, she completed a 12-day course of antibiotic therapy. She responded to treatment but developed residual bilateral hearing impairment.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Streptococcus suis meningitis complicated by septic shock. The diagnosis was confirmed by the growth of Streptococcus suis from both blood and cerebrospinal fluid cultures. The clinical presentation of acute meningitis, shock, a characteristic purpuric rash, and CSF findings of high neutrophilic pleocytosis and a very low CSF/serum glucose ratio were all consistent with this diagnosis. Her occupation as a pig farmer and butcher provided a clear epidemiological link for this zoonotic infection.", "disease_name_short": "Streptococcus suis meningitis", "source_filename": "85---A-55-Year-Old-Female-Pig-Farmer-from-Vietnam-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 21-year-old male migrant from rural Mali who is illiterate. His past medical history is notable for a prior work-up for splenomegaly and bicytopenia at a haematology department, where the underlying condition could not be established. HIV serology was negative; EBV and CMV serologies indicated previous infection.", "chief_complaint": "Referral for massive splenomegaly, bicytopenia, intermittent fever, and a feeling of abdominal heaviness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient reports intermittent fever and a sensation of \u201cheaviness\u201d on the left side of his abdomen for some time, though the exact duration is difficult to specify. He denies night sweats and his weight has been stable. Despite the splenomegaly, he is clinically quite well.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a migrant from rural Mali, an area with stable malaria transmission. He arrived in Germany one year prior to presentation. His journey involved crossing Niger and spending a few weeks at a detention centre in Libya before traveling through Italy to Germany. He comes from a poor family.", "vitals": "At presentation, he was afebrile with a temperature of 36.1\u00b0C (96.98\u00b0F). Blood pressure was 120/80mmHg and pulse was 64 bpm.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared clinically well. The spleen was massively enlarged, palpable around 15 cm below the left costal margin. There was no clinical anaemia or lymphadenopathy. Heart sounds were clear with no murmurs. An abdominal ultrasound confirmed a spleen of 21.8 cm in longitudinal diameter with homogenous parenchyma. The liver was normal in size and appearance, and flow in the portal and splenic veins was normal. There was no lymphadenopathy or free fluid.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a mild bicytopenia. White blood cell count was 3.3 x 10\u2079/L and haemoglobin was 13.8 g/dL. Platelet count was not specified but noted as low in the referral. Inflammatory markers were normal, with CRP at 0.5 and ESR at 9/21 mm/h. LDH was less than 220 U/L. Immunoglobulin M (IgM) was markedly elevated at 8.59 g/l. Thick and thin blood films for Plasmodium spp. were negative. However, P. falciparum serology by IFAT was positive at a titer of 1:256. Subsequently, a PCR for P. falciparum came back positive. Serologies for schistosomiasis and leishmaniasis were negative, and no ova of S. mansoni were found in the stool. Blood cultures were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included lymphoma or leukaemia, which was considered unlikely due to the normal differential count and non-elevated LDH, CRP, and ESR. Visceral leishmaniasis was less likely as the patient did not appear severely ill and lacked pancytopenia. Schistosomiasis was ruled out by the normal liver ultrasound, which showed no signs of portal hypertension or fibrosis. Bacterial endocarditis and brucellosis were also considered less probable given the massive, rather than moderate, splenomegaly and absence of cardiac murmurs. The combination of massive splenomegaly, very high IgM, positive malaria serology, and origin from an endemic area made hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly (HMS) the most likely diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a single course of dihydroartemisinin/piperaquine. Following treatment, his reported episodes of fever subsided. A follow-up malaria PCR at 2 weeks was still positive, but has remained negative since. He has been monitored for 2.5 years. His IgM levels took 2 years to return to normal. The spleen size slowly decreased, measuring 14 cm in diameter at his last visit. His overall condition improved, and he was able to learn to read, write, and attend school in Germany.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Hyperreactive Malarial Splenomegaly (HMS). This was established based on the patient's presentation with massive splenomegaly, a history of intermittent fevers, and his origin from rural Mali, a malaria-endemic region. The diagnosis was confirmed by laboratory findings of a markedly elevated polyclonal IgM, a positive P. falciparum IFAT serology (1:256), and a positive P. falciparum PCR, despite negative malaria blood films. The significant clinical improvement and slow resolution of splenomegaly and IgM levels following a single course of antimalarial therapy further solidified the diagnosis, while other causes like hematologic malignancy and schistosomiasis were ruled out.", "disease_name_short": "Hyperreactive Malarial Splenomegaly", "source_filename": "73---A-21-Year-Old-Male-Migrant-from-Rural-Mali-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 24-year-old man of Turkish origin, residing in Germany. He had a known history of cystic echinococcosis (CE), diagnosed three years prior with two calcified liver cysts confirmed by imaging and positive serology for Echinococcus granulosus.", "chief_complaint": "Right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and vomiting.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with acute right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and vomiting. Three years earlier, he was diagnosed with cystic echinococcosis after presenting with similar pain. At that time, an ultrasound and CT scan revealed two calcified cystic lesions in his liver. Serology was positive for Echinococcus granulosus. Since the cyst walls were calcified and he was asymptomatic, anthelmintic treatment was not offered and he refused surgical treatment. The cysts were monitored by ultrasound.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a resident of Germany but visits his family in rural eastern Turkey (Anatolia), a hyperendemic region for cystic echinococcosis, every year for about 6 weeks.", "vitals": "Blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg, pulse was 64 bpm, and temperature was 36\u00b0C (96.8\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient was in fair general condition with scleral jaundice. There was right upper quadrant and epigastric tenderness on palpation, but no guarding.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The full blood count was normal. An endoscopic retrograde cholangiography (ERC) revealed biliary obstruction caused by a cystobiliary fistula, and cyst content was found in the common bile duct. Imaging from three years prior had identified two liver cysts: one 7 x 6 cm in the right lobe (WHO classification CE3b) and another 5 x 4 cm in the left lobe (WHO classification CE1), both with calcified walls.", "differential_diagnosis": "The main differential diagnoses for cystic liver lesions in this patient included a congenital simple hepatic cyst and pseudocysts associated with alveolar echinococcosis (E. multilocularis). Other considerations included infectious causes like bacterial or amoebic abscesses or tuberculoma, as well as benign and malignant liver tumours or metastases.", "management_and_clinical_course": "An endoscopic retrograde cholangiography (ERC) was performed, during which cyst content was removed from the common bile duct, restoring bile flow. The patient's resulting cholangitis was treated with ciprofloxacin and metronidazole. Once the inflammation settled, he was referred for surgery, and a partial cystectomy was performed on both cysts. He has been followed up for 9 years after surgery with no recurrence and is considered cured.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was complicated cystic echinococcosis due to the larval form of the tapeworm Echinococcus granulosus. The patient's known CE, with risk from travel to rural Turkey, presented as a cystobiliary fistula causing biliary obstruction and cholangitis. The diagnosis was confirmed by endoscopic retrograde cholangiography (ERC) which visualized the fistula and cyst material in the bile duct. His complete recovery following targeted treatment including surgical cystectomy solidified the diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Cystic echinococcosis", "source_filename": "50---A-24-Year-Old-Man-of-Turkish-Origin-With-Jau_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old Caucasian male with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Fatigue and a rash on his neck that started as a papule 2 days earlier.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a rash on his neck that began as a papule two days prior. The lesion was non-pruritic but was associated with significant, non-painful swelling and a pressure sensation in the neck. He reported no fever, but had at least one episode of diaphoresis with mild confusion and headache. He recalled a break in the skin at the site three days before the lesion appeared while shaving. He had been seen 24 hours earlier for similar symptoms, was given a cephalosporin, and discharged, but returned due to worsening malaise, increased neck swelling, and mild difficulty breathing.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a postal worker in the United States. He denied any foreign travel within the previous year, contact with animals, or insect bites. The presentation occurred during an investigation into a possible bioterrorism event. It was later confirmed that he was exposed during his occupation while handling contaminated mail.", "vitals": "Temperature was 36.9\u00b0C (98.4\u00b0F), pulse was 118 bpm, blood pressure was 138/90 mmHg, and the respiratory rate was 20 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "Examination revealed a 2cm irregularly shaped, indurated, non-tender patch on the left anterior neck with mild erythema and several 2-3mm vesicles. The main lesion had a 6mm shallow ulceration. There was massive neck oedema, with neck circumference increased from a baseline of 57cm to 81cm, making lymph nodes difficult to assess. The remainder of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood work, including full blood count and chemistries, was normal except for a mildly elevated haemoglobin at 18.7 g/dL. Blood cultures taken at the first clinic visit grew gram-positive rods. Gram stain and culture from the skin lesion were negative for B. anthracis. A full-thickness skin biopsy performed at a reference laboratory showed abundant bacilli in the dermis, and immunohistochemical (IHC) staining detected B. anthracis antigens. Convalescent serology revealed the presence of antibody to protective antigen.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for an ulcerative skin lesion with massive soft tissue swelling included bacterial ecthyma (Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus aureus), rickettsial diseases, necrotic arachnidism, rat-bite fevers, ulceroglandular tularemia, and bubonic plague. Given the context of a possible bioterrorism event in the United States at the time, cutaneous anthrax was considered the most important diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with a first-generation cephalosporin but his condition worsened. Upon hospital admission, he received intravenous levofloxacin and ampicillin-sulbactam and subsequently recovered.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cutaneous anthrax caused by Bacillus anthracis. Although the direct culture of the skin lesion was negative, the diagnosis was confirmed by several key findings. Blood cultures grew gram-positive rods. A skin biopsy showed abundant bacilli, and immunohistochemical staining was positive for B. anthracis antigens. Furthermore, serology on convalescent serum was positive for antibodies to the protective antigen. The exposure was determined to be occupational, from handling contaminated mail during a bioterrorism event.", "disease_name_short": "Cutaneous anthrax", "source_filename": "21---A-35-Year-Old-American-Man-With-Fatigue-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropica.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 24-year-old woman from rural Uganda.", "chief_complaint": "A 5-day history of a febrile illness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began five days prior with fever, sore throat, and generalized aching. She subsequently developed abdominal pain and diarrhoea. Over the course of the past few days, she has become increasingly unwell, experiencing significant weakness to the point of needing assistance to stand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in rural Uganda. Her husband died from a severe febrile illness 6 days before she fell ill. He worked in a local gold mine, and she had cared for him at home during his final illness. Marburg virus disease has been associated with mines.", "vitals": "Her blood pressure is 85/65 mmHg, pulse rate is 105 bpm, and temperature is 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient appears very unwell and has bilateral conjunctivitis. There is no rash or lymphadenopathy. The heart sounds are normal, her chest is clear, and the abdominal examination is normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A malaria slide showed a low level of parasitaemia with P. falciparum. A sample was sent for Marburg and Ebola PCR, which later returned a positive result for Marburg virus.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis was broad given the non-specific presentation. Malaria and typhoid fever were considered but do not typically cause conjunctivitis. Other bacterial sepsis, including meningococcal disease, was a possibility. Severe viral infections like adenovirus or influenza were considered less likely due to the severity of illness. Measles was considered due to conjunctivitis but was less likely without a rash. Zika virus was also considered but usually causes a milder illness. The death of her husband from a similar illness raised high suspicion for a viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF), particularly Marburg virus disease, which has been associated with mines.", "management_and_clinical_course": "A presumed diagnosis of Marburg virus disease was made. The patient was isolated and treated empirically with IV artesunate followed by artemether/lumefantrine for malaria, IV ceftriaxone for possible sepsis, and was resuscitated with IV fluids. She remained very unwell for several days before starting to improve. After recovery, she was kept in isolation for two additional days before being discharged home. Public health authorities were alerted, and her contacts were monitored for 21 days.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Marburg virus disease. This was confirmed by a positive Marburg virus PCR result. The clinical presentation of a severe febrile illness with shock and conjunctivitis, along with the critical epidemiological link to her husband who worked in a mine and died of a similar illness, strongly pointed towards a viral hemorrhagic fever. A co-infection with Plasmodium falciparum was noted but was not considered the cause of her severe presentation.", "disease_name_short": "Marburg virus disease", "source_filename": "53---A-24-Year-Old-Woman-from-Uganda-With-Fe_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old woman from Malawi who is known to be HIV-positive and has been on antiretroviral treatment for the past 3 years. Her CD4 count is unknown.", "chief_complaint": "Referral for an ocular tumour in the left eye, with symptoms progressing over 8 months.", "history_of_present_illness": "Symptoms started 8 months prior with a whitish lesion growing on the conjunctiva of her left eye. Despite treatment with non-specified eye drops and later, herbal eye drops from a traditional healer, the lesion grew bigger and turned reddish. Over the months, the lesion continued to grow, she lost her eyesight in the affected eye, and the pain increased, leading her to seek further care.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi, a country in sub-Saharan Africa. Ocular Surface Squamous Neoplasia (OSSN) is commonly seen in HIV-positive individuals in tropical regions. HIV infection, ultraviolet radiation, and human papilloma virus (HPV) are strongly associated with OSSN.", "vitals": "The patient is afebrile.", "physical_exam": "There is localized swelling of the left eyeball and orbit with incomplete lid closure. Visual acuity is 6/6 on the right, while the left eye has no light perception. Her left preauricular lymph nodes are swollen. The rest of her physical examination is unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The patient is known to be HIV-positive. No other diagnostic tests, such as a biopsy for histopathological examination, were performed as the patient refused surgical intervention.", "differential_diagnosis": "The most likely diagnosis was considered to be advanced ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN). Other malignant lesions that can look similar include amelanotic melanomas, lymphomas, or adenocarcinomas. In its early stages, the lesion might be confused with benign conditions such as pterygium, an amelanotic naevus, or a lipoma.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The recommended management was analgesic treatment and an extended exenteration of the left eyeball and orbit. However, the patient refused surgical intervention and would only accept conservative treatment. Her left orbit was covered with antibiotics and bandaged. After her pain was sufficiently controlled, she was discharged with an offer to return at any time and was asked to report to her antiretroviral therapy (ART) clinic for control of her viral load.", "final_diagnosis": "The suspected diagnosis is advanced ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN). This diagnosis is based on the clinical presentation of a progressive ocular tumor leading to blindness and pain in an HIV-positive woman from a high-prevalence region. Although histopathological confirmation was not obtained because the patient refused surgery, the clinical picture is highly characteristic of this condition.", "disease_name_short": "Ocular surface squamous neoplasia", "source_filename": "43---A-35-Year-Old-Malawian-Woman-With-a-Pain_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropica.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 14-year-old girl from the Solomon Islands.", "chief_complaint": "Painless, non-healing large ulcer of the left leg for one month.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 14-year-old girl reported a painless, non-healing large ulcer on her left leg that began one month earlier. A smaller, similar satellite lesion was also noted lower down on the same leg, which is a typical presentation for early yaws lesions. There were no other reported symptoms.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient was from the Solomon Islands, a region in the West Pacific where yaws is still a common problem. Yaws is spread by skin-to-skin contact in warm, humid environments and is normally seen in young children.", "vitals": null, "physical_exam": "On examination, there was a large lesion approximately 3 cm in diameter on the left lower leg, covered by a layer of crust. A smaller, similar lesion was noted further down the leg. There was no regional lymphadenopathy or evidence of any other skin or bone lesions.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A rapid plasma reagin (RPR) test was positive at a titre of 1:64. The patient was seen again 12 months later, and a repeat RPR test was positive at a titre of 1:8.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary diagnosis considered was yaws. Other relevant differential diagnoses included skin lesions caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, pyoderma, tropical ulcer, cutaneous diphtheria, and Aeromonas hydrophila infection.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a single intramuscular dose of benzathine benzylpenicillin, and the skin lesion resolved over the next 10 days. She presented again 12 months later with a similar ulcer on the right leg. The repeat RPR titre showed a greater than four-fold fall to 1:8, which was considered evidence of cure from the initial infection. The new ulcer on the other leg was therefore interpreted to be of a different aetiology.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was yaws, a chronic infectious disease caused by Treponema pallidum subspecies pertenue. The diagnosis was based on the clinical findings of a characteristic chronic leg ulcer with a satellite lesion in a patient from an endemic region. The diagnosis was supported by a high-titer positive rapid plasma reagin (RPR) test of 1:64, and confirmed by the lesion's resolution after treatment and a greater than four-fold fall in the RPR titre at follow-up, indicating cure.", "disease_name_short": "Yaws", "source_filename": "86---A-14-Year-Old-Girl-in-the-Solomon-Islands-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 34-year-old man who is very wasted with a body mass index of 14kg/m2. He was found to have a new diagnosis of HIV and was highly immunosuppressed with a CD4 cell count of 2/\u03bcL.", "chief_complaint": "A 2-week history of fever and a papular rash.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a two-week history of fever and a papular rash affecting his whole body, particularly his face and trunk. He also reported a significant weight loss of 7kg over the last three months.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in Phuket, Thailand. He arrived in Germany three days prior to presentation to visit friends.", "vitals": "The patient was febrile with a temperature of 38.2\u00b0C (100.76\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "On examination, his conjunctivae were pale. He had a generalized non-pruritic rash, predominantly on his face and trunk, consisting of small umbilicated papules. There was generalized lymphadenopathy, with swollen lymph nodes in the left supraclavicular, inguinal, and axillary regions. Abdominal examination revealed a palpable spleen two fingers below the left costal margin and a liver span of 15cm in the midclavicular line.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results revealed pancytopenia with a WBC of 2.1 \u00d710^9/L, haemoglobin of 9.8g/dL, and platelets of 110 \u00d710^9/L. The C-reactive protein was elevated at 150mg/L. An HIV test was positive, and the CD4 cell count was 2/\u03bcL. Histopathology of a lymph node biopsy showed multiple yeast-like structures. Blood cultures, as well as cultures from a skin biopsy and lymph node aspirate, all grew Talaromyces (Penicillium) marneffei.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for fever, generalized lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly included infectious diseases and neoplasms. Viral causes such as CMV and EBV were considered. Disseminated tuberculosis, atypical mycobacteria, and melioidosis were important considerations. Other possibilities included Bartonella spp. causing bacillary angiomatosis, and fungal infections like cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis, and talaromycosis, which can present with umbilicated papules. Visceral leishmaniasis and lymphomas were also considered.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with talaromycosis and started on a 2-week course of intravenous liposomal amphotericin B, followed by oral itraconazole. Antiretroviral therapy (ART) was also initiated. At a 3-week follow-up, he was afebrile and gaining weight. Six months later, he developed a recurrence of cervical lymphadenopathy, which was found to be due to an infection with atypical mycobacteria, interpreted as an immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS).", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was disseminated talaromycosis in the setting of severe immunosuppression from a new diagnosis of HIV. The causative agent, Talaromyces (Penicillium) marneffei, was definitively identified from cultures of blood, skin biopsy, and a lymph node aspirate. The diagnosis was further supported by histopathology of the lymph node which showed multiple yeast-like structures, consistent with the fungus.", "disease_name_short": "Talaromycosis", "source_filename": "17---A-34-Year-Old-Man-from-Thailand-With-Feve_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 34-year-old Malawian woman who was diagnosed with smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis 5 months prior to presentation. At that time, she was also found to be HIV-reactive with a baseline CD4 count of 54/\u03bcL. She is on antituberculous therapy, vitamin B6, antiretroviral therapy (ART), and co-trimoxazole prophylaxis.", "chief_complaint": "Slowly progressive weakness of the left arm and leg for approximately 3 months.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient's problems began insidiously about 3 months earlier, starting with a limp in her left leg. The weakness progressed, and over the following weeks, her left arm also became affected. She has experienced slow, continuous progression since onset. She denies any head trauma, headache, recent fever, nausea, visual impairment, or weight loss. A review of systems was unremarkable.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and lives in an urban high-density area. She works as a street vendor selling mobile phone vouchers.", "vitals": "She is afebrile and her vital signs are normal.", "physical_exam": "General examination was normal apart from slightly pale conjunctivae. Fundoscopy was normal with no signs of papilledema or retinitis. The neurological examination revealed a spastic hemiparesis on the left with hyperreflexia. Power was graded 2/5 in the left leg and 3/5 in the left arm. A pronator drift was present on the left. Sensation of pain was reduced in her left leg and hand. Cranial nerve examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Full blood count showed WBC 3.8 x 10^9/L, hemoglobin 9.9 g/dL, and platelets 140 x 10^9/L. Routine CSF examination was normal, and India Ink stain and fungal cultures were negative. A cerebral MRI scan showed multifocal T2 hyperintense lesions exclusively affecting the white matter, more prominent in the right hemisphere (frontal and temporal lobes), with a small area of demyelination in the left cerebellar peduncle.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential was for focal brain lesions in an HIV-infected individual. Ischemic or hemorrhagic strokes were considered unlikely due to the subacute, progressive onset. Key considerations included cerebral toxoplasmosis, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), CNS lymphoma, cryptococcoma, and CNS tuberculosis (tuberculoma). The timing of onset, 2 months after starting ART, raised suspicion for Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS), which could unmask or paradoxically worsen a pre-existing CNS infection like PML or tuberculoma.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was started on empirical antitoxoplasmosis treatment with high-dose co-trimoxazole while continuing her ART and antituberculous therapy. Prednisolone 1mg/kg was added to cover for presumed IRIS. Her clinical status was unchanged at 4 weeks, and by 8 weeks, she showed further deterioration of power in her left hand. After the MRI findings, a presumed diagnosis of PML was made. She was referred to a rehabilitation centre for walking aids, and the palliative care team was involved.", "final_diagnosis": "A presumed diagnosis of Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (PML) was made, likely as a form of immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (PML-IRIS). The diagnosis was based on the clinical picture of an insidious, progressive neurological deficit in a severely immunocompromised HIV patient who recently started ART. The diagnosis was supported by characteristic MRI findings of multifocal, non-enhancing T2 hyperintense lesions in the white matter and a lack of clinical response to empirical treatment for cerebral toxoplasmosis. PML is caused by the reactivation of the JC polyomavirus.", "disease_name_short": "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy", "source_filename": "51---A-34-Year-Old-HIV-Positive-Woman-from-Malawi-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-T.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 7-year-old girl from South Sudan who is severely malnourished (Z-score <3). She has never been admitted to a hospital before the current illness.", "chief_complaint": "A 4-week history of undulating fever.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-week history of undulating fever, occurring mainly in the afternoon hours and accompanied by chills and sometimes convulsions. Initially, she was well between febrile episodes, but over time she developed progressive anorexia, a dry cough, chest pain, and joint and back pain. She had recently visited another clinic where she received unspecified tablets without improvement.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from South Sudan, residing in an area known to be endemic for visceral leishmaniasis (VL). Her family history is significant for an uncle who had been treated for VL and a sister who was recently treated for tuberculosis (TB).", "vitals": "On presentation, her vital signs were: temperature 39.6\u00b0C (103.3\u00b0F), pulse 96 bpm, and blood pressure 100/60 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "The girl was alert and pale, but not jaundiced, and severely malnourished. Her chest sounds were clear with normal heart sounds. The abdomen was soft with a splenomegaly of 4 cm below the left costal margin. There were multiple enlarged lymph nodes of about 1 cm in diameter in the cervical, axillary, inguinal, and epitrochlear regions. No skin lesions or peripheral oedema were noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A full blood count showed pancytopenia, with a white blood cell count of 1.35 x10\u2079/L and haemoglobin of 6.8 g/dL. Initial rapid diagnostic tests were negative for Plasmodium falciparum and visceral leishmaniasis (rK39-antibody RDT). A blood film for malaria parasites was also negative, as was Brucella species serology (IgG and IgM). Subsequently, a Direct Agglutination Test (DAT) for visceral leishmaniasis came back positive with a high titre of \u22651:6400.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses included visceral leishmaniasis and brucellosis, given the chronic fever, splenomegaly, and wasting in a child from South Sudan. Tuberculosis and HIV infection were also considered due to the constellation of chronic fever, anorexia, weight loss, splenomegaly, and generalized lymphadenopathy. Malaria was deemed unlikely due to two negative tests and an atypical chronic course with lymphadenopathy. Other considerations included typhoid fever, splenic abscess, chronic schistosomiasis, and malignancies like leukaemia and lymphoma, though these were less likely given the full clinical picture.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the hospital, where her health continued to deteriorate with persistently high fever and increasing pallor. Based on the positive DAT result and her critical condition, she was started on liposomal amphotericin B and a broad-spectrum antibiotic (ceftriaxone). She also received nutritional support with high-energy/high-protein ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF) and vitamin/mineral supplementation. Her fever settled after 5 days, and she started to recover. Two weeks later, she had gained weight and was able to walk and play again.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar). This was based on the clinical presentation of chronic undulating fever, splenomegaly, generalized lymphadenopathy, and pancytopenia, combined with strong epidemiological evidence of originating from a known endemic area with a family history of the disease. The diagnosis was confirmed by a positive Direct Agglutination Test (DAT) at a high titre (\u22651:6400) and a favorable clinical response to treatment with liposomal amphotericin B.", "disease_name_short": "Visceral leishmaniasis", "source_filename": "44---A-7-Year-Old-Girl-from-South-Sudan-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropica.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old Peruvian man with a past medical history of hepatitis of unknown etiology 20 years prior. He had not received any vaccinations since his early childhood.", "chief_complaint": "Fever, retro-orbital headache, jaundice, bleeding, and altered mental status for eight days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began eight days prior to presentation with fever and retro-orbital headache. Two days into the illness, he developed jaundice, coffee-ground vomiting, gross haematuria, and increasing mental obtundation, leading to admission at a local hospital in the jungle. During transfer to the referral hospital in Lima, he experienced a generalized seizure.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient worked as a logging supervisor for the past 5 years in a remote jungle area near Pucallpa, Peru, on the shores of the Ucayali River. This occupation created possible contact with sylvatic mosquitoes and animals. He reported not taking any malaria prophylaxis.", "vitals": "On admission, his temperature was 38.8\u00b0C, blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg, heart rate was 110 beats per minute, and respiratory rate was 24 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was agitated, unresponsive to verbal commands, and intermittently stuporous. He was jaundiced, with spontaneous bleeding from his oral mucosa and at venipuncture sites, and had multiple large ecchymoses on his face, trunk, and limbs. Neurological examination revealed a marked flapping of hands (asterixis) with no focal or meningeal signs. Lung auscultation revealed crepitant rales in both bases. The liver was felt 3 cm below the right costal margin, while the spleen was not palpable and there was no lymphadenopathy.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial labs showed anaemia (haematocrit 30%), thrombocytopenia (platelets 110,000/\u03bcL), severe transaminitis (AST 2890 U/L, ALT 2676 U/L), hyperbilirubinemia (total bilirubin 11.6 mg/dL), and coagulopathy (INR 2.6, aPTT 80 s). Urinalysis showed proteinuria and hematuria. Three malaria thick films were negative, as were blood cultures and serologies for Brucella and Hepatitis B (IgM anti-Hbc). The diagnosis was confirmed by a highly positive IgM capture ELISA for yellow fever at a 1:10000 titer. Anti-dengue IgM was negative. A chest x-ray eventually showed bilateral pulmonary infiltrates compatible with ARDS.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for this acute febrile illness with liver failure included severe malaria, though the transaminases were exceptionally high. Other considerations were viral hepatitis (A, B, C, D, E), toxin-mediated hepatitis, leptospirosis, typhoid fever, and brucellosis. Severe dengue was less likely due to the prolonged fever and low hematocrit. Given his occupation, jungle exposure, and unvaccinated status, a viral haemorrhagic fever, particularly yellow fever, was highly suspected.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient received presumptive treatment for malaria, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and supportive care. Over the subsequent three days, his condition worsened with increasing hepatic encephalopathy, renal failure (creatinine 6.5 mg/dL), coma, and disseminated intravascular coagulation with severe bleeding. He developed ARDS confirmed by chest x-ray. His liver transaminases dropped suddenly on day 3, indicating acute hepatic disintegration. The patient died one day later.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Yellow Fever. This was based on the clinical presentation of fever, jaundice, bleeding, and multi-organ failure in an unvaccinated individual with significant occupational exposure as a logger in an endemic region of Peru. The diagnosis was confirmed serologically by a highly positive IgM capture ELISA for yellow fever (1:10000). Alternative diagnoses such as malaria, bacterial sepsis, hepatitis B, and dengue were ruled out by negative laboratory investigations.", "disease_name_short": "Yellow fever", "source_filename": "93---A-35-Year-Old-Male-Logger-from-Peru-With-Fe_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 55-year-old woman of Turkish provenance with a history of rheumatic fever in childhood. She has undergone mechanical mitral and aortic valve replacement at the ages of 37 and 53 years, respectively (St Jude Medical prostheses).", "chief_complaint": "Intermittent fever up to 39.5\u00b0C, night sweats, chest pain, and fatigue for several weeks.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with intermittent fever, night sweats, chest pain, and fatigue. She was initially diagnosed with a urinary tract infection based on urinary cultures and treated with co-trimoxazole. Her fever initially settled and her condition improved, but she subsequently developed increasing dyspnoea and progressed into congestive heart failure.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had visited her relatives in Turkey several weeks prior to her presentation in Germany.", "vitals": "The patient had intermittent fever up to 39.5\u00b0C (103.1\u00b0F). Her blood pressure and heart rate were within normal limits.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed unremarkable prosthetic heart sounds and a systolic grade 1 murmur over the aortic area without radiation. The liver and spleen were not enlarged, and no lymph nodes were palpable. No haemorrhages or petechiae were detectable on the skin.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a slight anaemia (Hb 11.5g/dL), a C-reactive protein of 15mg/dL, and normal white blood cell count, lymphocyte\u2013neutrophil ratio, platelet count, serum creatinine, and transaminases. Initial blood cultures were negative, but urinary cultures yielded Enterobacteriaceae. After 4 weeks of incubation, the initial blood cultures grew Brucella melitensis biovar 2. A chest radiography showed no infiltrates, and an initial transthoracic echocardiography demonstrated competent prosthetic valves. A subsequent transoesophageal echocardiography revealed a large vegetation attached to the prosthetic mitral valve. Two months later, a follow-up echocardiogram demonstrated an annular abscess cavity around the aortic prosthesis.", "differential_diagnosis": "Differential diagnoses considered upon her deterioration included urosepsis. Due to her preceding stay in Turkey, other infections such as brucellosis, tuberculosis, or Q-fever were also suspected. Given her two prosthetic heart valves, infective endocarditis was a primary concern.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was first treated with co-trimoxazole for a presumed urinary tract infection, which led to temporary improvement. After being diagnosed with Brucella endocarditis, she was started on a triple therapy of rifampicin, doxycycline, and gentamicin, leading to rapid improvement and normalization of her C-reactive protein after 6 weeks. However, two months later, while on oral rifampicin and doxycycline, an annular abscess was found. She underwent a third thoracotomy for prostheses exchange, after which she made a complete and sustained recovery.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with active Brucella endocarditis. The diagnosis was confirmed by the isolation of Brucella melitensis biovar 2 from blood cultures after four weeks of incubation, in conjunction with a transoesophageal echocardiogram that revealed a large vegetation on her prosthetic mitral valve. Her travel history to Turkey provided a strong epidemiological link for this zoonotic infection.", "disease_name_short": "Brucella endocarditis", "source_filename": "76---A-55-Year-Old-Woman-from-Turkey-With-Feve_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 46-year-old Thai woman with a BMI of 26 kg/m2. A diagnostic work-up found no evidence of immunosuppression.", "chief_complaint": "A 2-month history of recurrent cutaneous and subcutaneous abscesses, progressive lymphadenopathy, and weight loss.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 2-month history of progressive symptoms including recurrent skin abscesses, lymphadenopathy, and weight loss. These symptoms began 6 to 8 weeks after her return from a trip to Thailand. During her stay in Thailand, she had suffered from an illness with high fever, dry cough, and fatigue. Her condition had deteriorated despite various antibiotic therapies prior to transfer to a German clinic.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient's symptoms started 6 to 8 weeks after she returned from a family visit to northern Thailand, a region where histoplasmosis is endemic.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient was afebrile with a blood pressure of 130/80 mmHg and a pulse of 80 bpm.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed enlarged, tender cervical, nuchal, inguinal, and axillary lymph nodes. There was massive, tender swelling of the upper eyelids. Disseminated, fluctuant cutaneous and subcutaneous abscesses with surrounding erythema were present, which discharged pus on slight pressure. A painful swelling of the left elbow was also noted. Otherwise, the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a white blood cell count of 30.8 x 10^9/L, hemoglobin of 8.2 g/dL, and an ESR >110 mm/h. Pus and blood cultures yielded no growth, and an initial lymph node biopsy showed non-specific lymphadenitis. A radiograph of the left elbow revealed osteolytic lesions, osteomyelitis, and an articular effusion. The definitive diagnosis was made from an extirpated submandibular lymph node, where histology showed fungal cells identified as Histoplasma capsulatum by PCR and later confirmed by culture. Serology for histoplasmosis was negative. After developing a seizure, CSF analysis showed lymphocytic pleocytosis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis focused on systemic infection. Given the failure of antibiotics and negative standard cultures, considerations included fungal infections like histoplasmosis, mycobacterial infections, or specific bacteria such as Burkholderia pseudomallei (melioidosis), Brucella, or Actinomyces. Underlying immunodeficiency was also considered. If microbiology remained negative, rare autoimmune syndromes like idiopathic nodular panniculitis were a possibility.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated empirically with imipenem for suspected melioidosis, which resulted in only slight improvement. After the diagnosis of histoplasmosis was confirmed, she was started on liposomal amphotericin B (3mg/kg per day). During this treatment, she developed a generalized seizure, considered a possible cerebral manifestation of the disease. Her clinical condition and inflammatory markers improved dramatically on antifungal therapy. She was subsequently transitioned to itraconazole maintenance therapy for an additional 6 months. On follow-up, her general condition was fair, with resolved lymphadenopathy and partial recalcification of osteolytic lesions.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was disseminated histoplasmosis caused by Histoplasma capsulatum. This was established by histopathology of a submandibular lymph node biopsy showing fungal cells, which were definitively identified as H. capsulatum by both PCR and subsequent culture. The diagnosis was made despite negative serology. The clinical syndrome of disseminated abscesses, lymphadenopathy, and osteomyelitis following travel to an endemic region (northern Thailand), along with the dramatic clinical response to specific antifungal therapy, confirmed the diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Disseminated histoplasmosis", "source_filename": "61---A-48-Year-Old-Woman-from-Thailand-With-Fever-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
