{"patient_information": "A 43-year-old German man with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Recurrent fever for three days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient complains of recurrent fever for the past three days, with a temperature as high as 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F) the day before presentation. He also reports some headache and diarrhoea. Approximately three weeks prior, while in Mozambique, he experienced fever and self-treated for presumed malaria with atovaquone/proguanil from April 27th to 29th.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a traveller who was in Mozambique from April 16th to April 30th, followed by a trip to Chile from April 30th to May 17th. He did not take any malaria chemoprophylaxis while in Mozambique. He reports freshwater contact in a small lake near Maputo, Mozambique.", "vitals": "On presentation, the patient was febrile with a temperature of 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F). The previous day, his temperature had reached 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was normal, with no rash, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, or lymphadenopathy noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a normal total white blood cell count (9.0 x 10^9/L) but significant eosinophilia (2.1 x 10^9/L). C-reactive protein was elevated at 57.9 mg/L, and liver enzymes (AST, ALT, GGT) and LDH were slightly raised. Thick films for Plasmodium species were negative. A chest radiograph and CT scan revealed nodular lesions (2-5mm) in the periphery of the lower lung zones bilaterally. Initial microscopy of three stool and urine samples for Schistosoma eggs were negative, as were initial antischistosomal antibody tests (enzyme-immunoassay and IFA). However, four weeks later, antischistosomal antibodies became positive (IFA 1:1280; cercarial and egg-ELISA positive), and Schistosoma mansoni ova were found in the stool.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis for fever in a returned traveler from Mozambique was malaria, especially given the lack of prophylaxis. Other considerations included typhoid fever and amoebic liver abscess. Viral infections like hepatitis, EBV, and CMV were considered due to elevated liver enzymes, but the absence of lymphocytosis made them less likely. The pronounced eosinophilia, however, strongly suggested a helminth infection. Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome) was the most likely diagnosis given the combination of fever, eosinophilia, and a history of freshwater exposure. Acute fascioliasis was also a rare possibility.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent diagnostic investigations. After initial tests for malaria and schistosomiasis were negative, repeat testing was performed. Four weeks after presentation, schistosomiasis was confirmed by positive serology and the detection of ova in the stool. The patient was subsequently treated with praziquantel.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome) caused by Schistosoma mansoni. This was established by the delayed appearance of positive antischistosomal antibodies (IFA 1:1280 and positive ELISA) and the eventual identification of S. mansoni ova in the stool, four weeks after initial negative tests. The clinical picture of fever, profound eosinophilia, nodular pulmonary lesions, and a clear history of freshwater exposure in Mozambique strongly supported this diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Acute schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "20---A-43-Year-Old-Male-Traveller-Returning-from-M_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 7-year-old girl from a tropical region of a West African country. There is no history of relevant trauma or other systemic symptoms.", "chief_complaint": "Progressive skin ulcers on the right hand and wrist with associated hand contracture over a period of three months.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began three months prior after an insect bite, starting as an itchy papule on the back of her right hand that gradually enlarged. Initial treatments from a traditional healer with herbal remedies were ineffective. The lesion ulcerated a few weeks later, and she received diclofenac and dexamethasone at a local health post. A second lesion then appeared, and a course of oxacillin was initiated with no effect. Concurrently, she became progressively unable to use her right hand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from a district in the tropical region of a West African country. The history mentions an insect bite, but this is considered an incidental finding as the mode of transmission for the causative organism is unknown.", "vitals": "Pulse was 108 bpm, blood pressure was 100/70 mmHg, and temperature was 37.9\u00b0C (100.2\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient was an anxious girl in good general condition, holding her right wrist in a 45\u00b0 flexion and 20\u00b0 abduction position. Two skin ulcers were present: a 3 x 3 cm ulcer on the back of her right hand filled with necrotic tissue, and a 0.5 x 1 cm ulcer on the medial side of her right wrist. The ulcers were surrounded by an ill-defined induration (12 x 8 cm) with hypo- and hyperpigmentation, lichenification, desquamation, and oedema extending from the lower arm to the fingers. A 0.5 x 0.5 cm nodule was also noted above the medial right elbow.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory confirmation was achieved via PCR from a wound swab, which confirmed the presence of Mycobacterium ulcerans.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary suspected diagnosis was Buruli ulcer, based on the geographical region, patient's age, and clinical picture of progressive cutaneous ulcers on an extremity without major trauma. Other differential diagnoses for ulcers in tropical countries include various infectious (bacterial, viral, fungal, parasitic) and non-infectious (trauma, envenoming, autoimmune, haematological, neoplastic) causes.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent 8 weeks of specific antimycobacterial therapy and wound care. During this period, the ulcer on the back of her hand healed almost completely, but the second, smaller ulcer paradoxically increased in size to 8 x 10 cm. Due to this, she required surgical debridement and skin grafting. The contracture of her wrist was corrected with physiotherapy, and she made an uneventful recovery.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Buruli ulcer disease caused by Mycobacterium ulcerans. The diagnosis was strongly suspected based on the clinical presentation of progressive, necrotizing skin ulcers with surrounding induration and oedema, leading to a joint contracture in a child from an endemic region in West Africa. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a positive PCR test for M. ulcerans from a wound swab.", "disease_name_short": "Buruli ulcer", "source_filename": "41---A-7-Year-Old-Girl-from-West-Africa-With-Two-Ski_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 4-year-old unimmunized boy from a very poor family in Laos, who appears stunted, thin, and chronically malnourished. He had a history of fever and rash about 2 months prior, from which he recovered.", "chief_complaint": "A dark sore on the cheek, present for three days, that progressed rapidly.", "history_of_present_illness": "Three days prior to presentation, the family noticed a dark sore on the child\u2019s cheek. The lesion progressed quickly, eating through his cheek. Associated symptoms included bad breath, poor appetite, and listlessness. About two months ago, the child had a fever and rash and recovered.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The child lives in a remote village in Laos and was seen at a remote district hospital. His family is very poor. Risk factors for his condition include extreme poverty, chronic malnutrition, and being unimmunized. The discussion mentions that living in close proximity to livestock and poor sanitation are also risk factors.", "vitals": null, "physical_exam": "The child appears small, quiet, stunted, and thin. His mouth has a gangrenous lesion that has destroyed part of his upper and lower lips and cheek, exposing his teeth.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "There were no diagnostic laboratory tests performed, as the diagnosis is made clinically.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included a tooth abscess and cellulitis, especially early on. However, the rapid destruction of tissue is typical of noma, an opportunistic infection affecting malnourished children. Other possibilities like oral cancer, syphilis, and yaws were considered unlikely in a young child. Cutaneous leishmaniasis was deemed unlikely due to the rapid and destructive pace, and mucocutaneous leishmaniasis is non-endemic in Laos.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The child was treated with penicillin and metronidazole to cover suspected aerobic and anaerobic oropharyngeal bacteria, along with crucial nutritional support using liquefied local foods. Recommendations also included looking for and treating other diseases like malaria, intestinal parasites, and tuberculosis. Necrotic tissue was to be removed, and physiotherapy was planned to prevent contractures. The child was treated successfully with antibiotics and nutritional support. His face healed with contractures, resulting in disfigurement and salivary incontinence. At age 9, he underwent two reconstructive surgeries which improved his appearance and corrected the salivary incontinence.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is noma. This is a clinical diagnosis based on the presence of a rapidly advancing gangrenous lesion of the face in a chronically malnourished and unimmunized child from a poor, developing country. Other ulcerating facial lesions were considered less likely due to the patient's age and the lesion's rapid, destructive progression. The diagnosis was supported by the patient's positive response to antibiotics and nutritional support.", "disease_name_short": "Noma", "source_filename": "5---A-4-Year-Old-Boy-from-Laos-With-a-Lesion-o_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 33-year-old male refugee from Afghanistan.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-month history of recurrent fever, night sweats, and progressive back pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient began feeling ill approximately 6 months prior to presentation while living in a refugee camp in Iran. His symptoms included recurrent fever, night sweats, and progressive back pain. He received treatment with tablets and intragluteal injections for several weeks, which led to a temporary subsidence of symptoms, but they recurred after the treatment ended. All medical documents were lost during his subsequent journey to Germany.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a refugee from Afghanistan, a country where spinal brucellosis is highly endemic. He lived in a refugee camp in Iran before traveling through Turkey and Eastern Europe to reach Germany four months prior to presentation.", "vitals": "Upon presentation, the patient was afebrile and his vital signs were normal.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed marked spinal tenderness in the lumbar region. The remainder of the examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory tests showed normal differential blood count, liver and renal function, and LDH. Inflammatory markers were slightly raised, with a C-reactive protein of 26 mg/L (<5 mg/L) and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 40 mm/hour (<20 mm/hour). Urinalysis was unremarkable. An MRI of the spine revealed signal enhancement of the L3 and L4 vertebral bodies, the corresponding vertebral disc, and the right psoas muscle, with disc space narrowing and irregularities of the adjacent vertebral endplates. A chest x-ray showed no abnormalities. Several blood cultures and sputum samples for acid-fast bacilli were negative. A CT-guided biopsy of the spinal lesion was performed, and culture of the tissue sample grew Brucella melitensis. An echocardiogram showed no signs of endocarditis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis for infectious spondylitis in this patient included tuberculosis, which is the main cause globally, and spinal brucellosis, which is highly prevalent in his country of origin, Afghanistan. Other less likely infectious causes considered were Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, and Gram-negative bacteria such as Salmonella species.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's condition was classified as complicated brucellosis with spondylitis and para-vertebral abscess formation. He was treated with a 16-week course of doxycycline 100 mg twice daily and rifampicin 900mg daily. Additionally, he received intramuscular streptomycin (1 g daily) for the first 3 weeks. His fever subsided within several days, and inflammatory markers returned to normal several weeks after initiating therapy. A follow-up MRI at the end of treatment showed full resolution of the paravertebral abscess. One year after completing treatment, the patient showed no signs of relapse.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is complicated brucellosis with spondylitis of L3/L4 and a paravertebral abscess. This diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the isolation of the causative agent, Brucella melitensis, from a culture of a CT-guided biopsy tissue sample from the affected vertebra. The clinical presentation of chronic fever and back pain, imaging findings of spondylodiscitis, and the patient's epidemiological background from a highly endemic region strongly supported this diagnosis over alternatives like spinal tuberculosis.", "disease_name_short": "Brucellosis", "source_filename": "81---A-33-Year-Old-Refugee-from-Afghanistan-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 56-year-old Peruvian male with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 2-week history of fever, jaundice, and confusion.", "history_of_present_illness": "Daily fever began 3 months after a trip to a rural area. In the second week of his illness, the patient developed dark urine and jaundice. A few days prior to admission, his wife noted he had become confused and somnolent.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient spent 3 weeks on vacation in a rural area in the highlands of northern Peru at an altitude of 2400m. While there, he and his wife were bitten at night by tiny mosquitoes and used no personal protection. He had no other animal contact. The illness began 3 months after leaving this area.", "vitals": "On admission, his blood pressure was 90/60mmHg, pulse was 110 bpm and regular, temperature was 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F), and respiratory rate was 22 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was confused and disorientated (GCS 14/15) without focal neurological findings or meningeal signs. His skin and conjunctivae were markedly pale with scleral jaundice. Cardiovascular and pulmonary exams were normal. The liver was slightly enlarged, but there was no splenomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a WBC of 14.9 x 10^9/L with 13.1 x 10^9/L neutrophils and 0.9 x 10^9/L lymphocytes. Creatinine, electrolytes, and alkaline phosphatase were normal. Coomb\u2019s test was negative and CSF results were normal. A CT scan of the brain was normal, and an abdominal ultrasound revealed hepatomegaly without focal lesions. Blood films were negative for malaria. A thin film revealed massive red blood cell infestation by pleomorphic cocco-bacillary structures compatible with B. bacilliformis. Blood cultures, including on special media for Bartonella, were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses were malaria and bartonellosis. Plasmodium vivax was considered due to travel to the Peruvian highlands, but the patient's severe symptoms were atypical. Bartonellosis was highly suspected given the travel history to an endemic area for sandflies. Less common considerations included rickettsial diseases, leptospirosis, typhoid fever, brucellosis, and viral diseases such as yellow fever, though these were less likely as they do not typically cause significant hemolysis and some are not endemic to the region.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's condition worsened, progressing to shock requiring transfer to the ICU and treatment with vasopressors. He was started on intravenous ciprofloxacin and ceftriaxone for 10 days. His altered mental status resolved after 3 days of treatment, and his fever subsided after 5 days. By day 4, the bacteria were no longer visible on his blood smears. No further complications were observed during a 3-month follow-up period.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Bartonellosis, specifically the acute phase known as Oroya fever, caused by Bartonella bacilliformis. This conclusion is based on the clinical triad of prolonged fever, severe hemolytic anemia, and altered mental status following exposure in an endemic highland region of Peru. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the microscopic identification of massive infestation of red blood cells by cocco-bacillary structures consistent with B. bacilliformis on a thin blood smear, while tests for malaria were negative. The patient's rapid response to treatment with ciprofloxacin and ceftriaxone further supports the diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Bartonellosis (Oroya fever)", "source_filename": "52---A-56-Year-Old-Man-from-Peru-With-Prolonged-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 34-year-old man from northern Peru, living in Chile for 9 years. He is cachectic with a BMI of 17. His mother and one brother died from chronic abdominal symptoms and wasting of unknown origin. His Peruvian wife and children were reported as healthy initially.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-month history of severe chronic diarrhoea with colicky abdominal pain and a 20 kg weight loss.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 6-month history of severe chronic diarrhoea, colicky abdominal pain, and a weight loss of approximately 20 kg. For more than 12 years, he has suffered from recurrent episodes of diarrhoea and abdominal cramps, with an increased frequency over the past 2 years.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient originates from northern Peru, a tropical region endemic for Strongyloides stercoralis and HTLV-1. He has resided in Chile for the past 9 years. His family history is notable for a mother and brother who died from similar chronic wasting illnesses. Subsequent testing revealed asymptomatic S. stercoralis infection in his wife and one child, and his wife also tested positive for HTLV-1.", "vitals": "The patient was afebrile on presentation.", "physical_exam": "The patient was cachectic with severe loss of muscle mass, weighing 44 kg (BMI 17). Physical examination revealed slight pain on abdominal palpation but was otherwise normal, with no enlargement of the liver or spleen, no palpable lymphadenopathy, and no peripheral oedema.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a WBC of 11.7 x 10^9/L, hemoglobin of 12.4 g/dL, total protein of 4.9 g/dL, and albumin of 2.2 g/dL. An HIV test was negative. Abdominal CT and MRI scans were without pathological findings. An oesophagogastroduodenoscopy was macroscopically normal, but a duodenal biopsy showed chronic duodenitis with structures compatible with a helminth infection. A Baermann test on a stool sample demonstrated a high load of rhabditiform larvae of S. stercoralis. Larvae were also found in respiratory secretions but not in urine. HTLV-1 serology was confirmed to be positive.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for chronic diarrhoea and wasting included helminth infections, specifically Strongyloides stercoralis and Capillaria philippinensis, which are known to cause such severe symptoms. Given the patient's origin from a region endemic for S. stercoralis, this was considered the most probable cause. Intestinal protozoa like Cryptosporidium or Cystoisospora belli were also considered, particularly in the context of a potential immunocompromised state.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was hospitalized and stabilized with symptomatic treatment and parenteral nutrition. He was treated with oral ivermectin (200 \u00b5g/kg per day) for 7 days, which was repeated after 2 weeks. He recovered from his diarrhoea and abdominal symptoms within weeks. A parasitological follow-up examination at 3 months was negative, and he had gained 13 kg. His wife and one child were also treated for asymptomatic S. stercoralis infection.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Strongyloides stercoralis hyperinfection syndrome associated with HTLV-1 coinfection. The diagnosis was established by the clinical presentation of severe chronic diarrhea and wasting, confirmed by the identification of a high load of S. stercoralis rhabditiform larvae in both stool (via Baermann testing) and respiratory secretions. The underlying predisposing immunodeficiency was identified as HTLV-1 infection, confirmed by serology. The patient's dramatic clinical and weight improvement after ivermectin treatment further supported the diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome", "source_filename": "79---A-34-Year-Old-Male-Immigrant-from-Peru-With-Ch_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-T.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 20-year-old woman from a rural village in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. She has no relevant past medical history and is the mother of three children. She was born after worldwide smallpox vaccination programs were stopped and is therefore unvaccinated.", "chief_complaint": "Fever, malaise, headache, dry cough, neck swelling, and a disseminated skin rash that appeared two days after the initial symptoms began.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a history of fever, malaise, headache, and dry cough. Two days after these symptoms began, a disseminated skin rash appeared. The skin lesions first emerged on her face and then spread centrifugally over her body, including the palms and soles, as well as the oral mucosa. The lesions evolved sequentially from macules to papules, then to vesicles, and finally to pustules. The oral lesions were painful, making it difficult for her to drink and eat.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in a rural village in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, an area where monkeypox is endemic. The villagers' livelihood is based on agriculture. As she was born in the early 2000s, she was not vaccinated against smallpox following the cessation of routine vaccination programs in the early 1980s.", "vitals": "On presentation, her vital signs were: temperature 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F), pulse 110 beats per minute, and blood pressure 105/82 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed disseminated, uniform, umbilicated vesiculopustular skin lesions, 0.5 to 1 cm in diameter, distributed all over the body with a predilection for the face, hands, and feet. There was also generalized lymphadenopathy with prominent swelling of the cervical lymph nodes. Examination of the lungs, heart, abdomen, and central nervous system was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a WBC of 4.4 x 10^9/L (73% neutrophils, 21% lymphocytes), hemoglobin of 12.3 g/dL, and an ESR of 75 mm/h. No specific diagnostic tests such as PCR or culture were performed, as the diagnosis in this endemic setting was made clinically.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was a zoonotic poxvirus, particularly monkeypox, given the clinical presentation and the patient's location in an endemic region of Central Africa. Varicella was also considered, but the uniform stage of lesion development and involvement of palms and soles strongly favored monkeypox. Cowpox was deemed less likely as it typically presents with a single lesion and uncommonly features fever and lymphadenopathy.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's management was exclusively supportive, as no specific treatment for monkeypox is available. She made an uneventful recovery and was discharged from the hospital, though with residual scarring from the skin lesions.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was human monkeypox. This was a clinical diagnosis based on the classic presentation in a patient from an endemic region. The key features were an acute febrile illness followed by a disseminated, uniform vesiculopustular rash spreading centrifugally, involving the palms and soles, and accompanied by prominent lymphadenopathy. Her lack of prior smallpox vaccination was a contributing risk factor. Definitive laboratory confirmation was not available.", "disease_name_short": "Human monkeypox", "source_filename": "94---A-20-Year-Old-Woman-from-the-Democratic-Republic-_2022_Clinical-Cases-i.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 44-year-old male rice farmer from Laos with a history of type 2 diabetes diagnosed 4 years prior. He was reportedly not compliant with his oral antidiabetic medications or follow-up.", "chief_complaint": "A one-month history of fever, headache, generalized myalgia, arthralgia, and a painful swelling over the left scapula.", "history_of_present_illness": "For one month, the patient experienced fever, body aches, and a painful swelling on his left scapula that gradually increased in size without preceding trauma. A week before admission, the lesion was incised and drained at a local health center, and he was started on oral cloxacillin 1g four times daily. Despite this, his condition did not improve. In the two days prior to admission, he deteriorated with high fever, chills, and severe malaise.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a rice farmer in Southern Laos and presented during the rainy season. His occupation and geographic location are significant risk factors for melioidosis, which is commonly acquired via percutaneous inoculation through cuts while working in rice fields.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient appeared septic with a fever of 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F), a heart rate of 136 bpm, blood pressure of 140/80 mmHg, and a respiratory rate of 30 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was pale and jaundiced. There was a 2.5 x 4 cm erythematous swelling in the left scapular region with pus discharging from a central wound. No regional lymphadenopathy was noted. Chest auscultation revealed bilateral crepitations and reduced breath sounds at both lung bases. Heart sounds were normal, and the abdomen was soft without organomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a white blood cell count of 16.2 x10^9/L with 92.1% polymorphs, haemoglobin of 12.5 g/dL, and elevated glucose at 20.6 mmol/L. Liver function tests were abnormal with a total bilirubin of 111.2 \u00b5mol/L and direct bilirubin of 56.4 \u00b5mol/L. Pus culture from the back abscess grew Burkholderia pseudomallei after 48 hours, and blood cultures later grew the same organism. A chest radiograph revealed bilateral pleural effusions. An abdominal ultrasound showed hepatosplenomegaly but no liver or splenic abscesses.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included a bacterial abscess with sepsis caused by common organisms like Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus species. However, given the patient's epidemiological risk factors (diabetic rice farmer from Laos in the rainy season) and clinical presentation, melioidosis caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei was a primary consideration. Tuberculosis was also considered due to the sub-acute history and respiratory findings, though the abscess was not typical of a 'cold' tuberculous abscess. Disseminated fungal infection was deemed unlikely.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated empirically with ceftriaxone and gentamicin. After 48 hours, upon suspicion of melioidosis based on culture results, treatment was switched to ceftazidime. He improved after 7 days, became afebrile by day 14, and was then transitioned to oral co-trimoxazole to complete a 20-week course. His glycaemic control was also established. He was discharged after 21 days of hospitalization to continue the 5-month oral eradication therapy and remains well.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with melioidosis, presenting as septicemia with a back abscess and pneumonia. The diagnosis was confirmed by the isolation of Burkholderia pseudomallei from both pus culture from the abscess and blood cultures. The clinical picture of sepsis with disseminated infection in a patient with uncontrolled diabetes and relevant occupational exposure in an endemic region was highly consistent with this diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Melioidosis", "source_filename": "32---A-44-Year-Old-Male-Farmer-from-Laos-With-Di_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 24-year-old woman from rural Uganda.", "chief_complaint": "A 5-day history of a febrile illness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began five days prior with fever, sore throat, and generalized aching. She subsequently developed abdominal pain and diarrhoea. Over the course of the past few days, she has become increasingly unwell, experiencing significant weakness to the point of needing assistance to stand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in rural Uganda. Her husband died from a severe febrile illness 6 days before she fell ill. He worked in a local gold mine, and she had cared for him at home during his final illness. Marburg virus disease has been associated with mines.", "vitals": "Her blood pressure is 85/65 mmHg, pulse rate is 105 bpm, and temperature is 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient appears very unwell and has bilateral conjunctivitis. There is no rash or lymphadenopathy. The heart sounds are normal, her chest is clear, and the abdominal examination is normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A malaria slide showed a low level of parasitaemia with P. falciparum. A sample was sent for Marburg and Ebola PCR, which later returned a positive result for Marburg virus.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis was broad given the non-specific presentation. Malaria and typhoid fever were considered but do not typically cause conjunctivitis. Other bacterial sepsis, including meningococcal disease, was a possibility. Severe viral infections like adenovirus or influenza were considered less likely due to the severity of illness. Measles was considered due to conjunctivitis but was less likely without a rash. Zika virus was also considered but usually causes a milder illness. The death of her husband from a similar illness raised high suspicion for a viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF), particularly Marburg virus disease, which has been associated with mines.", "management_and_clinical_course": "A presumed diagnosis of Marburg virus disease was made. The patient was isolated and treated empirically with IV artesunate followed by artemether/lumefantrine for malaria, IV ceftriaxone for possible sepsis, and was resuscitated with IV fluids. She remained very unwell for several days before starting to improve. After recovery, she was kept in isolation for two additional days before being discharged home. Public health authorities were alerted, and her contacts were monitored for 21 days.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Marburg virus disease. This was confirmed by a positive Marburg virus PCR result. The clinical presentation of a severe febrile illness with shock and conjunctivitis, along with the critical epidemiological link to her husband who worked in a mine and died of a similar illness, strongly pointed towards a viral hemorrhagic fever. A co-infection with Plasmodium falciparum was noted but was not considered the cause of her severe presentation.", "disease_name_short": "Marburg virus disease", "source_filename": "53---A-24-Year-Old-Woman-from-Uganda-With-Fe_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 2-month-old baby girl from Laos, born at term, who had been well until the current illness. She was exclusively breastfed by her 24-year-old mother, a rice farmer.", "chief_complaint": "Three days of irritability, dyspnoea, and grunting.", "history_of_present_illness": "The infant suddenly became unwell three days prior to presentation. She developed irritability, dyspnoea, and grunting, along with poor breastfeeding. Her mother reported that she was neither feverish nor coughing.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in Vientiane, Laos. She was exclusively breastfed by her mother who practiced postpartum food avoidance, a common tradition in lowland Lao culture. The mother's diet consisted mainly of milled glutinous rice and she avoided beef, pork, vegetables, and fruits. The mother herself had symptoms of thiamine deficiency, including anorexia, weakness, a husky voice, and paresthesias. Infantile beriberi is noted to be relatively common in this region.", "vitals": "Temperature was 37.0\u00b0C (98.6\u00b0F), pulse was 140 bpm, and respiratory rate was 40 breath cycles per minute. Blood pressure was not taken.", "physical_exam": "On examination, the infant was irritable, crying, and grunting. She was dyspnoeic with central cyanosis. Findings consistent with heart failure were present, including hepatomegaly and oedematous extremities. Her chest was clear on auscultation, and no heart murmurs were heard.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Blood samples were taken from both the child and her mother. Analysis showed that both were thiamine-deficient.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses included congenital heart disease, respiratory diseases, meningitis, and infantile beriberi. Respiratory diseases were considered unlikely due to the absence of cough, wheeze, fever, and a normal chest exam. Congenital heart disease was less likely given the sudden onset of symptoms well after birth and the absence of a heart murmur. Meningitis was also unlikely in the absence of fever or bulging fontanels, making infantile beriberi the most probable diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The child received 50 mg of intravenous thiamine immediately upon admission. She had a quick and dramatic response; six hours later, she was able to breastfeed normally. The infant was discharged the following day. Her mother was given oral thiamine supplementation and counseled on returning to a well-balanced, thiamine-rich diet.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was infantile beriberi, caused by severe thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. The diagnosis was made based on the classic clinical presentation of acute heart failure with dyspnoea, cyanosis, and irritability in an exclusively breastfed infant from an endemic area. It was strongly supported by the mother's history of a restricted postpartum diet and her own symptoms of beriberi. The diagnosis was confirmed by the infant's rapid and dramatic clinical improvement following intravenous thiamine administration and by blood tests showing thiamine deficiency in both the mother and child.", "disease_name_short": "Infantile beriberi", "source_filename": "45---A-2-Month-Old-Girl-from-Laos-With-Dyspnoea-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 14-year-old boy from rural Malawi.", "chief_complaint": "The patient presented to a local hospital with an extensive necrotic wound on his right foot, three weeks after being bitten by a snake.", "history_of_present_illness": "Three weeks prior to presentation, the patient was bitten by a snake while playing. Shortly after the bite, he developed haemorrhagic bullae on his right leg, his gums started to bleed, and he vomited extensively. He was initially taken to a traditional healer, after which the bleeding and vomiting settled. However, he subsequently developed intense pain and swelling at the site of the bite, which progressed to the necrotic wound seen on admission.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from rural Malawi and was bitten on a path leading through rocky grassland to his family's maize field. The snake was described as brown with V-shaped black bands on its back, approximately one metre long, and it hissed loudly before biting. This description is consistent with an African puff-adder (Bitis arietans).", "vitals": "On admission, the patient appeared weak and was in respiratory distress. His temperature was 39.4\u00b0C, pulse was 126 bpm, blood pressure was 85/60 mmHg, and respiratory rate was 30 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "There was an extensive, foul-smelling necrosis affecting his right foot and ankle, with exposed tendons. The right lower leg was swollen, and he was unable to bend his right ankle. The inguinal lymph nodes were enlarged on the right side. His urine was clear, a rectal exam showed no signs of bleeding, there was no haemoptysis upon provoked coughing, and fundoscopy was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The 20-minute whole blood clotting test (WBCT20) was normal. Blood tests showed a haemoglobin of 9.5 mg/dL (reference 12\u201314) and potassium (K+) of 4.2 mmol/L. The WBC and platelet counts were not specified in the provided table.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary diagnostic challenge was identifying the snake species. The African puff-adder (Bitis arietans) was considered the most likely cause due to the snake's described appearance (one metre long, V-pattern on back), aggressive behavior (hissing), and the clinical syndrome of massive local swelling, haemorrhagic bullae, extensive necrosis, and systemic haemorrhage. The saw-scaled viper (Echis species) can produce similar symptoms. Bites by mambas were considered less likely as they are relatively uncommon.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Due to the three-week delay, antivenom was not administered. The patient was diagnosed as septic and managed with broad-spectrum antibiotics, fluid resuscitation, and 'ventilatory support'. He also received tetanus prophylaxis. The wound required surgical management with several debridements, followed by successful mesh-grafting. He was discharged several weeks later with some residual functional impairment of his right foot, requiring outpatient physiotherapy.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was a severe cytotoxic and haematotoxic snake envenomation with delayed presentation, complicated by extensive tissue necrosis and sepsis. The causative agent was most probably an African puff-adder (Bitis arietans), a conclusion based on the eyewitness description of the snake, the geographic location, and the clinical evolution characterized by initial systemic haemorrhage (bleeding gums) and severe, progressive local effects (haemorrhagic bullae, swelling, and deep necrosis).", "disease_name_short": "Snake envenomation", "source_filename": "26---A-14-Year-Old-Boy-from-Malawi-Who-Has-Bee_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 32-year-old Malawian woman who appeared wasted and slightly anaemic. An HIV test was positive, and her CD4 count was very low at 22/\u03bcL.", "chief_complaint": "A 3-week history of headache and blurred vision.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a headache of gradual onset over 3 weeks that did not respond to over-the-counter painkillers. She also reported blurred vision. There was no fever, history of convulsions, or head trauma. Prior to presentation, she received presumptive antimalarial treatment with artemether/lumefantrine and a 5-day course of amoxicillin from a local health centre, neither of which provided any benefit.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and resides in a region with a high prevalence of HIV.", "vitals": "All vital signs were normal and the patient was afebrile on presentation.", "physical_exam": "The patient was wasted and slightly anaemic with a GCS of 15/15 and no neck stiffness. Visual acuity was normal. Examination revealed an abduction deficit of the left eye on left lateral gaze, consistent with an abducens nerve palsy. The rest of the examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial blood tests showed a WBC of 3.7 x 10^9/L, normocytic anaemia with a haemoglobin of 10.2 g/dL, and thrombocytopenia. A malaria RDT was negative. HIV serology was positive with a CD4 count of 22/\u03bcL. A lumbar puncture revealed a markedly increased opening pressure of 50 cmH2O. The CSF was clear with 0 leukocytes/\u03bcL, elevated protein at 0.8 g/L, and low glucose at 1.97 mmol/L. India Ink stain of the CSF was negative, but the fungal culture was positive for Cryptococcus neoformans.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was chronic meningitis, with the most likely causes being cryptococcal meningitis and tuberculous meningitis, given the patient's presentation and epidemiology. Partially treated bacterial meningitis was considered but was less likely due to the gradual onset of symptoms. Malaria was considered unlikely due to the absence of fever, a negative RDT, and lack of cranial nerve palsy.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with cryptococcal meningitis and started on oral fluconazole 1200mg, as amphotericin B and flucytosine were unavailable. She was also started on co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. To manage the raised intracranial pressure and headache, she received repeated therapeutic lumbar punctures. Antiretroviral therapy was initiated 4 weeks after starting antifungal treatment. Despite this management, the patient returned to her village and died 6 weeks later of an unknown cause.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was cryptococcal meningitis caused by Cryptococcus neoformans. This was confirmed by a positive fungal culture from the cerebrospinal fluid. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical presentation of chronic headache and a cranial nerve palsy in a severely immunocompromised patient (newly diagnosed with HIV and a CD4 count of 22/\u03bcL), along with a markedly elevated CSF opening pressure.", "disease_name_short": "Cryptococcal meningitis", "source_filename": "35---A-32-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-Heada_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 4-year-old, HIV-negative boy from rural Mozambique.", "chief_complaint": "Severe oedema and skin lesions.", "history_of_present_illness": "Three weeks prior to presentation, the patient was treated for pneumonia. He was then admitted to a hospital 5 days earlier with a suspected diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome, presenting with edema that did not subside despite treatment with furosemide and prednisolone. The boy was reported to be miserable, apathetic, and refusing to eat.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from a rural area in Mozambique. The discussion notes that kwashiorkor mainly occurs in areas with a monotonous diet low in protein-to-energy ratio, with incidence peaking during the rainy season.", "vitals": null, "physical_exam": "The patient was oedematous with a puffy face and pitting oedema on the lower legs. His hair was brittle, sparse, and fair in colour. The skin was dry and hyperpigmented, peeling off like \u2018flaky paint\u2019, with ulcerative skin lesions most prominent in the groins.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a low serum albumin of 2.2 g/dL (reference range 3.0\u20135.2 g/dL) and a low haemoglobin of 7 g/dL (12\u201314 g/dL). A urine dipstick was suggested to differentiate from nephrotic syndrome, as kwashiorkor typically presents with minimal proteinuria (rarely exceeding 1+).", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was nephrotic syndrome, which also causes generalized edema and low serum albumin. However, this patient's additional clinical features, including characteristic skin and hair changes (flaky paint dermatosis, brittle fair hair) and apathy, are typical of kwashiorkor. Furthermore, nephrotic syndrome is characterized by heavy proteinuria (4+), which is not expected in kwashiorkor.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Initial treatment for suspected nephrotic syndrome with furosemide and prednisolone was ineffective. The patient was then managed according to WHO guidelines for severe acute malnutrition. This included prophylactic antibiotics and carefully calculated feeds, initially via a nasogastric tube as he did not tolerate oral feeds. His superinfected skin lesions were successfully treated with Gentian violet solution. By day 5, the edema began to settle, and he could eat by himself. After transitioning well to higher caloric feeds, he was discharged to a community feeding program on day 15.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is kwashiorkor, a form of severe acute malnutrition. This was established based on the clinical triad of generalized edema, hypoalbuminemia (2.2 g/dL), and characteristic dermatosis ('flaky paint' skin). The diagnosis was supported by the presence of hair changes, apathy, anemia, and a lack of response to diuretics, which helped distinguish it from nephrotic syndrome. The patient's clinical improvement and resolution of edema with therapeutic feeding confirmed the nutritional etiology.", "disease_name_short": "Kwashiorkor", "source_filename": "72---A-4-Year-Old-Boy-from-Mozambique-With-Sever_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 7-year-old girl from South Sudan who is severely malnourished (Z-score <3). She has never been admitted to a hospital before the current illness.", "chief_complaint": "A 4-week history of undulating fever.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-week history of undulating fever, occurring mainly in the afternoon hours and accompanied by chills and sometimes convulsions. Initially, she was well between febrile episodes, but over time she developed progressive anorexia, a dry cough, chest pain, and joint and back pain. She had recently visited another clinic where she received unspecified tablets without improvement.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from South Sudan, residing in an area known to be endemic for visceral leishmaniasis (VL). Her family history is significant for an uncle who had been treated for VL and a sister who was recently treated for tuberculosis (TB).", "vitals": "On presentation, her vital signs were: temperature 39.6\u00b0C (103.3\u00b0F), pulse 96 bpm, and blood pressure 100/60 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "The girl was alert and pale, but not jaundiced, and severely malnourished. Her chest sounds were clear with normal heart sounds. The abdomen was soft with a splenomegaly of 4 cm below the left costal margin. There were multiple enlarged lymph nodes of about 1 cm in diameter in the cervical, axillary, inguinal, and epitrochlear regions. No skin lesions or peripheral oedema were noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A full blood count showed pancytopenia, with a white blood cell count of 1.35 x10\u2079/L and haemoglobin of 6.8 g/dL. Initial rapid diagnostic tests were negative for Plasmodium falciparum and visceral leishmaniasis (rK39-antibody RDT). A blood film for malaria parasites was also negative, as was Brucella species serology (IgG and IgM). Subsequently, a Direct Agglutination Test (DAT) for visceral leishmaniasis came back positive with a high titre of \u22651:6400.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses included visceral leishmaniasis and brucellosis, given the chronic fever, splenomegaly, and wasting in a child from South Sudan. Tuberculosis and HIV infection were also considered due to the constellation of chronic fever, anorexia, weight loss, splenomegaly, and generalized lymphadenopathy. Malaria was deemed unlikely due to two negative tests and an atypical chronic course with lymphadenopathy. Other considerations included typhoid fever, splenic abscess, chronic schistosomiasis, and malignancies like leukaemia and lymphoma, though these were less likely given the full clinical picture.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the hospital, where her health continued to deteriorate with persistently high fever and increasing pallor. Based on the positive DAT result and her critical condition, she was started on liposomal amphotericin B and a broad-spectrum antibiotic (ceftriaxone). She also received nutritional support with high-energy/high-protein ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF) and vitamin/mineral supplementation. Her fever settled after 5 days, and she started to recover. Two weeks later, she had gained weight and was able to walk and play again.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar). This was based on the clinical presentation of chronic undulating fever, splenomegaly, generalized lymphadenopathy, and pancytopenia, combined with strong epidemiological evidence of originating from a known endemic area with a family history of the disease. The diagnosis was confirmed by a positive Direct Agglutination Test (DAT) at a high titre (\u22651:6400) and a favorable clinical response to treatment with liposomal amphotericin B.", "disease_name_short": "Visceral leishmaniasis", "source_filename": "44---A-7-Year-Old-Girl-from-South-Sudan-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropica.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old woman from Malawi who is known to be HIV-positive and has been on antiretroviral treatment for the past 3 years. Her CD4 count is unknown.", "chief_complaint": "Referral for an ocular tumour in the left eye, with symptoms progressing over 8 months.", "history_of_present_illness": "Symptoms started 8 months prior with a whitish lesion growing on the conjunctiva of her left eye. Despite treatment with non-specified eye drops and later, herbal eye drops from a traditional healer, the lesion grew bigger and turned reddish. Over the months, the lesion continued to grow, she lost her eyesight in the affected eye, and the pain increased, leading her to seek further care.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi, a country in sub-Saharan Africa. Ocular Surface Squamous Neoplasia (OSSN) is commonly seen in HIV-positive individuals in tropical regions. HIV infection, ultraviolet radiation, and human papilloma virus (HPV) are strongly associated with OSSN.", "vitals": "The patient is afebrile.", "physical_exam": "There is localized swelling of the left eyeball and orbit with incomplete lid closure. Visual acuity is 6/6 on the right, while the left eye has no light perception. Her left preauricular lymph nodes are swollen. The rest of her physical examination is unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The patient is known to be HIV-positive. No other diagnostic tests, such as a biopsy for histopathological examination, were performed as the patient refused surgical intervention.", "differential_diagnosis": "The most likely diagnosis was considered to be advanced ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN). Other malignant lesions that can look similar include amelanotic melanomas, lymphomas, or adenocarcinomas. In its early stages, the lesion might be confused with benign conditions such as pterygium, an amelanotic naevus, or a lipoma.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The recommended management was analgesic treatment and an extended exenteration of the left eyeball and orbit. However, the patient refused surgical intervention and would only accept conservative treatment. Her left orbit was covered with antibiotics and bandaged. After her pain was sufficiently controlled, she was discharged with an offer to return at any time and was asked to report to her antiretroviral therapy (ART) clinic for control of her viral load.", "final_diagnosis": "The suspected diagnosis is advanced ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN). This diagnosis is based on the clinical presentation of a progressive ocular tumor leading to blindness and pain in an HIV-positive woman from a high-prevalence region. Although histopathological confirmation was not obtained because the patient refused surgery, the clinical picture is highly characteristic of this condition.", "disease_name_short": "Ocular surface squamous neoplasia", "source_filename": "43---A-35-Year-Old-Malawian-Woman-With-a-Pain_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropica.json"}
