{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old woman from northern Tanzania, married with three children, with no significant past or family medical history. She does not smoke or take alcohol, and her HIV status was negative during her last pregnancy 12 months prior. There is no history of trauma.", "chief_complaint": "Loss of power and feeling in her legs for two days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was well until two days prior to presentation when she experienced acute, severe, continuous, burning back pain that radiated in a band-like fashion to her umbilicus, unrelieved by analgesics or position. Within 12 hours, the pain lessened, but she developed ascending numbness in her feet and legs up to her waist, loss of leg power, and loss of bladder control. She had a febrile illness three weeks previously which was treated as malaria.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from northern Tanzania. No other specific travel, vector, animal, or occupational exposures are mentioned.", "vitals": "Vital signs were normal upon clinical examination.", "physical_exam": "The patient was well-nourished with an unremarkable general examination, including no spinal tenderness, deformity, or gibbus. Neurologically, she was fully oriented with normal higher mental functions, cranial nerves, and upper limbs. Examination of the lower limbs revealed a flaccid paraparesis with a sensory level at T10. Her feet were in a slightly plantarflexed position. Tone was reduced, power was MRC grade 1/5 in all muscle groups, and reflexes were absent bilaterally. Plantar reflexes were extensor. Sensation to light touch was reduced up to the umbilicus, with impaired joint position sense in the feet and ankles and absent vibration sense up to the anterior iliac crests.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Full blood count, renal, and liver function tests were normal. ESR was <10 mm/h. Serological tests for HIV (Serology and p24 Antigen), VDRL, and schistosomiasis were negative. Urine and stool analysis for Schistosoma ova were also negative. X-rays of the chest and thoracolumbar spine were normal. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis showed a normal opening pressure, 11 white cells/\u03bcl (90% lymphocytes), and an elevated protein of 1.11 g/L. CSF glucose was normal, and Gram stain, Ziehl-Neelsen stain, Xpert MTB/RIF, and VDRL were all negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary clinical syndrome was acute flaccid paraparesis. The differential diagnosis included lesions of the peripheral nerves (polyneuropathy) and acute spinal cord lesions (myelopathy). The presence of a distinct sensory level at T10 and extensor plantar reflexes localized the lesion to the spinal cord. For a non-compressive myelopathy, the main considerations were acute transverse myelitis and vascular spinal cord ischaemia (\u201cspinal stroke\u201d).", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with corticosteroids and acyclovir. Management also included supportive care such as patient and family counselling, physiotherapy, regular turning, analgesia, and urinary catheterization. After three weeks of treatment, she showed partial improvement. At discharge, the power in her legs had improved to MRC grade 2-3/5, and she had a urinary catheter in situ. She was scheduled for outpatient follow-up.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is acute transverse myelitis. This is based on the acute onset of flaccid paraparesis with a distinct sensory level at T10 and bladder dysfunction, which localizes the lesion to the spinal cord. The diagnosis was confirmed by cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing inflammatory changes, specifically an increased lymphocyte count and elevated protein level. Extensive investigations ruled out common infectious causes in the region, including HIV, syphilis, tuberculosis, and schistosomiasis, making this a case of idiopathic acute transverse myelitis.", "disease_name_short": "Acute transverse myelitis", "source_filename": "48---A-31-Year-Old-Woman-from-Tanzania-With-Acu_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 34-year-old Malawian woman who was diagnosed with smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis 5 months prior to presentation. At that time, she was also found to be HIV-reactive with a baseline CD4 count of 54/\u03bcL. She is on antituberculous therapy, vitamin B6, antiretroviral therapy (ART), and co-trimoxazole prophylaxis.", "chief_complaint": "Slowly progressive weakness of the left arm and leg for approximately 3 months.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient's problems began insidiously about 3 months earlier, starting with a limp in her left leg. The weakness progressed, and over the following weeks, her left arm also became affected. She has experienced slow, continuous progression since onset. She denies any head trauma, headache, recent fever, nausea, visual impairment, or weight loss. A review of systems was unremarkable.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and lives in an urban high-density area. She works as a street vendor selling mobile phone vouchers.", "vitals": "She is afebrile and her vital signs are normal.", "physical_exam": "General examination was normal apart from slightly pale conjunctivae. Fundoscopy was normal with no signs of papilledema or retinitis. The neurological examination revealed a spastic hemiparesis on the left with hyperreflexia. Power was graded 2/5 in the left leg and 3/5 in the left arm. A pronator drift was present on the left. Sensation of pain was reduced in her left leg and hand. Cranial nerve examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Full blood count showed WBC 3.8 x 10^9/L, hemoglobin 9.9 g/dL, and platelets 140 x 10^9/L. Routine CSF examination was normal, and India Ink stain and fungal cultures were negative. A cerebral MRI scan showed multifocal T2 hyperintense lesions exclusively affecting the white matter, more prominent in the right hemisphere (frontal and temporal lobes), with a small area of demyelination in the left cerebellar peduncle.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential was for focal brain lesions in an HIV-infected individual. Ischemic or hemorrhagic strokes were considered unlikely due to the subacute, progressive onset. Key considerations included cerebral toxoplasmosis, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), CNS lymphoma, cryptococcoma, and CNS tuberculosis (tuberculoma). The timing of onset, 2 months after starting ART, raised suspicion for Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS), which could unmask or paradoxically worsen a pre-existing CNS infection like PML or tuberculoma.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was started on empirical antitoxoplasmosis treatment with high-dose co-trimoxazole while continuing her ART and antituberculous therapy. Prednisolone 1mg/kg was added to cover for presumed IRIS. Her clinical status was unchanged at 4 weeks, and by 8 weeks, she showed further deterioration of power in her left hand. After the MRI findings, a presumed diagnosis of PML was made. She was referred to a rehabilitation centre for walking aids, and the palliative care team was involved.", "final_diagnosis": "A presumed diagnosis of Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (PML) was made, likely as a form of immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (PML-IRIS). The diagnosis was based on the clinical picture of an insidious, progressive neurological deficit in a severely immunocompromised HIV patient who recently started ART. The diagnosis was supported by characteristic MRI findings of multifocal, non-enhancing T2 hyperintense lesions in the white matter and a lack of clinical response to empirical treatment for cerebral toxoplasmosis. PML is caused by the reactivation of the JC polyomavirus.", "disease_name_short": "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy", "source_filename": "51---A-34-Year-Old-HIV-Positive-Woman-from-Malawi-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-T.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 24-year-old Malawian businessman who tested positive for HIV a week prior to presentation and had not yet started antiretroviral medication. His CD4 count was 134 cells/\u03bcL.", "chief_complaint": "Dark spots on his arm and leg for one month, facial swelling, and a three-month history of cough and worsening shortness of breath.", "history_of_present_illness": "For the last month, the patient noticed progressive dark spots on his arm and leg, leading to facial swelling. Concurrently, he reported a 3-month history of a cough productive of white sputum and worsening shortness of breath. He denied constitutional symptoms of weight loss, fevers, or night sweats and had no history of previous tuberculosis.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a businessman from Malawi. His wife was also HIV-positive but not on antiretroviral therapy, and his two children, aged 6 and 4, had not yet been tested for HIV.", "vitals": "Temperature was 36\u00b0C (96.8\u00b0F), respiratory rate was 32 breath cycles per minute, and pulse was 102 bpm.", "physical_exam": "The patient was comfortable at rest with moderate facial oedema. Examination revealed widespread dark-purplish plaques on the skin and palate. The respiratory exam showed decreased air entry and dullness at the right lung base. His right leg was swollen from the foot to the knee with prominent, coalescing dark plaques. The rest of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "The patient was newly diagnosed with HIV with a CD4 count of 134 cells/\u03bcL. His hemoglobin was 10.2 g/dL and MCV was 88.4 fL. Sputum microscopy and GeneXpert tests were negative for tuberculosis. A chest radiograph showed bilateral patchy opacifications in the lower and mid zones of the lungs with peribronchovascular changes. A bronchoscopy was attempted but failed.", "differential_diagnosis": "The main differential diagnoses were tuberculosis and Kaposi's sarcoma (KS). Tuberculosis was considered due to the patient's HIV status and chronic cough. The skin and palatal lesions were typical for Kaposi's sarcoma, raising suspicion of pulmonary involvement. Dual pathology with both TB and KS was also considered a possibility.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient did not improve with a short course of oral antibiotics. He was referred to start antiretroviral medication (ART) and palliative chemotherapy for presumed pulmonary Kaposi's sarcoma.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is disseminated Kaposi's sarcoma (KS), an HIV-associated malignancy caused by human herpesvirus-8 (HHV-8). The diagnosis was made clinically based on the characteristic widespread dark-purplish cutaneous and palatal plaques in a newly diagnosed, ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive patient with a low CD4 count (134 cells/\u03bcL). Pulmonary involvement was strongly suspected given his respiratory symptoms and typical chest radiograph findings after tuberculosis was excluded by negative sputum and GeneXpert tests.", "disease_name_short": "Kaposi's sarcoma", "source_filename": "59---A-24-Year-Old-Man-from-Malawi-With-Skin-Le_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 27-year-old male Swiss tourist with no other relevant medical history mentioned.", "chief_complaint": "Persisting, incapacitating polyarthralgias for several weeks after returning from travel.", "history_of_present_illness": "During the second week of a 3.5-week trip, the patient developed an acute febrile illness with chills, malaise, frontal headache, and generalized myalgia. About one week after fever onset, a transient, non-pruritic maculopapular rash started on his forearms, spread to his trunk, neck, and face, then faded after 3 days. He also experienced slowly progressive, debilitating polyarthralgias in peripheral joints with transient swelling, and self-limiting painful cervical and inguinal lymphadenopathy. While in Peru, he was clinically diagnosed with dengue fever and treated with paracetamol. The fever and other symptoms subsided within a week, but the polyarthralgias persisted, showing a symmetrical pattern affecting the small joints of the hands and feet, wrists, ankles, and knees. He reported stiffness in these joints, mainly in the morning and after immobility. The joint pain continued for over two months after returning home.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had spent 3.5 weeks travelling in the rainforests of the Amazon Basin in northern Peru. His illness began during the second week of his trip. Mayaro virus infection is strongly associated with occupational or recreational exposure in rainforest environments in South America.", "vitals": null, "physical_exam": "Upon evaluation in Switzerland, the physical examination was completely unremarkable. The affected joints did not reveal any clinical signs of inflammation such as swelling, redness, or effusion.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial serological testing in Switzerland for dengue virus, chikungunya virus, parvovirus B19, Epstein-Barr virus, Borrelia burgdorferi, Chlamydia trachomatis, Salmonella Typhi, and S. Paratyphi did not reveal a cause. Subsequent tests showed a normal full blood count, normal liver function tests, and a mildly elevated C-reactive protein at 9 mg/L (normal <5). The diagnosis was confirmed by serology, which was positive for Mayaro virus on two separate samples using indirect immunofluorescence assay (IgM-IFA, IgG-IFA) and a plaque reduction neutralization test (PRNT). Tests for other alphaviruses including Sindbis, Chikungunya, Ross River, and Barmah Forest were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The main differential diagnoses considered were reactive arthritis and viral arthritis. Reactive arthritis was considered, but the patient's symmetrical polyarthritis affecting small peripheral joints was more characteristic of viral arthritis. Given the travel history and clinical syndrome, arthropod-borne alphaviruses were the primary suspicion among viral causes.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient initially received symptomatic treatment with paracetamol while in Peru. After the diagnosis of Mayaro infection was confirmed, he was treated symptomatically with ibuprofen. The polyarthralgias subsided slowly over the following weeks and months before disappearing completely.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Mayaro fever, caused by the Mayaro virus (MAYV), an alphavirus. This was confirmed serologically with positive IgM and IgG antibodies on indirect immunofluorescence assay (IFA) and a positive plaque reduction neutralization test (PRNT). The diagnosis is strongly supported by the clinical presentation of an acute febrile illness with a rash, followed by persistent, debilitating, symmetrical polyarthralgias, and a relevant travel history to the Amazon rainforest in Peru, an endemic region for MAYV.", "disease_name_short": "Mayaro fever", "source_filename": "87---A-27-Year-Old-Male-Traveller-Returning-from-the_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old Peruvian man with a past medical history of hepatitis of unknown etiology 20 years prior. He had not received any vaccinations since his early childhood.", "chief_complaint": "Fever, retro-orbital headache, jaundice, bleeding, and altered mental status for eight days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began eight days prior to presentation with fever and retro-orbital headache. Two days into the illness, he developed jaundice, coffee-ground vomiting, gross haematuria, and increasing mental obtundation, leading to admission at a local hospital in the jungle. During transfer to the referral hospital in Lima, he experienced a generalized seizure.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient worked as a logging supervisor for the past 5 years in a remote jungle area near Pucallpa, Peru, on the shores of the Ucayali River. This occupation created possible contact with sylvatic mosquitoes and animals. He reported not taking any malaria prophylaxis.", "vitals": "On admission, his temperature was 38.8\u00b0C, blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg, heart rate was 110 beats per minute, and respiratory rate was 24 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was agitated, unresponsive to verbal commands, and intermittently stuporous. He was jaundiced, with spontaneous bleeding from his oral mucosa and at venipuncture sites, and had multiple large ecchymoses on his face, trunk, and limbs. Neurological examination revealed a marked flapping of hands (asterixis) with no focal or meningeal signs. Lung auscultation revealed crepitant rales in both bases. The liver was felt 3 cm below the right costal margin, while the spleen was not palpable and there was no lymphadenopathy.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial labs showed anaemia (haematocrit 30%), thrombocytopenia (platelets 110,000/\u03bcL), severe transaminitis (AST 2890 U/L, ALT 2676 U/L), hyperbilirubinemia (total bilirubin 11.6 mg/dL), and coagulopathy (INR 2.6, aPTT 80 s). Urinalysis showed proteinuria and hematuria. Three malaria thick films were negative, as were blood cultures and serologies for Brucella and Hepatitis B (IgM anti-Hbc). The diagnosis was confirmed by a highly positive IgM capture ELISA for yellow fever at a 1:10000 titer. Anti-dengue IgM was negative. A chest x-ray eventually showed bilateral pulmonary infiltrates compatible with ARDS.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for this acute febrile illness with liver failure included severe malaria, though the transaminases were exceptionally high. Other considerations were viral hepatitis (A, B, C, D, E), toxin-mediated hepatitis, leptospirosis, typhoid fever, and brucellosis. Severe dengue was less likely due to the prolonged fever and low hematocrit. Given his occupation, jungle exposure, and unvaccinated status, a viral haemorrhagic fever, particularly yellow fever, was highly suspected.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient received presumptive treatment for malaria, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and supportive care. Over the subsequent three days, his condition worsened with increasing hepatic encephalopathy, renal failure (creatinine 6.5 mg/dL), coma, and disseminated intravascular coagulation with severe bleeding. He developed ARDS confirmed by chest x-ray. His liver transaminases dropped suddenly on day 3, indicating acute hepatic disintegration. The patient died one day later.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Yellow Fever. This was based on the clinical presentation of fever, jaundice, bleeding, and multi-organ failure in an unvaccinated individual with significant occupational exposure as a logger in an endemic region of Peru. The diagnosis was confirmed serologically by a highly positive IgM capture ELISA for yellow fever (1:10000). Alternative diagnoses such as malaria, bacterial sepsis, hepatitis B, and dengue were ruled out by negative laboratory investigations.", "disease_name_short": "Yellow fever", "source_filename": "93---A-35-Year-Old-Male-Logger-from-Peru-With-Fe_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 6-year-old Malawian boy described as thin but not malnourished.", "chief_complaint": "Painless, proptosed left eye for 3 weeks.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a painless, proptosed left eye that his family first noticed 3 weeks prior to presentation. The condition had worsened rapidly, although his vision was still normal. He denied any pain in the eye.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from the lakeshore of Lake Malawi and resides in an area endemic for malaria and schistosomiasis. Endemic Burkitt's lymphoma is noted to be the most common childhood cancer in areas where malaria is holoendemic.", "vitals": "On examination, he was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "The right eye was normal. The left eye was proptosed but non-pulsating. The left pupil was round, clear, and responded well to light. The left eyeball demonstrated significantly limited movement when he was asked to follow an object. No other swellings or abnormalities were noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "An urgent full work-up was performed to confirm the diagnosis, including a fine-needle aspirate (FNA), bone marrow aspirate (BMA), full blood count (FBC), lumbar puncture (LP), HIV antibody test, stool and urine microscopy, and an abdominal scan. The specific results of these investigations are not detailed in the text.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was Burkitt\u2019s lymphoma, given the rapid development of proptosis in a boy from a malaria-endemic area. Other considerations included another type of B cell lymphoma and rhabdomyosarcoma. Retinoblastoma was considered less likely due to the patient's age. Infection was excluded by the painless nature of the process, and an arteriovenous malformation was deemed unlikely as the mass was non-pulsating.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was started on oral allopurinol and hyperhydration. During a lumbar puncture, he received intrathecal methotrexate and hydrocortisone as prophylaxis against CNS involvement. Chemotherapy was initiated the following day. The proptosis improved within 48 hours and was back to normal within a week. He completed four courses of chemotherapy over 30 days. A year later, he was free of disease and pronounced cured.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is endemic Burkitt's lymphoma. This diagnosis was made based on the classic clinical presentation of a rapidly progressive, painless proptosis in a young boy from a malaria-holoendemic region. Although a diagnostic workup including a fine-needle aspirate was performed, the diagnosis was strongly supported by the dramatic and rapid clinical response to chemotherapy, which was initiated urgently to prevent irreversible eye damage.", "disease_name_short": "Burkitt's lymphoma", "source_filename": "60---A-6-Year-Old-Boy-from-Malawi-With-Proptos_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 52-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and poorly controlled hypertension.", "chief_complaint": "Abdominal pain and vomiting.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-day history of retro-orbital headache, lethargy, myalgia, and fevers. These initial symptoms had begun to improve over the 24 hours prior to presentation, at which point he developed abdominal pain and vomiting.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in an urban hospital setting in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam, a high-endemicity area for dengue. He had no history of travel outside the city for several years. The risk of malaria is very low in this urban area, and there was no suspected rodent exposure.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient was afebrile with a temperature of 36.5\u00b0C. He was in compensated shock with a heart rate of 120 bpm, blood pressure of 105/90 mmHg resulting in a narrow pulse pressure, a respiratory rate of 28 breaths per minute, and a peripheral oxygen saturation of 93% on air.", "physical_exam": "The patient was drowsy but rousable with a GCS of 14/15. He had a weak radial pulse. Respiratory examination revealed a dull percussion note and reduced breath sounds at both lung bases. The abdomen showed mild distension with shifting dullness and a tender liver edge palpable 3cm below the costal margin. Skin examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed haemoconcentration with a haematocrit of 53.9%, which later rose to 55.1%. He had leukopenia (WCC 2.1 x10^9/L) and significant thrombocytopenia. C-reactive protein was low (<4 mg/L). There was evidence of shock with a raised serum lactate of 3.5 mmol/L. Ultrasound scans confirmed moderate bilateral pleural effusions, ascites, mild hepatomegaly, and a thickened gallbladder wall. The diagnosis of dengue was confirmed by a positive NS1 ELISA and Reverse Transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR).", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was severe dengue with vascular leak and compensated shock, which was considered highly likely given the clinical picture and location. Other important considerations included rickettsial infections like scrub typhus, even without an eschar. Malaria was considered but less likely due to the low urban risk and lack of haemolysis. Leptospirosis was also a possibility, though the absence of rodent exposure made it less probable. Melioidosis was considered given the patient's diabetes and Southeast Asian origin.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with compensated shock and admitted to a high-dependency unit for supportive care. He received careful fluid resuscitation with Ringer\u2019s lactate solution, starting at 10mL/kg over 1 hour and increasing to 15mL/kg/hr due to persistent tachycardia, oliguria, and a rising haematocrit. The fluid rate was subsequently tapered down as his condition improved. He was monitored closely with two-hourly clinical assessments and six-hourly haematocrit checks. The patient made a complete recovery and was discharged from the hospital 3 days later.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was severe dengue. This was based on the typical clinical progression from a febrile phase to a critical phase at the time of defervescence, characterized by signs of vascular leakage (pleural effusions, ascites, haemoconcentration) and compensated shock. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a positive dengue NS1 ELISA and a positive RT-PCR for dengue virus.", "disease_name_short": "Severe Dengue", "source_filename": "9---A-52-Year-Old-Man-from-Vietnam-With-Evo_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old woman from Malawi, who was diagnosed with HIV two months prior following an episode of cryptococcal meningitis. She was being treated with a maintenance dose of fluconazole. One month before presentation, she began antiretroviral therapy with stavudine (d4T), lamivudine (3TC), and nevirapine (NVP), along with co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. She has no known allergies.", "chief_complaint": "Generalized skin rash for three days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient developed a generalized skin rash that began on her trunk three days ago and subsequently spread to her extremities and mucous membranes, including the lips, oral mucosa, conjunctivae, and genital mucosa. One day before the rash appeared, she developed a productive cough with whitish sputum. For the past two days, she has also experienced a sore throat and dysuria. The rash is non-itchy, and she has difficulty and pain with talking and eating due to swollen lips.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in Malawi. Her primary exposure risk is pharmacological, having commenced a new antiretroviral regimen (stavudine, lamivudine, nevirapine) and co-trimoxazole prophylaxis one month prior to the onset of her symptoms.", "vitals": "Her temperature is 37.7\u00b0C (99.9\u00b0F), blood pressure is 120/68 mmHg, pulse is 90 bpm, and respiratory rate is 24 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "There is a generalized, non-itchy maculopapular rash involving the skin and mucous membranes, with sparing of the palms and soles. Examination reveals bilateral conjunctivitis. The eyelids and lips are covered with haemorrhagic crusts, and the lips are swollen, making it difficult for her to open her mouth. The chest is clear on auscultation.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Blood cultures and a rapid malaria test were performed upon admission and both were negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The most likely diagnosis is Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)/toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN), which is a potentially life-threatening mucocutaneous hypersensitivity reaction most commonly caused by drugs. The timing of the rash, appearing weeks after initiation of nevirapine and co-trimoxazole, strongly supports this diagnosis, especially given her HIV-positive status which increases the incidence. Other possibilities for the prodromal symptoms, such as a bacterial or viral infection or malaria, were less likely given the subsequent mucocutaneous eruption and negative tests.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Upon admission, all antiretroviral drugs and co-trimoxazole were stopped. The patient received supportive care including IV fluids, a urinary catheter, 0.9% normal saline as a mouthwash, and dexamethasone eye ointment. An NG tube was declined by the family. Her fever and cough resolved spontaneously, and she was discharged after 10 days. After all lesions had healed completely, she was successfully started on a new antiretroviral combination where nevirapine was replaced by efavirenz. Co-trimoxazole was not re-initiated. The new regimen was well tolerated without any recurrence of the rash.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS), a severe mucocutaneous hypersensitivity reaction. The diagnosis was made clinically based on the characteristic generalized maculopapular rash involving skin and multiple mucosal surfaces (oral, conjunctival, genital). The condition was attributed to her recently initiated medications, with nevirapine and co-trimoxazole identified as the most likely causative agents. The diagnosis was further supported by the resolution of symptoms after withdrawal of these drugs and the absence of recurrence upon starting a different antiretroviral regimen.", "disease_name_short": "Stevens-Johnson syndrome", "source_filename": "68---A-31-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-a-Gene_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A previously healthy 4-year-old girl from rural eastern Uganda.", "chief_complaint": "Coma for 4 hours following a seizure.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began yesterday with a bad headache in the early afternoon, followed by shaking chills in the evening. The family suspected malaria. The child slept restlessly and at 5 a.m. today, she had a seizure lasting about ten minutes. The family took 4 hours to reach the hospital, during which time she has not awoken. There was no recent head trauma.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in rural eastern Uganda during the rainy season. Malaria is reported to be a common problem in her village. High malaria transmission is expected in this setting.", "vitals": "Her temperature is 38.7\u00b0C (101.7\u00b0F), pulse is 150 bpm, respiratory rate is 36 breath cycles per minute, and blood pressure is 98/40 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "The patient is in a coma with a Blantyre Coma Scale of 1/5. She has nasal flaring with respirations but no neck stiffness or jaundice. Capillary refill is normal. Pupils are 2mm and reactive, with normal extraocular movements. Direct ophthalmoscopy shows no papilloedema. Stimulation elicits decerebrate posturing. Cardiac examination reveals a gallop rhythm. Her liver is palpable 2cm below the right costal margin and her spleen is 4cm below the left costal margin.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A rapid glucose test was normal. Laboratory results on admission showed a haematocrit of 17.6% and a platelet count of <30 x10^9/L. The Malaria Rapid Diagnostic Test (RDT) was positive. A lumbar puncture was performed, and the CSF was clear and acellular with a normal opening pressure. Blood cultures were taken. Ophthalmoscopy after mydriatic drops revealed white-centred haemorrhages in both eyes, consistent with malarial retinopathy. An EEG showed diffuse slowing but no epileptiform activity.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses for the patient's coma included cerebral malaria, acute bacterial meningitis, viral encephalitis, and intoxication. Metabolic abnormalities like hypoglycaemia and non-convulsive status epilepticus were also considered. Hypoglycaemia was ruled out by a rapid test, and a post-ictal state was deemed unlikely given the 4-hour duration of unresponsiveness. Although the Malaria RDT was positive, the high rate of asymptomatic parasitaemia in the region meant that other causes of coma could not be immediately excluded.", "management_and_clinical_course": "A diagnosis of retinopathy-positive cerebral malaria was made. The child was treated with intravenous artesunate 2.4mg/kg, repeated at 12 and 24 hours, and then daily. She was admitted to a high-dependency unit for monitoring. Twelve hours after admission, she had a brief, self-resolving seizure. Forty hours after admission, her Blantyre Coma Score had improved to 4/5. She was discharged home on hospital day five with a follow-up appointment scheduled.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was retinopathy-positive cerebral malaria. This was based on the clinical presentation of an unexplained coma in a child from a malaria-endemic region with a positive Malaria RDT. The diagnosis was strongly supported by the presence of characteristic white-centred haemorrhages on ophthalmoscopy, a finding highly specific for cerebral malaria. The presence of severe anaemia and thrombocytopenia were also consistent findings. Other causes of coma, such as bacterial meningitis, were ruled out by a normal lumbar puncture.", "disease_name_short": "Cerebral malaria", "source_filename": "4---A-4-Year-Old-Girl-from-Uganda-in-a-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-Medi.json"}
