{"patient_information": "A 26-year-old Malawian woman with a past medical history described as unremarkable. She is a businesswoman who sells vegetables and lives in an urban high-density area. She has three healthy children, but four of her children have died as toddlers. Her husband died a year prior from a 'high fever'. An HIV test performed during her admission was reactive.", "chief_complaint": "Headache for one week, confusion for two days, and a drooping left eyelid for one day.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient had been unwell for at least one week with a headache of insidious onset. Two days prior to presentation, she became confused. One day before admission, her relatives noticed a drooping eyelid on the left side.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and resides in an urban high-density area. She works as a businesswoman selling vegetables. Her husband died a year ago of 'high fever', and four of her children died as toddlers. The case occurred in a malarious and high TB prevalence setting.", "vitals": "On examination, her temperature was 38.4\u00b0C (101.1\u00b0F), blood pressure was 115/75mmHg, heart rate was 86 bpm, and respiratory rate was 18 breaths per minute. Her Glasgow Coma Scale was 14/15.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared seriously unwell. The neurological examination revealed a complete ptosis on the left. On primary gaze, the left eye was in a 'down and out' position, consistent with a third nerve palsy. There was no neck stiffness, and the remainder of her neurological examination was normal. Her chest was clear.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Blood results showed a WBC of 3.2 x10\u2079/L and Hb of 10.2 g/dL. A thick film for malaria and trypanosomes was negative. A lumbar puncture revealed a markedly raised opening pressure of 32cmH2O. CSF analysis showed a protein of 3.0 g/L and glucose of 1.3 mmol/L (paired with a serum glucose of 4.0 mmol/L). An HIV test was reactive. In the CSF, India Ink stain, cryptococcal culture, and bacterial culture were all negative. However, acid-fast bacilli were detected in the CSF by Ziehl\u2013Neelsen stain. A subsequent MRI scan of her brain revealed bilateral basal ganglia infarctions.", "differential_diagnosis": "The clinical syndrome was infectious meningitis. Cerebral malaria was considered due to fever and impaired consciousness in a malarious area but was less likely in a semi-immune adult, especially with cranial nerve palsies and a negative thick film. Infectious encephalitis was also considered but was less likely given the cranial nerve involvement and CSF findings of very high protein and very low glucose. The main differential diagnosis comprised bacterial, tuberculous, and cryptococcal meningitis, which could not be reliably differentiated by initial clinical and CSF findings alone.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was started on pragmatic treatment for bacterial, cryptococcal, and tuberculous meningitis with ceftriaxone 2g bid, fluconazole 1200mg od, and empiric anti-tuberculosis therapy. A few days into treatment, her condition deteriorated and she slipped into a coma. She ultimately died in the hospital.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was tuberculous meningitis, caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. This was established by the definitive detection of acid-fast bacilli in the cerebrospinal fluid via Ziehl\u2013Neelsen stain. The diagnosis was supported by the clinical presentation of subacute meningoencephalitis with a cranial nerve palsy, the patient's newly diagnosed HIV-positive status, and CSF findings of a markedly elevated opening pressure, high protein, and low glucose. The patient developed severe complications, including bilateral basal ganglia infarctions, leading to coma and death.", "disease_name_short": "Tuberculous meningitis", "source_filename": "3---A-26-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-with-Headache-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
