{"patient_information": "A 16-year-old Malawian girl who delivered a baby 5 months prior. An HIV test during her antenatal care was negative. Her past medical history is otherwise unremarkable.", "chief_complaint": "Fever, generalized abdominal pain, and frontal headache for 5 days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient, a 16-year-old girl, presented to an emergency room with a 5-day history of fever, generalized abdominal pain, and frontal headache.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives with her family in an urban, high-density area in Malawi. The house has no running water or electricity, and the family gets water from a community tap. She recently dropped out of primary school during her pregnancy.", "vitals": "On admission, her temperature was 38.1\u00b0C (100.6\u00b0F), blood pressure was 110/60mmHg, pulse was 78 bpm, and respiratory rate was 20 breaths per minute. Her Glasgow Coma Scale was 15/15.", "physical_exam": "The patient was in a fair nutritional state with mild scleral jaundice. Abdominal examination revealed diffuse tenderness without guarding, and the spleen was palpable 2 cm below the left costal margin. The liver was not enlarged. There was no neck stiffness, the chest was clear, and there was no lymphadenopathy. Pelvic examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a WBC of 3.2x10^9/L, hemoglobin of 10.9 mg/dL, and mild normocytic anaemia. A blood film for malaria parasites was positive with a parasitaemia described as \u201clow\u201d. A repeat HIV test was negative. Blood cultures subsequently grew Salmonella Typhi.", "differential_diagnosis": "The main differential diagnoses considered were malaria and enteric fever. Malaria could explain many symptoms, but abdominal pain would be unusual, and low parasitaemia can be subclinical in semi-immune individuals. Enteric fever (typhoid) was strongly considered due to the combination of abdominal tenderness, splenomegaly, frontal headache, and relative bradycardia.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially started on oral artemether/lumefantrine for malaria and intravenous ceftriaxone. After the blood culture result confirmed Salmonella Typhi, the antibiotic was switched to ciprofloxacin 500mg twice daily. Her fever began to resolve on day 5 of antibiotic treatment, and she was discharged on day 7 to complete a 10-day course of ciprofloxacin. She was also given a single dose of praziquantel due to the high endemicity of schistosomiasis in Malawi.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is Typhoid Fever caused by Salmonella Typhi. This was definitively established by the growth of the organism in blood cultures. While a blood film was positive for malaria parasites, her clinical syndrome and response to appropriate antibiotic therapy confirmed enteric fever as the primary diagnosis. The positive malaria smear likely represented a co-infection or an incidental finding in a person from a malaria-endemic region.", "disease_name_short": "Typhoid fever", "source_filename": "13---A-16-Year-Old-Girl-from-Malawi-With-Fever_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
