{"patient_information": "A previously healthy 20-year-old man from southern India. One month before the onset of symptoms, he received five doses of anti-rabies vaccine (purified chick embryo cell vaccine) following a dog bite, but did not receive rabies immunoglobulin (RIG).", "chief_complaint": "Fever and quadriparesis.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient had a fever for 4 days, which was followed by tingling and weakness that began in his left leg. Over the subsequent 2 days, the weakness progressed to involve all four limbs, resulting in quadriparesis.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from southern India, a rabies-endemic country. He had a history of a stray dog bite (WHO category III) on his left lower limb one month prior to symptom onset. He received a full course of five active anti-rabies vaccinations but did not receive anti-rabies immunoglobulin as part of his post-exposure prophylaxis.", "vitals": "On admission, his blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg, pulse was 90 beats per minute, respiratory rate was 26 breaths per minute, and his temperature was 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The patient was conscious with normal mental functions (GCS 15/15). Neurological examination revealed a flaccid, areflexic quadriparesis.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Blood investigations including serum electrolytes, renal function, and liver function tests were normal. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis showed a protein of 52 mg/dL, glucose of 60 mg/dL, and 340 cells/mm3 (60% polymorphs and 40% lymphocytes). A saliva sample collected at admission was positive for rabies viral RNA by RT-PCR; a CSF sample was negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary clinical differential was Guillain Barr\u00e9 syndrome, given the rapidly progressive ascending paralysis. Other possibilities considered included post-vaccination encephalomyelitis and other encephalitides such as those caused by arthropod-borne viruses or herpes simplex virus.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Following admission, the patient developed autonomic instability, evidenced by increased perspiration and significant variability in heart rate and blood pressure. He subsequently developed dysphagia and respiratory distress, requiring mechanical ventilation on the third day of hospitalization. His condition deteriorated, and he died from a sudden cardiac arrest on the fifth day of hospitalization, nine days after the onset of his symptoms.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was paralytic rabies. This was based on the clinical presentation of fever and rapidly progressive flaccid quadriparesis one month after a WHO category III dog bite in a rabies-endemic area. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the detection of rabies viral RNA via RT-PCR in a saliva sample. The development of the disease, despite vaccination, was attributed to the omission of rabies immunoglobulin (RIG) from the post-exposure prophylaxis.", "disease_name_short": "Paralytic rabies", "source_filename": "91---A-20-Year-Old-Male-from-India-With-Fever-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 32-year-old man from Malawi with a past medical history notable for an episode of a blistering and painful skin lesion on the right side of his abdomen three years prior, which healed spontaneously and was suspected to be herpes zoster.", "chief_complaint": "Pain in the right upper abdomen and a feeling of faintness, especially when standing.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient was well until one month prior to admission when he began to experience a feeling of fullness in his upper abdomen. In the week before admission, he developed pain in the right upper abdomen, which was particularly apparent when sleeping on his right side. He recently started to feel breathless on lying down and faint upon standing and walking.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi. The clinical context notes that in Africa, tuberculosis is the most common cause of pericardial effusion and there is a strong association between tuberculosis and HIV infection.", "vitals": "Pulse was regular at 130 bpm with a pronounced pulsus paradoxus measured at 15 mmHg. Blood pressure in the supine position was 90/60 mmHg. He was slightly breathless in the supine position.", "physical_exam": "The patient was thin. His jugular venous pulse appeared elevated, the apex beat was impalpable, and heart sounds were quiet without murmurs. Chest auscultation was normal. Examination of the abdomen revealed an enlarged, tender, palpable liver measured at 8 cm below the right costal junction. There was also evidence of mild peripheral oedema of the legs and sacral area.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Haemoglobin was 10.5 g/dL and the white blood cell count was 9.8 G/L. A chest radiograph showed an enlarged globular heart with clear lung fields. A therapeutic pericardial aspiration yielded 500 mL of bloodstained pericardial fluid. An HIV test was subsequently found to be positive.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary clinical syndrome was identified as pericardial tamponade from a large pericardial effusion. The differential diagnosis for the cause in this African setting included tuberculosis, other bacterial infections, malignancy, and HIV-related Kaposi\u2019s sarcoma. Given the history of a likely herpes zoster attack, HIV-associated tuberculosis was considered the most probable diagnosis.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent therapeutic pericardial aspiration, with 500 mL of fluid removed, leading to an immediate increase in blood pressure to 120/80 mmHg. He was started on standard first-line antituberculous treatment (rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for 2 months, followed by rifampicin and isoniazid for 4 months) and prednisolone at 60 mg daily, tapered over ten weeks. Following a positive HIV test, antiretroviral therapy was started four weeks after commencing antituberculous treatment. He made a full and uneventful recovery.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with HIV-associated tuberculous pericarditis complicated by a large pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade. The diagnosis was based on the classic clinical signs of tamponade (tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus, hypotension), radiographic evidence of a globular heart, a history suggestive of immunosuppression (herpes zoster), a positive HIV test, and a complete response to pericardial drainage and empiric antituberculous therapy.", "disease_name_short": "HIV-associated tuberculous pericarditis", "source_filename": "66---A-32-Year-Old-Man-from-Malawi-With-Pain-in-the-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
