{"patient_information": "The patient is a 23-year-old male farmer from Myanmar.", "chief_complaint": "Left-sided scrotal swelling for a duration of 3 years.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 23-year-old farmer presented with a 3-year history of left-sided scrotal swelling that was non-tender and had gradually increased in size. He reported no history of fever. An attempt to treat the condition with traditional herbal medicine was unsuccessful.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from the central part of Myanmar. He reported that scrotal swelling is not an uncommon problem in his home region, suggesting a possible infectious etiology endemic to the area.", "vitals": "His vital signs were normal and he was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "The patient was in fair general condition. Examination revealed a non-tender, non-reducible, unilateral scrotal swelling. There were no palpable inguinal lymph nodes.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "An ultrasound confirmed the presence of a hydrocoele.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis for the chronic, non-tender, unilateral scrotal swelling included inguinal hernia, hydrocoele, and testicular tumour. Hernias are often reducible, unlike the patient's swelling. The high prevalence of scrotal swelling in his home region suggested an infectious cause, with lymphatic filariasis caused by Wuchereria bancrofti being the most important consideration, as hydrocoele is its most common clinical abnormality in men. Urogenital schistosomiasis was considered but is not endemic in Southeast Asia. Testicular tuberculosis was deemed unlikely due to the long duration of symptoms without other signs.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent a hydrocoelectomy, during which 3 litres of clear fluid were drained. He also received antifilarial treatment. He was subsequently lost to followup as he lived in a remote area.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with lymphatic filariasis. This diagnosis was based on the classic clinical presentation of a chronic, gradually increasing, non-tender hydrocoele, which was confirmed on ultrasound. The patient's origin from an endemic region in Myanmar where this condition is common provided strong epidemiological evidence. The presumed causative agent is Wuchereria bancrofti, the typical cause of filarial hydrocoele.", "disease_name_short": "Lymphatic filariasis", "source_filename": "36---A-23-Year-Old-Farmer-from-Myanmar-With-Uni_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropi.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 38-year-old European expatriate male with an unremarkable previous medical history. He reports having sexual contact only with his wife.", "chief_complaint": "Progressive constipation and difficulty passing urine for the past 3 weeks.", "history_of_present_illness": "For three weeks, the patient experienced progressive difficulty passing urine, requiring increasing abdominal pressure to pass small amounts, associated with lower abdominal discomfort, abnormal sensations in the groins and genitals, and erectile dysfunction. He denied weakness, dysaesthesia, or loss of sensation in his legs, but felt that his walking was abnormal in an unspecified way. He had no backache. Over the past two months, he had unintentional weight loss of 8kg and fatigue, without fever or night sweats. One week prior to presentation, he noticed reddish discoloration of his urine without painful micturition or fever, for which he received four types of medication from a local clinic.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a European expatriate living in Malawi. He had a history of swimming regularly in Lake Malawi.", "vitals": "He had normal vital signs.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared healthy. General examination was normal except for a palpable bladder. A rectal examination revealed a low anal sphincter tone, and the genital examination was normal. Neurological examination showed lively, symmetrical tendon reflexes in the legs without clonus or pathological plantar reflexes, with no other abnormalities.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Urine microscopy from a local clinic one week prior had identified Schistosoma haematobium eggs. A full blood count showed a normal absolute WBC of 9 x 10^9/L but with a relative eosinophilia of 21.8% and an absolute eosinophil count of 1.15 x 10^9/L. ALT was slightly elevated, while creatinine and CRP were normal. HIV and VDRL tests were negative. Repeat stool and urine microscopy were normal. An abdominal ultrasound showed a large bladder residue. An MRI scan of the spinal cord revealed an enhancing 1cm lesion at the anterior aspect of the conus medullaris with associated oedema extending to the T9 level, as well as nodular thickening of the bladder wall.", "differential_diagnosis": "The patient's presentation was compatible with a conus syndrome. The most important differential diagnosis considered was spinal cord schistosomiasis. Other infections considered were spinal tuberculosis, a spinal abscess, and neurosyphilis. A tumour or metastases impinging on the conus medullaris was also a possibility to be ruled out.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient had previously been treated at a local clinic with praziquantel, ciprofloxacin, buscopan, and bisacodyl. After diagnosis, he required urinary catheterization for 2 weeks. He was treated with a second course of praziquantel and a course of high-dose prednisolone which was tapered off over 2 months. He made a full recovery and remained well at over 2 years of follow-up.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with spinal cord schistosomiasis due to Schistosoma haematobium, presenting as a conus syndrome. The diagnosis was based on the prior identification of S. haematobium eggs in his urine, supported by significant peripheral eosinophilia, a history of fresh water exposure in Lake Malawi, and characteristic MRI findings of an enhancing lesion in the conus medullaris and bladder wall thickening.", "disease_name_short": "Spinal cord schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "63---A-38-Year-Old-European-Expatriate-Living-in-M_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 31-year-old man, diagnosed with HIV 6 years ago, who is not yet on antiretroviral therapy (ART) or co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. His CD4 count was 280/\u03bcL one month prior to presentation, and on admission was 150/\u03bcL with an HIV viral load of 1,500,000 copies/mL. He denies any recreational drug use and has never smoked tobacco. He has no other significant past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-week history of increasing shortness of breath on exertion, dry cough, and drenching night sweats.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 6-week history of progressively worsening shortness of breath on exertion, a dry cough, and drenching night sweats. He also reported general fatigue and a weight loss of about 10kg over the preceding 5 months.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a business traveller who travels extensively. In the past 6 months, he had work trips to Europe, China, Korea, Japan, Singapore, and the United States. He lives with his male partner.", "vitals": "On presentation, his temperature was 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F), blood pressure was 120/70mmHg, pulse was 100 bpm, and respiratory rate was 26 breaths per minute. His oxygen saturation was 87% on ambient air, which improved to 97% on 15L of O2.", "physical_exam": "The patient was alert and able to complete full sentences, but was short of breath at rest. Auscultation of the chest was clear. The rest of the physical examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results showed a normal full blood count and normal renal and liver function tests. C-reactive protein was elevated at 6.7 mg/dL. Arterial blood gases on ambient air showed a pH of 7.44 and a PaO2 of 7.6 kPa, consistent with type 1 respiratory failure. His CD4 count was 150/\u03bcL. A chest radiograph showed bilateral perihilar infiltrates. A CT-pulmonary angiogram ruled out pulmonary embolism but revealed extensive \u201cground-glass\u201d infiltrates in both lungs, especially in the lower lobes. Serum 1,3 \u03b2-D-glucan was markedly elevated at >500 pg/mL. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid analysis confirmed the diagnosis, with Grocott staining revealing Pneumocystis jirovecii cystic forms and a strongly positive PCR for P. jirovecii DNA.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP), given the patient's presentation as an ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive individual with a low CD4 count, progressive shortness of breath, and typical chest radiograph findings. Other considerations included tuberculosis, atypical bacterial pneumonia, cryptococcosis, or histoplasmosis. A CT pulmonary angiogram was performed to rule out pulmonary embolism.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was managed with high-flow oxygen, high-dose co-trimoxazole, and prednisolone. His tachycardia and hypoxia improved with treatment. Ten days into treatment, he commenced antiretroviral therapy with tenofovir, emtricitabine, and efavirenz. Two weeks into PCP treatment, he developed a pruritic maculopapular rash, leading to a change from co-trimoxazole to clindamycin and primaquine for the final week of his 21-day course, and his efavirenz was replaced. After completing treatment, he was started on dapsone and pyrimethamine for secondary prophylaxis.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is severe Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) caused by Pneumocystis jirovecii. This was established in an ART-na\u00efve HIV-positive patient with a low CD4 count (150/\u03bcL) presenting with classic subacute respiratory failure and hypoxia. The diagnosis was supported by imaging showing bilateral ground-glass infiltrates and a highly elevated serum \u03b2-glucan level (>500 pg/mL). It was definitively confirmed by bronchoalveolar lavage fluid analysis, which identified P. jirovecii cystic forms via Grocott staining and was strongly positive by PCR.", "disease_name_short": "Pneumocystis pneumonia", "source_filename": "23---A-31-Year-Old-HIV-Positive-Business-Traveller-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 25-year-old woman from Bani Suwaif in Upper Egypt.", "chief_complaint": "Severe diarrhoea for 2 months with significant weight loss and amenorrhoea.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient's symptoms began two months prior with stomach rumbles and colicky abdominal pain, followed by anorexia and vomiting. She developed voluminous diarrhoea, occurring five to ten times in 24 hours, both during the day and at night, unrelated to meals. She experienced a weight loss of about 15 kg and amenorrhoea. Treatment with various antibiotics, including metronidazole, and antidiarrhoeal drugs provided no improvement. During the last month, she developed lower limb swelling and severe prostration.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient resides in Bani Suwaif, Upper Egypt, which is an endemic area for Capillaria philippinensis. The infection is typically transmitted through the consumption of raw or undercooked fish.", "vitals": "The patient was afebrile with a heart rate of 100 bpm and blood pressure of 100/60 mmHg.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared generally unwell, pale, and had angular stomatitis. She exhibited decreased skin turgor. Examination revealed a scaphoid abdomen with borborygmi and pitting oedema of the lower limbs.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory tests revealed electrolyte derangement including potassium of 2.8 mmol/L and total calcium of 2.1 mmol/L, along with hypoalbuminaemia. The full blood count showed a haemoglobin of 10.8 g/dL and mild eosinophilia with an eosinophil count of 0.53 x 10^9/L. A D-xylose test showed evidence of malabsorption. Stool microscopy was diagnostic, revealing numerous helminth ova described as typical peanut-shaped eggs of Capillaria philippinensis. During treatment, numerous adult helminths were also found in stool samples.", "differential_diagnosis": null, "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the hospital and initiated on albendazole 400mg twice daily for 3 weeks. She also received supportive care with oral and parenteral fluids and electrolytes for resuscitation, a high protein diet, and vitamins. Her general condition improved significantly within the first week, vomiting stopped, and the diarrhoea subsided after 2 weeks of treatment.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is intestinal capillariasis caused by the helminth Capillaria philippinensis. This was confirmed by the patient's clinical presentation of severe chronic diarrhoea, malabsorption, and protein-losing enteropathy while residing in an endemic region of Egypt. The diagnosis was definitively established by stool microscopy, which demonstrated the characteristic peanut-shaped ova of C. philippinensis.", "disease_name_short": "Intestinal capillariasis", "source_filename": "58---A-25-Year-Old-Woman-from-Egypt-With-Severe-Ch_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 14-year-old girl from the Solomon Islands.", "chief_complaint": "Painless, non-healing large ulcer of the left leg for one month.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 14-year-old girl reported a painless, non-healing large ulcer on her left leg that began one month earlier. A smaller, similar satellite lesion was also noted lower down on the same leg, which is a typical presentation for early yaws lesions. There were no other reported symptoms.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient was from the Solomon Islands, a region in the West Pacific where yaws is still a common problem. Yaws is spread by skin-to-skin contact in warm, humid environments and is normally seen in young children.", "vitals": null, "physical_exam": "On examination, there was a large lesion approximately 3 cm in diameter on the left lower leg, covered by a layer of crust. A smaller, similar lesion was noted further down the leg. There was no regional lymphadenopathy or evidence of any other skin or bone lesions.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A rapid plasma reagin (RPR) test was positive at a titre of 1:64. The patient was seen again 12 months later, and a repeat RPR test was positive at a titre of 1:8.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary diagnosis considered was yaws. Other relevant differential diagnoses included skin lesions caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, pyoderma, tropical ulcer, cutaneous diphtheria, and Aeromonas hydrophila infection.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was treated with a single intramuscular dose of benzathine benzylpenicillin, and the skin lesion resolved over the next 10 days. She presented again 12 months later with a similar ulcer on the right leg. The repeat RPR titre showed a greater than four-fold fall to 1:8, which was considered evidence of cure from the initial infection. The new ulcer on the other leg was therefore interpreted to be of a different aetiology.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was yaws, a chronic infectious disease caused by Treponema pallidum subspecies pertenue. The diagnosis was based on the clinical findings of a characteristic chronic leg ulcer with a satellite lesion in a patient from an endemic region. The diagnosis was supported by a high-titer positive rapid plasma reagin (RPR) test of 1:64, and confirmed by the lesion's resolution after treatment and a greater than four-fold fall in the RPR titre at follow-up, indicating cure.", "disease_name_short": "Yaws", "source_filename": "86---A-14-Year-Old-Girl-in-the-Solomon-Islands-W_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
